+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_86","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc \"python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path; import json; s=json.loads(Path(\\\"docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json\\\").read_text()); print(\\\"rework ids\\\",s.get(\\\"rework_task_ids\\\")); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(str(t) for t in s[\\\"tasks\\\"] if t[\\\"id\\\"] in s.get(\\\"rework_task_ids\\\",[])))' \"","aggregated_output":"rework ids ['6', '7', '8', '9']\n{'name': '[D1] src/ovcaq/', 'description': '### Findings (verbatim)\\n- ⚪ src/ovcaq/events/model.py:224: Hot-path scan cost. `_payload` scans the whole payload at line 224, then `sanitize_json` scans it again at line 252, and `_usage_payload` scans again through `sanitize_json`. Each scan uses a per-character Python generator: about 22 ms per MiB, against about 2.5 ms for a precompiled `re.compile(\"[\\\\ud800-\\\\udfff]\").search`. Every frame also gets re-validated by `replace()` in `_bind_observations` and by `observation_record` in the writer. Behavior-preserving fix: use the regex in the predicate, and call `_sanitize_json` inside `_payload` and `_usage_payload` once the up-front check has passed (found by: ALICE, BOB)\\n- 🟠 src/ovcaq/drivers/codex_events.py:64: Removing controls before redaction now leaks tokens that the old order caught. When a bare C0 control, DEL or lone ESC sits between a word character and a token, removal glues them together: `\"x\\\\x00sk-\" + \"a\"*30` becomes `xsk-aaaa…` and `\"auth\\\\x00Bearer \" + \"a\"*26` becomes `authBearer …`. `_LIKELY_SECRET` needs `\\\\b` before `sk-`/`Bearer`, so neither `bounded_detail` nor the Observation\\'s second sanitizer redacts them. The old order redacted both, because the control supplied the word boundary. Confirmed by tracing the regex by hand; not executed (read-only sandbox) (found by: BOB)\\n- 🟡 src/ovcaq/events/tail.py:119: The preflight\\'s `except (ValueError, RecursionError)` branch, which hands the frame to the driver, has no test. No RawFirstDecoder test feeds a deeply nested frame. Narrowing it to `except ValueError` would still pass the suite: deep frames would then get \"driver failed to decode frame\" instead of the driver\\'s own native gap, against the contract\\'s \"ValueError or RecursionError → delegate unchanged\" (found by: BOB)\\n- 🟡 src/ovcaq/events/tail.py:116: The preflight parse and scan sit inside the tolerant driver catch, so the try blocks nest 4 levels deep (the limit). Hoist the `json.loads` try and the scalar check above it and wrap only `tuple(self._driver.decode(frame))` in `except Exception`. This also matches the contract\\'s \"consume decode under the existing tolerant catch\" (found by: BOB)\\n', 'model': 'sonnet', 'id': '6', 'status': 'pending'}\n{'name': '[D1] tests/events/', 'description': \"### Findings (verbatim)\\n- 🟡 tests/events/test_unicode_frames.py:1: The raw-first regressions (byte-exact raw output, gap digest/preview, unchanged adapter state, later healthy terminal) live in `tests/events/test_unicode_frames.py`. The PRD's Task 2 acceptance command (`pytest tests/events/test_unicode_safety.py tests/events/test_tail.py`) and its repository structure both name `test_unicode_safety.py`, so the PRD's own command does not run them. The full suite covers them, and the split keeps the file under 800 lines. Fix: record the file in the PRD or design acceptance text (found by: ALICE, BLAKE, BOB)\\n- 🟡 tests/events/test_unicode_safety.py:314: Duplicated test tables: the completion construct and record-decode tests repeat the same `additions` dict and body. Hoist it to one module constant or merge them into one test parametrized over construct vs decode (found by: BOB)\\n\", 'model': 'sonnet', 'id': '7', 'status': 'pending'}\n{'name': '[D1] tests/drivers/test_codex_detail_safety.py', 'description': '### Findings (verbatim)\\n- 🟡 tests/drivers/test_codex_detail_safety.py:281: The receiver-lane test special-cases the exact cap input with `if detail != CAP_REPRODUCER`. It then calls `assert_safe_details(tmp_path, lanes, lane_detail)`, which compares the lane detail to itself, so the cap case never checks the exact redacted value or that `[REDACTED]` is present. I confirmed the actual lane detail equals the expected `\"x\"*4080 + \" [REDACTED]\"`. Simpler and stronger: drop the guard and call `assert_safe_details(tmp_path, lanes, expected)`. Related: `assert TOKEN not in expected` and `assert \"sk-abcdefghijkl\" not in expected` (lines 87-88) check fixed test data inside the loop. They should check `observation.payload[\"detail\"]` (found by: ALICE, BOB)\\n', 'model': 'sonnet', 'id': '8', 'status': 'pending'}\n{'name': '[D1] prose', 'description': '### Findings (verbatim)\\n- ⚪ docs/driver-protocol.md:224: The same strip-after-redaction pattern survives outside `bounded_detail`. TUI `display_text` drops Cc/Cf characters after the sanitizer has already run. I checked that a NUL-split token in a Codex agent message (`codex_events.py:32`, where controls are never stripped) shows the full token on screen. So do ZWSP- and soft-hyphen-split tokens even in `bounded_detail` output, because format characters are not in its control regex. This is outside this PRD\\'s scope (\"no speculative control forms\"), but the new doc sentence \"prevents controls inside a credential from hiding it from redaction\" claims more than the code delivers. Fix: narrow the wording to the recognized C0/DEL/CSI forms and file a follow-up PRD to re-sanitize at the display layer (found by: ALICE)\\n- ⚪ CHANGELOG.md:43: The `**runtime**` changelog entry claims a runtime fix, but no runtime production code changed (Task 4 added tests only). It repeats the `**events**` gap entry. Fix: fold the continuation and propagation wording into the events entry (found by: ALICE)\\n', 'model': 'sonnet', 'id': '9', 'status': 'pending'}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
