+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc \"python3 -c \\\"from pathlib import Path; files=['/Users/bob/.claude/plugins/cache/buvis-plugins/git-ferry/0.4.0/skills/catchup/SKILL.md','docs/dev/project-management/meta/project-capsule.md','docs/dev/tmp/tess-1-retry-2-output.md','docs/dev/tmp/tess-1-gate-2.txt']; [print('\\\\nFILE '+f+'\\\\n'+Path(f).read_text()) for f in files]\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"\nFILE /Users/bob/.claude/plugins/cache/buvis-plugins/git-ferry/0.4.0/skills/catchup/SKILL.md\n---\nname: catchup\ndescription: Use when starting work on a project and need local branch/repo context (full diff, GitHub state, capsule refresh). Triggers on \"catchup\", \"catch up\", \"catch-up\", \"what changed on this branch\", \"project overview\", \"refresh capsule\".\n---\n\n# Catch Up\n\nHydrate context before starting work. Load raw sources directly. The capsule captures derived insight (invariants, decisions, health signals), not paraphrases of files you can read.\n\nLoad generously, but the diff and changed files are the priority. On a clearly huge branch, fall back to a stat-only pass rather than blowing the context window.\n\n## Phase 1: Gather (run concurrently)\n\nRun the scripts and read the files in parallel; don't serialize.\n\n**Scripts** (skip on failure, note the gap):\n\n```bash\n\"${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts/branch-diff.sh\"   # skip if on master\n\"${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts/github-state.sh\"  # skip if no gh / no remote\n\"${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts/load-memories.sh\" # skip if no memories\n```\n\nIf a script fails, read it and run its git/gh commands directly.\n\n**Sources** (read each if it exists):\n\n- `CLAUDE.md` / `AGENTS.md` / `GEMINI.md` — **first**. They may define project-specific catchup rules, priority areas, or \"always check X\" constraints. Follow any project catchup checklist in addition to this workflow, and let these rules shape what you flag in Phase 5.\n- `README.md`, `agent_docs/*`, PRDs in `dev/local/prds/wip/`\n- Build/config: `package.json`, `Cargo.toml`, `pyproject.toml`, `go.mod`, `Makefile`, etc.\n- Top 3 levels of `src/`/`lib/`/`app/`; domain model dirs (`models/`, `types/`, `schemas/`, `entities/`)\n- `dev/local/meta/project-capsule.md` (prior invariants/health), `dev/local/meta/decisions.md`, `dev/local/meta/troubleshooting.md`, `~/.claude/decisions.md`. These keepers live in `dev/local/meta/`; if `meta/` has no copy, read the older `dev/local/<name>` instead (unmigrated store).\n\n**Recent master:**\n\n```bash\ngit log --oneline -20 master\n```\n\n**Review harvest** (skip on failure, note the gap):\n\nHarvest this repo's review files into engram, best-effort, so their findings survive once those files are garbage-collected. Skip if `engram` is not on PATH, or if a glob below matches no files.\n\n```bash\nengram harvest dev/local/reviews/*.md dev/local/tmp/*review*.md\n```\n\n**Related context** (never silently absent — on any failure, write the section with a one-line gap note):\n\nRetrieve prior work related to what this session is about, so the capsule can point at it. If `engram` is not on PATH, skip the commands — but still write the section, saying engram was unavailable.\n\nBuild ONE topic string from what the session is already about: the current branch name (drop the `feature/`-style prefix and turn separators into spaces) plus, when `dev/local/prds/wip/` holds exactly one PRD, that PRD's title (its first `# ` heading). On master with no single wip PRD there is no topic: skip the queries rather than searching a branch name that says nothing, and record that as the gap note. Do not drop the section — \"no topic this session\" is itself worth telling the reader, and on a master-default workflow it is the common case.\n\n**The topic is untrusted input.** It is built from a branch name and a PRD title, either of which can contain `\"`, a backtick, or `$(`. Never paste it into a double-quoted shell string. Put it in a variable and pass that variable quoted, so the shell treats it as one literal argument:\n\n```bash\ntopic='<topic>'                                # single quotes; escape any literal ' in the topic\nengram index                                   # refresh this repo first (defaults to --scope repo)\nengram query --scope repo \"$topic\" -k 5\nengram query --scope portfolio \"$topic\" -k 5\nengram status --scope portfolio                # staleness of the widest scope you queried\n```\n\n`engram index` refreshes the repo scope only, so the portfolio hits may come from an older index — that is what the staleness stamp in the capsule section records. Ask `status` for `--scope portfolio` so the stamp actually covers the portfolio query above it; a repo-scoped stamp would describe a narrower scope than the hits it sits under. A nonzero `engram status` means stale or dead rows; report the stamp, don't hide it.\n\n## Phase 2: Branch context (feature branches only, skip on master)\n\nAfter `branch-diff.sh`, load the full picture:\n\n```bash\ngit diff $(git merge-base origin/master HEAD)..HEAD             # full diff, don't sample\ngit diff $(git merge-base origin/master HEAD)..HEAD --name-only # then read each changed file in full\n```\n\nThe diff shows what changed; the full file shows how it fits.\n\n**Blast radius:** for each changed file, trace its consumers (imports + call sites of any changed symbol) to surface ripple effects the diff alone won't show. Prefer `ast-grep`/`sg` over text grep — it won't false-positive on comments or strings. Read any consumer that looks impacted; always trace consumers of changed utilities, types, and interfaces.\n\n**The \"why\":** extract issue/PR references (`#NNN`, `fixes #NNN`) from branch commit messages and load full bodies:\n\n```bash\ngit log $(git merge-base origin/master HEAD)..HEAD --format=\"%B\"\ngh issue view {number} --json title,body,labels,state,comments\ngh pr view --json title,body,comments,reviews   # if an open PR exists\n```\n\n## Phase 3: Synthesize\n\nUpdate or create `dev/local/meta/project-capsule.md`, creating `meta/` if absent. Write there even when an older `dev/local/project-capsule.md` is what you read. It is NOT a summary of what you read — it captures cross-file insight no single file holds.\n\n```markdown\n# Project Capsule: {project name}\n\nGenerated: {date}\n\n## Key Invariants\n{domain rules, boundaries, data-flow constraints; implicit/undocumented rules; what agents most often get wrong}\n\n## Architecture Decisions\n{why it's structured this way; tradeoffs; patterns that look wrong but are intentional}\n\n## Component Boundaries\n{what talks to what; which modules own which data; where the seams are}\n\n## Active Work\n{current branch purpose, PRDs in wip/, recent focus; if autopilot batch active: completed/remaining PRDs, cycle counts}\n\n## Related context\n{engram hits for this session's topic, from Phase 1. Repo hits first, then portfolio. One line per hit: `file:line — score`, plus a half-line on why it is relevant. End with the topic queried and the staleness stamp from `engram status --scope portfolio`. Always write this section: when there were no hits, no topic, or engram was unavailable, say so in one line instead — a silently missing section reads as \"nothing related exists\", which is a different and wrong claim}\n\n## GitHub State\n{open issues + notable ones; open/stale PRs; active/orphaned branches; latest release + unreleased commits; failing/recurring CI}\n\n## Project Health\n{is CI green? are PRs flowing? is debt accumulating? risks or blockers}\n\n## Project Memories\n{gotchas, patterns, feedback from memory files — omit section entirely if none}\n```\n\nRules: don't restate README/CLAUDE.md/config (already in context). Focus on cross-file insight (\"auth assumes X because Y\", not \"auth is in src/auth/\"). Update changed sections, leave accurate ones alone. First-time capsule: leave Architecture/Health sparse, they fill in over sessions.\n\n## Phase 4: Restore tasks\n\nAuto-restore the task list from the most-recent prior session. No prompt; silent if nothing to restore.\n\n```bash\n\"${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts/list-task-sessions.sh\"   # JSON: {\"sessions\":[...]} or {\"error\":...}\n```\n\nHandle the result:\n\n- `{\"sessions\": [...]}` non-empty → take `sessions[0].sessionId` (most recent with tasks), proceed below.\n- Empty `sessions`, or `error` in (`no_project_data`, `no_tasks_dir`, `no_index`) → skip Phase 4 silently.\n- `error` is `schema_unexpected` or `index_unreadable` → **do not skip silently.** Claude Code's on-disk layout drifted and the script can no longer find tasks. Flag it in the Phase 5 report: \"task restore disabled — `list-task-sessions.sh` needs updating (`{detail}`).\"\n\nWhen proceeding, dump the chosen session:\n\n```bash\n\"${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts/dump-tasks.sh\" <sessionId>   # JSON array of {id,subject,description,activeForm,status,blocks,blockedBy}\n```\n\nFor each task in original `id` order: call `TaskCreate` with `subject`, `description`, `activeForm`. Then for any task with non-empty `blockedBy`, call `TaskUpdate` with `addBlockedBy`. Do NOT restore `status` — every restored task starts pending so the new session re-evaluates progress.\n\nNote in the Phase 5 report: `Restored N tasks from session {sessionId-short} ({modified-date})`.\n\n## Phase 5: Report\n\nSummarize what you loaded and learned. Flag:\n\n- **Gaps**: missing arch docs, no tests, unclear boundaries\n- **Risks**: failing CI, stale PRs, dependency/security advisories\n- **Blast radius**: impacted files not on the branch (reverse deps), open PRs touching the same areas\n- **CI status**: if red, include the specific errors/stack traces, not just \"CI is red\"\n- **Linked context**: issues/decisions explaining why current work exists\n- **AGENTS.md flags**: project rules/priorities that should guide upcoming work\n- **Suggestions**: things to address before starting new work\n\n## Capsule maintenance during work\n\nUpdate the capsule when you discover something that belongs there (new invariant, architecture decision, clarified boundary, PRD moved/started). Keep it surgical: change the section, bump the date, move on. GitHub State is refreshed only on catchup runs, not during work.\n\n## Error handling\n\n| Situation | Action |\n|-----------|--------|\n| On master, no capsule | Load sources + GitHub state, generate capsule, skip branch diff |\n| On master, capsule exists | Load sources, update stale capsule sections |\n| No remote | Use local master as base for branch diff |\n| Detached HEAD | Report current commit, ask user for base branch |\n| Not a git repo | Load sources only, skip all git/GitHub operations |\n| `gh` missing/unauthenticated, no GitHub remote, or rate limited | Skip GitHub state, note gap in report |\n\n\nFILE docs/dev/project-management/meta/project-capsule.md\n# Project Capsule: OVCAQ\n\nGenerated: 2026-10-09T19:25:49Z\nCatchup HEAD: `e4d765c42d1be7b42c27312e8a5f474d61178b52` (`master`)\n\n## Key Invariants\n\n- `StateStore.transaction()` is the only durable `state.json` writer; commands and later workflow/runtime services share it.\n- State plus a normalized session result decides lifecycle progress. Agent prose and observational logs never do.\n- The clean v1 state is a one-way cutover: no legacy importer, dual writer, compatibility shim, or permanent `autoclaude`/`autopilot`/`tracon`/`statectl` aliases.\n- Provider-specific argv, environment, event, model, and rate-limit logic remains at driver edges; provider-neutral state/runtime/TUI modules must not parse native events.\n- Raw session evidence survives normalization, and configured commands are always argv arrays spawned without shell evaluation.\n- PRD sequencing follows `docs/dev/project-management/plans/implementation-plan.md`: state first, then workflow, runtime, adapters, TUI, and provisioning.\n\n## Architecture Decisions\n\n- OVCAQ is one Python 3.12+ installable package with an `ovcaq` console command and a small runtime dependency set (Rich/Textual plus stdlib-first internals).\n- State, workflow, runtime, events, both native adapters, supervisor, and TUI are implemented through PRD 00009. Codex includes native decoding, cumulative usage, lane/tool projection, state-only default prompts and fake-supervisor conformance. Provisioning and real launcher/plugin smoke remain later-phase work.\n- State v1 groups ownership under `batch`, `work`, `tasks`, `review`, `control`, and `recovery`, with a monotonic top-level `revision`.\n- Every transaction holds one advisory lock across read, optional revision compare, deep-copy mutation, full validation, revision increment, raw-byte backup publication, state publication, and supported directory fsync.\n- Generic JSON-path operations are recovery tools. Transition-owned paths require explicit `--unsafe`; normal skills use compound commands instead.\n- External launcher skills/plugins remain in their marketplaces. Core owns only declarative requirements and lifecycle state needed to reconcile them.\n\n## Component Boundaries\n\n- `ovcaq.state.schema`: clean v1 types/defaults, full nested validation, cross-field invariants, and future-version refusal.\n- `ovcaq.state.store`: locked load/init/transaction/restore, compare-and-set, backup, atomic replacement, and durability boundaries.\n- `ovcaq.state.paths`: generic JSON-path parsing/mutation only; no workflow ownership policy.\n- `ovcaq.state.commands`: compound task/attempt/contract-card operations and recovery commands; all writes call the store.\n- `ovcaq.cli`: the live parser and stable machine-output/error boundary; help and state documentation derive from it.\n- `ovcaq.workflow`: completed pure selection, frontmatter, resume, transition, policy, recoverable filesystem-record, and report services; it owns no process or launcher behavior.\n- `ovcaq.paths` and `ovcaq.config`: shared project/runtime discovery, containment, configuration precedence, agent tier settings, and phase routing; launcher-specific interpretation remains at the driver edge.\n- `ovcaq.runtime`: now owns runtime paths/configuration, the authoritative run lock, fleet registry, process groups, watchdog cleanup, raw session evidence, retention/purge, capabilities, and completion cleanup. `runtime_cli.py` projects those shared models into status/doctor/logs without becoming a second liveness authority.\n- `ovcaq.events` owns immutable protocol-1 observations, reduction and raw-first framing; `ovcaq.drivers` owns native decoding for both launchers under driver API 2. `runtime.session` owns private raw evidence and the decoder publishes normalized observations only afterward. The raw-first boundary must reject invalid native strings before a stateful adapter mutates identity/usage; genuine evidence writer failures must still propagate.\n- `ovcaq.tui` now projects the shared registry, state, metrics, normalized events,\n  and capability models; it owns presentation and exact-run controls but no\n  lifecycle policy or provider-native decoding. `ovcaq.provision` remains a\n  later-phase boundary.\n\n## Active Work\n\n- Current host batch `202610092123` names PRD `00051-triage-a-single-valid-codex-record-can-crash-th-v1.md`, phase/next phase `build`, full lane, review cap 2, and no completed PRDs. This host's `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` is launcher orchestration state, distinct from the product's nested v1 state contract; catchup only read it.\n- Exactly one WIP PRD: **UTF-8-safe normalized evidence** (00051), four unchecked tasks. It owns shared Unicode scalar validation, rejection before adapter mutation, exact raw evidence plus safe gaps/continuation, and strip→sanitize→bound diagnostic details. No live model calls. Named new regression files are not present at catchup baseline.\n- Authorized repair batch order is 00051, 00052 (reviewed exact CLI evidence), 00053 (adapter policy/routing/probe behavior), 00054 (activity projection/rendering). Historical HOLD items remain HOLD. The October 7 repair triage and `reviews/repair-backlog-20261007.md` explain this split.\n- Previous batch `202609062243` completed 00004–00009 with cycles 16/2/5/2/2/2. PRD 00009 completed 30/30 tasks at review cap 2, leaving 33 cycle-2 findings deferred plus earlier deferrals and assumptions. Cap-deferred completion did not establish absence of defects.\n- All PRD tasks must finish before the mandated review handoff. Full review runs in a fresh session with all required lenses; catchup does not run that review or mutate phase state.\n\n## Related context\n\n- Topic queried: `master UTF-8-safe normalized evidence`. Review harvest attempted 241 host-store review Markdown files; exited 1 with `no_repo`. Repo index and query also exited 1: OVCAQ is not registered with engram. Portfolio query timed out after a bounded 15-second attempt with no returned hits; this does not rule out related work.\n- Portfolio status collected during this catchup exited 1: memory 192 (23 stale, 1 dead), PRD 7,510 (5,259 dead), discovery 223 (112 dead), transcripts 17,713 (17,604 dead), code 50,308 (99 stale, 805 dead), findings 12,683 (0 stale/dead), code skipped 4,395. Missing kind/repo coverage was also reported. This stamp covers the queried portfolio scope.\n\n## GitHub State\n\n- No git remote or tags are configured. Local `master` at `e4d765c` and its primary worktree are the only branch/worktree. The latest commit prepared the reviewed adapter repair batch on October 7. GitHub issues, PR overlaps, remote branches, releases and live CI remain unverified; GitHub gathering was skipped because there is no remote.\n- Recent 20 master commits and local dirty-file statistics were loaded. Feature-branch comparison and its full-file consumer trace are inapplicable on master. Initial dirty paths were dispatch metrics/session evidence and the selected 00051 move from backlog to WIP; these are active workflow artifacts, not cleanup candidates.\n\n## Project Health\n\n- Latest preserved review verification (`00009-cycle2-verification.json` and cycle-2 review) records 5,591 passing tests, zero failures/skips, two temporary-directory cleanup warnings, and sync/lint/format/mypy/build passing at reviewed HEAD. Catchup did not rerun project gates or claim current live CI is green.\n- PRD 00051 addresses two confirmed October 7 repros: escaped lone surrogates reach strict normalized UTF-8 publication; a control-split synthetic token crossing the 4096-character cap survives as a fragment. The ordinary short reconstructed token is redacted by observation construction, so no real credential exposure is established. Re-check the exact premises before production edits, as each PRD task requires.\n- Shared scalar validation reaches both adapters, reducer-generated observations, serialized reloads and runtime/TUI consumers. Raw-first decoder changes affect `runtime.supervisor` and existing writer-error/evidence-bound tests. `bounded_detail` feeds Codex failure/rate-limit and receiver-lane payloads. Preserve semantic state before rejected frames, real storage-error propagation, valid Unicode and classification/reset from full native text.\n- Driver protocol documentation now matches API 2 and `default_prompt`; the prior capsule's placeholder/API-1 warnings were stale. Some actual activity fixture/rendering documentation remains assigned to 00054. README/AGENTS status prose still says planning/bootstrap despite implemented modules; the sequencing authority remains the implementation plan.\n- Retained raw evidence remains indefinite and private by default; purge is explicit, preview-first, cutoff-scoped, and active-session-safe. No canary, provisioning or launcher-global mutation is authorized by this catchup.\n- Host meta decisions/troubleshooting files and `agent_docs` were absent. Architecture, operator/protocol docs, source boundaries and fixture/test suites exist. Project memory loaded but reflects the August extraction inventory and legacy store paths; current files and PRDs supersede its historical status/version claims.\n- Task discovery returned `no_tasks_dir`, a benign restore skip. No prior tasks were restored and no task-store schema drift was reported. Engram registration and unavailable GitHub state are context gaps; neither blocks the local fixture/fake repair acceptance.\n\n## Project Memories\n\n- The extraction source is the characterized personal `run-autopilot` implementation. Port proven behavior rather than redesigning it, while applying the accepted v1 nesting, deep-validation, revision, and directory-fsync changes.\n- Current legacy state has many flat operational fields, marker files, compound task verbs, a rotating raw-byte backup, and a recoverable stall saga. PRD 00003 owns their state-schema disposition; PRD 00004 owns workflow transitions and filesystem lifecycle sagas.\n- Codex/Claude launcher capabilities and marketplace work are later PRDs. PRD 00003 must not introduce launcher-specific content or hardcoded personal paths.\n\n\nFILE docs/dev/tmp/tess-1-retry-2-output.md\n# Tess task 1 quality retry 2\n\nRemoved only `test_record_decoder_rejects_invalid_scalars_before_semantic_decoding`, including its high/low parametrization. It combined an unsupported event type with an invalid scalar and required an unspecified validation precedence. All other tests and assertions are unchanged, including legitimate scalar rejection and valid Unicode coverage.\n\nValidation command: `UV_CACHE_DIR=/private/tmp/ovcaq-uv-cache mise exec -- uv run pytest tests/events/test_unicode_safety.py -k scalar -q --tb=line` (300-second subprocess timeout).\n\nResult: **68 failed, 9 passed**, exit code **1**. Rejection cases report `DID NOT RAISE ValueError`; valid Unicode cases pass. Direct observations still accept the tested invalid scalars, so the independent tests continue to expose the required production fix. No production files were read or changed; no state mutations or commits were made.\n\nRead input remained below 100K. Scope was limited to the rendered dispatch and existing test file. The targeted deletion is complete; acceptance remains red pending implementation.\n\nASSUMPTIONS: none\n\n\nFILE docs/dev/tmp/tess-1-gate-2.txt\nRemove only test_record_decoder_rejects_invalid_scalars_before_semantic_decoding: it combines an unsupported event type with a scalar and requires a specific validation precedence absent from the direct-model contract. Direct observations must reject invalid strings, but no requirement overrides existing invalid event-type validation when both inputs are invalid. Before-native-semantic-decode admission belongs to raw-first task 2, not this serialized shared-model test. Retain every legitimate scalar rejection case and all valid Unicode cases. Do not edit any other assertion.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
