00051-triage-a-single-valid-codex-record-can-crash-th-v1.md'\nself fails, or the subsequent statectl write still fails), WRITE THE MARKER `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state-write-failed` with the Write tool — ONE line of JSON `{\"site\": \"statectl_fail\", \"detail\": \"<the statectl command and its raw stderr>\"}` — and END THE TURN: the wrapper halts on the marker and surfaces the `detail` itself, so no further state write is needed. Interactive sessions ALSO report the same failure to the user directly (there is no wrapper watching an interactive terminal). The raw stderr rides into the marker's `detail` so the failure is diagnosable, never silent (`rules/operating-principles.md`). (Lock contention on the single-writer model implies a second loop or a stale lock; the concurrent-loop guard below now refuses a second loop up front, so a persistent lock is a stale one — the retry clears it or the marker records it.)\n\nCreate `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/` and subdirectories if missing. Initialize state file at PRD selection. Update state at every phase transition. Autopilot also keeps a per-PRD **decision audit log** at `docs/dev/project-management/reviews/<prd-base>-audit.md`, **rendered once at Phase 9 finalize from the `state.json` decision arrays** (`autonomous_decisions`, `deferred_decisions`, `doubts`). `state.json` is the single in-run source of truth — decisions are NOT mirrored to `audit.md` incrementally per decision. Each rendered entry carries a **source** label (`autonomous`, `deferred`, or `doubt`); cycle/phase context goes in the entry body so the Phase 9 `decisions.md` projection can filter autonomous entries by label. The render is `autopilot render audit` (PRD 00107); the label set and the projection live in `references/audit-log-format.md`.\n\n**Invariant:** every state mutation that advances `phase` SHOULD also set `next_phase` to the same value. The three gates are `build` | `review` | `done` (plus `paused`). `build` is ONE session: selection, catchup, design, planning, and work all run under `phase: \"build\"` with no mid-build handoff. The review surface runs in its own fresh session; blind and doubt scrutiny are LENSES inside every review cycle (Blake and Bob in `review-work-completion`'s roster), not separate phases — reviewers get isolated contexts by construction (subagent prompts, external CLIs). Legacy `blind`/`doubt` phase values in pre-00015 state files map to `review` on resume. Per-task implementor tiering inside `/autopilot:work` is unchanged. The authoritative resume signal is `phase` + `phases_completed`; build sub-step skipping is by ARTIFACT (capsule freshness, design-doc-exists, tasks-exist, all-done), not by `phases_completed` membership. This resume decision (phase + phases_completed + artifact checks → next step) is encoded canonically in `cli/resume.py` (`scripts/resume_target.py` re-exports it), which `scripts/test_autopilot_resume.py` imports — editing the resume logic there flips a test red rather than silently drifting from this prose. Run `autopilot resume-target` at Phase 0 to print what that encoding says the next action is, and reconcile it against the handler you are about to follow: a disagreement means the prose and the encoded contract have drifted, and the encoding wins. It also runs the schema-version preflight, so an old or future `state.json` is surfaced there rather than resumed blindly. It answers for the `build` and `review` gates; `phase: \"done\"` returns `unknown phase: done` by design — the done gate is routed by the table above, not by this function.\n\n### Retention\n\nEverything under `docs/dev/project-management/` is tracked work-management state. It is never age-purged. Completed PRDs, reviews, designs, audit logs, reports, ledgers, deferred records, telemetry, and the project capsule therefore remain available to remotes. Runtime control files such as `state.json`, marker files, lane files, and wave slots may still be removed by their explicit lifecycle transitions, but never by a generic stale-file collector.\n\n- **Durable** (never delete): `docs/dev/project-management/prds/done/` and its PRDs, `docs/dev/project-management/reviews/` (per-cycle review files, blind/doubt reviews, and the `<prd-base>-audit.md` audit renders), `docs/dev/project-management/designs/` (per-PRD design docs), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/reports/` (batch reports), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/deferred/` (the `{batch_id}-deferred.json` records), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/ledger/loop-metrics.jsonl` (the ledger mirror, the durable copy to read), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/ledger/attempts.jsonl` (per-attempt rows appended at every PRD close, `references/state-schema.md` § Attempt ledger), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/ledger/custody.jsonl` (the custody journal, the push guard's restore source when the `critical-on-master` marker is removed, `references/state-schema.md` § Custody journal), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/ledger/dispatch-metrics.jsonl` (per-dispatch timing rows and session-handoff edges, `work/references/subagent-dispatch.md` § Dispatch telemetry), `docs/dev/project-management/meta/project-capsule.md`, and `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/ledger/<wave id>-wave.md` (the ledger mirror of the wave summary report `autopilot wave assemble` writes, the durable copy to read). The `reports/<wave id>-wave.md` copy it mirrors is tracked as well, but the ledger mirror is the one to read.\n- **Not durable** (untracked scratch): `docs/dev/tmp/`, the only tree `purge-devtmp` touches.\n- **Disposable** (removed by their lifecycle transitions, by name): `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/last-session.log`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/wrapper.log`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/pause-requested`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/paused-by-operator`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/session-brief.md` (re-rendered at every handoff), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.session-left` (overwritten at every loop-mode handoff), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/lanes/` (one marker per live CLI reviewer lane, removed when the lane exits), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` (at batch end only — in loop mode the wrapper archives it to `reports/{batch_id}-state-final.json`; see `references/phase-done.md`), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/wave.json`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/wave-slots/` and `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/review-paths` (a wave's control file, its per-wave review-slot directory, and the review's path-scoping list — the last removed with the assembly worktree at wave end, same lifecycle as the other two — none is a durable PRD-lifecycle artifact; `references/waves.md`), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/replan-context.md`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json.bak`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/**/*.lock` (the flock files beside `state.json` and beside each `deferred/*.json`), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.turn-counts.json`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.handoff-requested`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.cap-fired`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/contract-card.md`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/park-requested`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/last-verification.json`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.review-gate-blocks`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.review-gate-failed`, and `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.lane-guard-blocks`.\n\nThe machine form of this list is `docs/dev/project-management/.gitignore`, shipped by the Phase 0 `mkdir` step; a test keeps the two in step.\n\nTemporary review context and other reproducible scratch material belongs under the globally ignored `docs/dev/tmp/` tree. `purge-devtmp` operates only there, preserves files linked to live PRDs, and never traverses `docs/dev/project-management/`.\n\nBatch end and cleanup steps enumerate lifecycle-owned runtime files explicitly; they never remove a directory wholesale. This keeps a completed PRD's complete review trail intact after the batch closes (`references/design-rationale.md` § Verified moves).\n\n### Resuming\n\nWhen `/autopilot:run-autopilot` is invoked and `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` exists with `batch.completed_prds`, this is a continuation after a session restart. Preserve `batch.completed_prds` (including `batch.id`) and proceed to the build gate's Phase 0 to pick the next PRD.\n\nDelete `state.pause_reason` from `state.json` if present — unconditional, on every invocation, NOT gated on `batch.completed_prds`. A new session means any pause is being resumed; `pause_reason` belongs only to an unresolved pause and is not overwritten by normal progression, so it must be cleared here or it halts the resumed PRD's next hand-off. Cap-pause detection is unaffected — it keys on `cap_pause_reason`, which this delete does not touch.\n\n### Operator runbook (unattended batches)\n\nAll interaction with a running `autoclaude` batch happens at session boundaries — the only safe point. (Operator-shell commands for the human's own terminal, not for the model's Bash tool — `tail -f` would hang a session and pipes are hook-blocked.)\n\n- **Watch**: `tail -f docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/last-session.log` — the wrapper tees the live event log of the running headless turn there.\n- **Pause**: `touch docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/pause-requested` — the wrapper consumes the marker at the next session boundary, notifies \"paused by operator\", and stops the loop with state intact. It leaves `paused-by-operator` behind so tracon shows `⏸ paused` rather than `⚠ orphaned`; plain `autoclaude` resumes (nothing to clear, no decision to make). The marker may carry a JSON reason, `{\"reason\": \"...\"}`, which the stop line and the notification quote; a session that stands down for a peer writes one with a `condition` beside it, after asking the peer first (§ Session Loop, stand-down procedure).\n- **Take over**: with the loop stopped (pause marker, or Ctrl-C on the wrapper), run `/autopilot:run-autopilot` in a normal interactive session — resume-by-artifact reads the same `state.json`, and full interactive semantics (questions, PAUSEs) apply because `$_AUTOPILOT_LOOP` is unset. Restart `autoclaude` afterwards to go unattended again.\n- **Resume after a PAUSE** (`phase: \"paused\"` in `state.json`, \"session paused\" banner/ntfy): same take-over recipe, and it is the ONLY way forward — re-running `autoclaude` reads the paused state and exits again immediately. Interactively, the phase that paused re-runs with `AskUserQuestion` available, so the blockers that halted the loop are asked as decisions instead. Answer them, let the session hand off or finish the PRD, then re-run `autoclaude`.\n- **Un-park a sidelined PRD**: a PRD the loop parked (a `wrapper_died` death-park, or a loop-mode `design_gate` / `blocking_escalation` stall) sits in `docs/dev/project-management/prds/hold/` with its stall recorded in the batch deferred JSON; the batch kept draining without it. To resume it, `mv docs/dev/project-management/prds/hold/<00XXX-…>.md docs/dev/project-management/prds/backlog/` (keep the `00XXX-` prefix). Autopilot never reads `hold/`, so the next `/autopilot:run-autopilot` (or `autoclaude` relaunch) re-selects it from `backlog/` and re-enters the build gate, reusing its existing design doc via the Phase 1.5 artifact skip (a `design_gate` park then re-fires the gate interactively). Batch-end review lists every parked PRD (`{m} stalled`), so none is silently forgotten.\n- **Forensics**: `claude --resume <session-id>` (the id is in the init event at the top of `last-session.log`) reopens a finished headless conversation for questioning. Harmless by construction: sessions are disposable; `state.json` plus the artifacts are the only orchestration contract, so a resumed chat cannot fork the loop.\n- **Run a wave (parallel lanes)**: `autopilot wave plan`, then `autopilot wave launch`, `autopilot wave status` and `autopilot wave abort` — one detached loop per lane worktree, each over its own slice of the backlog. Full operator surface, including what is deferred: `references/waves.md`.\n- **Product QA (agoge)**: once a batch drains at least one PRD, the loop runs a single product-QA pass (`/run-agoge` over the repo), logging to `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/reports/{batch}-agoge.log`. A batch that drained nothing skips it with a line saying so; a QA failure is recorded and swallowed, never turning a successful drain into a failed one. `_AUTOPILOT_AGOGE_CAP` (default 3600s) bounds a hung run.\n\n- **One loop per repo**: a second `autoclaude` on a repo that already has a live loop refuses to start, naming the incumbent loop's PID (both the tracon front-end and the plain/headless path check the loop registry via `scripts/tracon_wrapper_alive.py` before registering). This prevents two loops double-draining the backlog and racing `state.json` writes. If the refusal is wrong (the named PID is dead), the stale registry entry is pruned automatically on the next launch; force it by deleting `~/.claude/autopilot-loops/<pid>.json`.\n\nA running headless turn is never interrupted except by the wrapper's wall-clock cap (`_AUTOPILOT_SESSION_MAX`, default 7200s); review-phase launches use `_AUTOPILOT_SESSION_MAX_REVIEW` (default 10800s). Fifteen minutes before the cap (`_AUTOPILOT_SESSION_WARN`, seconds, `0` disables) the wrapper writes the same `.handoff-requested` marker the context-cap hook writes, so a session that reaches a task boundary inside that window hands off cleanly instead of being killed mid-task. Past the cap, the watchdog does not signal a session that is still working: it waits for the session's log to go silent for `_AUTOPILOT_SESSION_IDLE` seconds (default 1200s; `0` restores the old kill-at-cap behavior) before SIGTERMing it, or until the session reaches twice the cap (the ceiling), whichever comes first.\n\nThe agoge run above arms the runtime-security lane by default, passing `--authorized autoclaude-drain`; `_AUTOPILOT_AGOGE_AUTHORIZED=0` is the kill switch, dropping just that flag so QA still runs, unarmed. Only the literal `0` disables it: anything else, including `false`, `no`, `off`, or `0` with a trailing space, leaves it armed, because a security brake should fail toward armed, not away from it. It brakes a default-on assertion; it is not a separate way to invoke one, since the assertion itself stays invocation-only.\n\n### Task Counts\n\n`tasks_total` and `tasks_completed` are maintained by whoever writes the `state.tasks` snapshot: recompute both in the same write — `tasks_total = len(tasks)`, `tasks_completed = count(status == \"completed\")` (the pidash-era PostToolUse sync hook is retired, PRD 00063). The model does not re-derive these counts by any other means; the arithmetic runs on the snapshot already in hand. Keep `state.tasks` accurate at phase transitions and recompute the counts alongside each `tasks` snapshot, ap\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
+{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc \"python3 -c \\\"from pathlib import Path; import json; print(Path('docs/dev/project-management/prds/wip/00051-triage-a-single-valid-codex-record-can-crash-th-v1.md').read_text()); d=json.loads(Path('/Users/bob/.claude/plugins/installed_plugins.json').read_text()); print({k:d['plugins'][k][0]['version'] for k in ['aegis@buvis-plugins','warden@buvis-plugins']}); print(Path('docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json').read_text()); print(Path('/Users/bob/.claude/plugins/cache/buvis-plugins/autopilot/0.9.0/skills/run-autopilot/cli/__main__.py').read_text()[:2500])\\\" \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc \"python3 -c \\\"from pathlib import Path; import json; print(Path('docs/dev/project-management/prds/wip/00051-triage-a-single-valid-codex-record-can-crash-th-v1.md').read_text()); d=json.loads(Path('/Users/bob/.claude/plugins/installed_plugins.json').read_text()); print({k:d['plugins'][k][0]['version'] for k in ['aegis@buvis-plugins','warden@buvis-plugins']}); print(Path('docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json').read_text()); print(Path('/Users/bob/.claude/plugins/cache/buvis-plugins/autopilot/0.9.0/skills/run-autopilot/cli/__main__.py').read_text()[:2500])\\\" \"","aggregated_output":"---\ncatchup: run\ndesign: run\ndefault_model: opus\nmodel_tier_rationale: new shared Unicode validation predicate and raw-first failure boundary require semantic continuity across adapters\nledger: deferred/202609062243-deferred.json\nledger_key: 8696acfef4a2\nsource_prd: 00009-codex-adapter-v1.md\nseverity: high\n---\n\n# UTF-8-safe normalized evidence\n\n## Overview\n\n### Problem Statement\nPRD 00009 cycle-2 R7: escaped lone surrogates survive decoding and crash\n`NormalizedEventWriter.write` during strict UTF-8 encoding. The raw-first decoder's\nexception boundary excludes publication, so the supervisor kills a healthy child.\nR2: `bounded_detail` redacts before removing controls and bounding; a reconstructed\ncredential fragment can escape the observation's second sanitizer at the cap.\nSources: [resolved discovery](../../discovery/00001-ovcaq-v1.md),\n[review 00009 cycle 2](../../reviews/00009-codex-adapter-v1-review-2.md), and\n[repair triage](../../autopilot/reports/repair-triage-20261007.md).\nPreserve this PRD's existing ledger metadata.\n\n### Target Users\nThe repository owner supervising fixture/fake and later real agent sessions.\n\n### Success Metrics\nThe named focused tests prove invalid Unicode degrades to `raw.unknown`, later\nvalid frames complete normally, raw bytes remain exact, and synthetic credential\nfragments never enter normalized failure/rate/lane details. No live model calls.\n\n## Functional Decomposition\n\n### Capability: Safe observations\nProvider-neutral validation prevents unserializable strings from entering shared models.\n\n#### Feature: Unicode scalar validation\n- **Description**: Reject decoded strings containing lone UTF-16 surrogate code points.\n- **Inputs**: Observation identifiers, payload values, nested mapping keys and native JSON records.\n- **Outputs**: Valid observations or a bounded normalization gap.\n- **Behavior**: Validate all strings recursively, including keys, before native-record semantic decoding and mutation. An invalid frame produces `raw.unknown` and no session/thread/lane/usage mutation; later frames see the pre-frame adapter state. Direct observation construction rejects invalid strings. Valid escaped surrogate pairs decode to their non-BMP scalar and remain supported. Preserve ordinary valid Unicode and existing malformed/non-JSON fallback behavior. Merely escaping invalid strings with `ensure_ascii=True`, replacing identifiers, or silently dropping characters does not satisfy this contract.\n\n#### Feature: Raw-first continuity\n- **Description**: Retain evidence and continue after an invalid scalar record.\n- **Inputs**: Durable raw byte chunks, a driver and normalized evidence writer.\n- **Outputs**: Exact raw evidence, valid normalized JSONL and later decoded observations.\n- **Behavior**: Preserve existing raw-before-decode ordering, frame bounds, digest/preview bounds and session binding. Reject invalid scalar frames before driver state changes; fallback must itself be UTF-8-safe and redacted. Invalid records must not emit terminal/lane/tool semantics or poison subsequent frames. Genuine raw/normalized writer failures, evidence limits and disk errors still propagate through the existing supervisor failure path; do not catch publication failures as harmless decode gaps.\n\n### Capability: Safe bounded details\nCredential redaction remains effective when terminal controls and caps interact.\n\n#### Feature: Strip, sanitize, bound\n- **Description**: Correct Codex diagnostic detail processing order.\n- **Inputs**: Native failure/rate-limit/lane detail strings with terminal controls.\n- **Outputs**: Sanitized details of at most 4096 characters.\n- **Behavior**: Remove the existing recognized ANSI/control forms, redact credentials from the resulting full string, then bound. Preserve failure classification and reset extraction from full native text. Cover ordinary split tokens and the exact synthetic cap reproducer: `'x'*4080 + ' sk-abc\\x1b[0mdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789ABCDEF'`; normalized output must contain neither the full reconstructed token nor `sk-abcdefghijkl`. Existing short-path whole-token second sanitization already works; this PRD does not claim a real credential exposure.\n\n## Structural Decomposition\n\n### Repository Structure\n\n```text\nsrc/ovcaq/\n  events/model.py                # scalar-safe observations and JSON values\n  events/tail.py                 # raw-first frame rejection and continuation\n  drivers/codex_events.py        # strip/sanitize/bound detail order\ntests/\n  events/test_unicode_safety.py  # new shared-model and raw-first regressions\n  drivers/test_codex_detail_safety.py # new actual detail/payload regressions\n  runtime/test_unicode_continuity.py # new real-subprocess supervisor proof\n  events/test_tail.py            # existing writer-failure preservation coverage\ndocs/driver-protocol.md          # normalization safety contract\nCHANGELOG.md                    # fixed behavior\n```\n\n### Module: events\n- **Maps to capability**: Safe observations\n- **Responsibility**: Validate strings and reject unsafe records without publishing semantic observations or losing durable evidence.\n- **Exports**: Existing `Observation`, `sanitize_json`, `observation_record`, `RawFirstDecoder` and `NormalizedEventWriter`; no new public command or provider-specific shared branch.\n\n### Module: diagnostic details\n- **Maps to capability**: Safe bounded details\n- **Responsibility**: Correct bounded diagnostic sanitization at the provider edge.\n- **Exports**: Existing `bounded_detail`; adapter classification/reset contracts unchanged.\n\n### Module: continuity evidence\n- **Maps to capability**: Safe observations and Safe bounded details\n- **Responsibility**: Headless actual-pipeline regressions, real subprocess continuity and accurate protocol documentation.\n- **Exports**: Named focused pytest files and documented behavior; no production modules.\n\n## Dependency Graph\n\n### Foundation Layer (Phase 0)\n- **events validation**: No dependencies; establishes Unicode scalar acceptance and nested-string rejection.\n\n### Core Layer (Phase 1)\n- **raw-first events**: Depends on [events validation]; rejects native invalid scalars before decoding/mutation.\n- **diagnostic details**: Depends on [events validation]; produces valid redacted bounded detail payloads.\n\n### Integration Layer (Phase 2)\n- **continuity evidence**: Depends on [raw-first events, diagnostic details, existing supervisor/fake harness]; proves real-process continuation and documents the contract.\n\n## Implementation Phases\n\n### Phase 0: Foundation\n**Goal**: Establish safe string validation without changing valid observation semantics.\n\n**Tasks**:\n- [ ] Implement shared scalar validation and direct observation regressions (no deps) - Acceptance: Re-check that direct observations accept invalid scalars before editing; if already fixed, skip production edits and report while retaining independent tests. `mise exec -- uv run pytest tests/events/test_unicode_safety.py -k scalar` passes; cases cover lone high/low surrogates in identifiers, values and nested keys, ordinary Unicode and valid escaped pairs, with explicit rejected/accepted assertions.\n\n**Exit Criteria**: Invalid scalars cannot inhabit normalized observation identifiers or payloads.\n\n### Phase 1: Core\n**Goal**: Recover malformed native frames before mutation and close the credential-cap gap.\n\n**Tasks**:\n- [ ] Integrate pre-decode invalid-scalar rejection and safe raw fallback (depends on: Phase 0) - Acceptance: Re-check R7 before edits; if already safely recovered without adapter mutation, skip production edits and report while retaining independent tests. `mise exec -- uv run pytest tests/events/test_unicode_safety.py tests/events/test_tail.py` passes; byte-exact raw, gap digest/preview, unchanged adapter identity/usage and later healthy terminal are asserted; existing genuine writer failures still raise.\n- [ ] Correct strip/sanitize/bound ordering with real failure/rate/lane payload regressions (depends on: Phase 0) - Acceptance: Re-check the exact R2 cap reproducer before edits; if already redacted, skip production edits and report while retaining independent tests. `mise exec -- uv run pytest tests/drivers/test_codex_detail_safety.py tests/drivers/test_codex_decode_terminals.py` passes; the exact cap reproducer and ANSI/NUL splits redact before truncation, preserve limits, and do not change failure class/reset semantics.\n\n**Exit Criteria**: Unsafe records yield only normalization gaps; synthetic secrets and fragments are absent from bounded normalized details.\n\n### Phase 2: Integration\n**Goal**: Prove supervised child continuity and record the final operator contract.\n\n**Tasks**:\n- [ ] Add actual subprocess supervisor continuity tests and update protocol/changelog (depends on: Phase 1) - Acceptance: `mise exec -- uv run pytest tests/runtime/test_unicode_continuity.py` passes; a temporary fake child emits unsafe native records, later valid identity/usage and terminal evidence, remains alive until its normal exit, and leaves exact raw plus reloadable normalized evidence; a forced genuine normalized writer failure still aborts through the established runtime path. Re-check that R7/R2 premises remain before edits; if already fixed, retain independent regression coverage and report rather than force churn.\n\n**Exit Criteria**: Focused acceptance passes, then final project gates pass once: `mise exec -- uv sync --all-groups`, `mise exec -- uv run pytest`, `mise exec -- uv run ruff check .`, `mise exec -- uv run ruff format --check .`, `mise exec -- uv run mypy src/ovcaq`, `mise exec -- uv build`.\n\n## Test Strategy\n\n### Critical Scenarios\n- **Happy path**: Valid Unicode, escaped surrogate pairs and ordinary native JSON → unchanged supported semantic observations and normal completion.\n- **Edge case**: Invalid scalar thread/lane ID, agent/error text or nested key → one bounded raw gap, no semantic or adapter mutation, exact raw bytes and later valid completion.\n- **Edge case**: ANSI/NUL-split synthetic credential crossing detail cap → complete redaction before bounding in actual failure/rate/lane payloads.\n- **Error case**: Raw/normalized I/O failure or evidence exhaustion → existing failure propagation, never swallowed as malformed input.\n\n## Risks\n\n- **State contamination**: Reject invalid native strings before invoking stateful decoding; assert pre/post identity and usage with subsequent frames.\n- **Weakened storage errors**: Limit tolerant handling to validation/decoding; retain existing writer-failure tests and add supervised failure evidence.\n- **Scope expansion**: No version-pin refresh, model routing, lane status, retry policy, activity UI, provider-default naming or probe ownership changes; those have separate dispositions.\n\n{'aegis@buvis-plugins': '0.4.0', 'warden@buvis-plugins': '0.14.0'}\n{\n  \"phase\": \"build\",\n  \"next_phase\": \"build\",\n  \"schema_version\": 1\n}\n\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"cli/__main__.py - argparse dispatch for the autopilot state CLI.\n\nThe `autopilot` bash function (development.plugin.bash) runs this file\ndirectly: `python3 .../cli/__main__.py <subcommand> [flags...]`. It wraps\ncli/state.py and cli/records.py as real CLI verbs - no library semantics\nlive here, only argument mapping, exit-code passthrough, and default\n--state resolution.\n\nSubcommands:\n    init      --state --prd\n        Create a fresh state.json ({\"prd\", \"phase\": \"build\",\n        \"next_phase\": \"build\"}) via state.init().\n    enter     --state --prd --prds\n        enter.enter(): the Phase 0 step chain. Prints its one-line JSON\n        result (the `stop` key names the halt, when there is one); the\n        frontmatter warnings go to stderr, never onto that line.\n    stall     --state --prd --site --detail --prds\n        records.do_stall(); autopilot_dir is always the state file's own\n        parent directory (no flag).\n    park      --state --prds --autopilot-dir\n        records.do_park().\n    reset-prd --state\n        One state.transaction() running records.reset_prd_fields under an\n        owned-fields validator (the reset scalars only).\n    defer     --state --prd --batch --json\n        records.record_defer() with a JSON-parsed record payload.\n    restore   --state\n        state.restore(): rolls <state>.bak back over state.json.\n    check-plan --state --prd --ceiling\n        policy.plan_expansion(); exit 3 on a stall verdict (task count over\n        the loop ceiling, expansion ratio, or module drift), having written\n        <state dir>/split-notes/<prd-stem>.md; exit 2 on an unreadable\n        state or PRD, or when that split note cannot be written.\n    select    --prds\n        selection.select() over the wip/ and backlog/ listings, gated by\n        each backlog candidate's `eligibility:` frontmatter check\n        (cli/eligibility.py). Prints {\"prd\": ..., \"source\":\n        \"wip\"|\"backlog\"|\"drained\", \"skipped\": [...]}. A candidate whose\n        check does not exit 0 is SKIPPED - it stays in backlog/, gets no\n        session, and is never parked - and its record lands in the printed\n        list and in state.batch.skips[] when a state file exists. Decides\n        only - the verified backlog->wip move stays with the caller.\n    frontmatter --state --prd\n        frontmatter.parse(), plus lane.classify() over the same text\n        (`lane`, `lane_reason`, `lane_effective`; the one reader of\n        _AUTOPILOT_LANES), applied to state in \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
