+{"timestamp":"2026-10-10T08:31:41.853Z","ordinal":31,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a124f0-1d2f-7e71-b398-bf83f0d21ade","turn_id":"01a124f0-1d92-7032-9129-a3c02ede9f09","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-750e908a-405d-4280-8288-badf5849f827","process_id":"64480","command":["/opt/homebrew/bin/bash","-lc","mise exec -- python -c 'import pathlib; import signal; signal.alarm(60); paths = [\"/Users/bob/.agents/skills/python-patterns/SKILL.md\", \"/Users/bob/.agents/skills/python-testing/SKILL.md\", \"tests/drivers/test_codex_detail_safety.py\"]; [(print(\"PATH:\", p), print(pathlib.Path(p).read_text() if pathlib.Path(p).exists() else \"MISSING\")) for p in paths]'"],"cwd":"file:///Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/ovcaq","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"mise exec -- python -c 'import pathlib; import signal; signal.alarm(60); paths = [\"/Users/bob/.agents/skills/python-patterns/SKILL.md\", \"/Users/bob/.agents/skills/python-testing/SKILL.md\", \"tests/drivers/test_codex_detail_safety.py\"]; [(print(\"PATH:\", p), print(pathlib.Path(p).read_text() if pathlib.Path(p).exists() else \"MISSING\")) for p in paths]'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"PATH: /Users/bob/.agents/skills/python-patterns/SKILL.md\n---\nname: python-patterns\ndescription: Use when writing, reviewing, or refactoring Python code. Covers idioms, naming, traps, toolchain, and security. Triggers on Python file edits, \"pythonic\", \"PEP 8\", \"python best practices\", \"python idioms\", \"bandit\".\ncompatibility: \"Portable standalone compatibility copy of the strunk plugin skill for agents without that plugin; Claude Code uses the namespaced plugin skill.\"\n---\n\n# Python Patterns\n\nPick a side. These are the rulings, not a tutorial.\n\n## Idioms that earn their place\n\n| Rule | Why |\n|------|-----|\n| `X \\| Y`, not `Union[X, Y]` | 3.10+ runtime syntax; clearer. On 3.9 it parses only under `from __future__ import annotations` and still breaks `get_type_hints`/`isinstance` — keep `Optional`/`Union` if you support 3.9. |\n| `Protocol`, not `ABC`, for duck typing | Structural typing; no inheritance you don't control. |\n| `@dataclass(frozen=True)` for immutable records, plain `dataclass` only when you must mutate, `NamedTuple` for lightweight immutable rows | Pydantic only when you need validation. |\n| `raise New(...) from e` | Marks the explicit cause. A bare `raise New()` still chains implicitly (\"During handling...\"); use `from None` to suppress. |\n| `@functools.wraps` on every decorator | Without it the wrapper loses `__name__`, `__doc__`, signature. |\n| `pathlib.Path`, not `os.path` | One object, no string surgery. |\n\n## Naming (PEP 8)\n\n`snake_case` for modules, functions, variables. `CapWords` for classes. `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` for constants. Leading `_` for internal names.\n\n**Never name a `.py` file in kebab-case.** `my-script.py` is not importable (`import my-script` is a `SyntaxError`). Kebab-case belongs only in the `pyproject.toml` distribution name and the CLI command, both of which map to a `snake_case` module.\n\n## Traps\n\n```python\n# Mutable default argument — shared across calls\ndef bad(items=[]): ...\ndef good(items=None):\n    items = [] if items is None else items\n\n# Bare except — swallows SystemExit, KeyboardInterrupt\ntry: ...\nexcept Exception as e: ...        # name the exception\n\n# __slots__ kills __dict__ and dynamic attributes — use only on fixed,\n# memory-sensitive classes you won't extend.\n\n# __double_leading is name-mangled to _Class__double_leading.\n# Surprises you in subclasses, pickling, and monkey-patching.\n\nif value is None: ...             # never == None\n```\n\nAvoid `from module import *`. Use `isinstance`, never `type(x) ==`.\n\n## Toolchain\n\n**black** to format, **isort** for imports, **ruff** to lint. Type annotations on every function signature — no exceptions for \"obvious\" returns.\n\n## Resources and laziness\n\nContext managers (`with`) for anything holding a resource. Generators for lazy iteration over anything you would otherwise materialize into a list.\n\n## Security\n\n- Read secrets with `os.environ[\"API_KEY\"]`, never `.get()` — a missing secret must raise `KeyError` at startup, not surface as `None` at request time.\n- `python-dotenv` loads a local `.env` in development only. Never commit one.\n- `bandit -r src/` for static security analysis.\n\nPATH: /Users/bob/.agents/skills/python-testing/SKILL.md\n---\nname: python-testing\ndescription: Use when writing, fixing, or reviewing Python tests with pytest. Covers TDD, fixtures, mocking, parametrization, coverage. Triggers on \"pytest\", \"python test\", \"conftest\", \"pytest.mark\", \"parametrize\", \"pytest fixture\", \"python mock\".\ncompatibility: \"Portable standalone compatibility copy of the strunk plugin skill for agents without that plugin; Claude Code uses the namespaced plugin skill.\"\n---\n\n# Python Testing\n\nTDD: write the failing test (RED), make it pass (GREEN), refactor. One behavior per test. No logic in tests. Coverage target 80%, higher on critical paths.\n\n## Rulings\n\n**Mocking**\n- Patch where the name is *used*, not where it's defined: `@patch(\"myapp.service.requests\")`, not `@patch(\"requests\")`.\n- `autospec=True` so the mock rejects calls the real API would reject.\n- Mock at the boundary (I/O, network, clock). Never mock the thing under test.\n- Async: assert with `assert_awaited_once()`, not `assert_called_once()`.\n\n**Fixtures**\n- `yield` for teardown; the line after `yield` is the cleanup.\n- Default to function scope. Widen to `module`/`session` only for genuinely expensive resources.\n- `autouse` hides dependencies — use sparingly; prefer explicit injection.\n- `tmp_path` for temp files (auto-cleanup, `Path` API), not `tempfile`.\n\n**Parametrize**\n- `@pytest.mark.parametrize` over loops — distinct test IDs, filterable with `-k`.\n- Pass `ids=[...]` when params are objects or tuples; pytest already auto-names plain str/int/bool readably.\n\n**Config**\n- One config in `pyproject.toml` (`[tool.pytest.ini_options]`), not a separate `pytest.ini`.\n- `--strict-markers` so a typo'd marker fails instead of silently passing.\n- Register custom markers — e.g. `slow`, `@pytest.mark.unit`, `@pytest.mark.integration` — so `--strict-markers` catches a typo.\n- Coverage locally: `pytest --cov=src --cov-report=term-missing` — the missing-line report is the point; a bare percentage is not actionable.\n\n**Async (pytest-asyncio)**\n- Default mode is `strict`: an async test with no `@pytest.mark.asyncio` is silently not collected. Set `asyncio_mode = \"auto\"` in `[tool.pytest.ini_options]` to drop the per-test marker.\n- Use `@pytest_asyncio.fixture` for async fixtures in strict mode.\n\nPATH: tests/drivers/test_codex_detail_safety.py\nMISSING\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":7958}},"started_at_ms":1791621101853,"completed_at_ms":1791621101853}}
+{"timestamp":"2026-10-10T08:31:41.855Z","ordinal":32,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a124f0-791f-7172-a6fe-d068ac97fa0d","call_id":"call_866e4c42637445cb82467cea0003dc9a","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"7b811d\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000007958,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":1378,\"output\":\"PATH: /Users/bob/.agents/skills/python-patterns/SKILL.md\\n---\\nname: python-patterns\\ndescription: Use when writing, reviewing, or refactoring Python code. Covers idioms, naming, traps, toolchain, and security. Triggers on Python file edits, \\\"pythonic\\\", \\\"PEP 8\\\", \\\"python best practices\\\", \\\"python idioms\\\", \\\"bandit\\\".\\ncompatibility: \\\"Portable standalone compatibility copy of the strunk plugin skill for agents without that plugin; Claude Code uses the namespaced plugin skill.\\\"\\n---\\n\\n# Python Patterns\\n\\nPick a side. These are the rulings, not a tutorial.\\n\\n## Idioms that earn their place\\n\\n| Rule | Why |\\n|------|-----|\\n| `X \\\\| Y`, not `Union[X, Y]` | 3.10+ runtime syntax; clearer. On 3.9 it parses only under `from __future__ import annotations` and still breaks `get_type_hints`/`isinstance` — keep `Optional`/`Union` if you support 3.9. |\\n| `Protocol`, not `ABC`, for duck typing | Structural typing; no inheritance you don't control. |\\n| `@dataclass(frozen=True)` for immutable records, plain `dataclass` only when you must mutate, `NamedTuple` for lightweight immutable rows | Pydantic only when you need validation. |\\n| `raise New(...) from e` | Marks the explicit cause. A bare `raise New()` still chains implicitly (\\\"During handling...\\\"); use `from None` to suppress. |\\n| `@functools.wraps` on every decorator | Without it the wrapper loses `__name__`, `__doc__`, signature. |\\n| `pathlib.Path`, not `os.path` | One object, no string surgery. |\\n\\n## Naming (PEP 8)\\n\\n`snake_case` for modules, functions, variables. `CapWords` for classes. `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` for constants. Leading `_` for internal names.\\n\\n**Never name a `.py` file in kebab-case.** `my-script.py` is not importable (`import my-script` is a `SyntaxError`). Kebab-case belongs only in the `pyproject.toml` distribution name and the CLI command, both of which map to a `snake_case` module.\\n\\n## Traps\\n\\n```python\\n# Mutable default argument — shared across calls\\ndef bad(items=[]): ...\\ndef good(items=None):\\n    items = [] if items is None else items\\n\\n# Bare except — swallows SystemExit, KeyboardInterrupt\\ntry: ...\\nexcept Exception as e: ...        # name the exception\\n\\n# __slots__ kills __dict__ and dynamic attributes — use only on fixed,\\n# memory-sensitive classes you won't extend.\\n\\n# __double_leading is name-mangled to _Class__double_leading.\\n# Surprises you in subclasses, pickling, and monkey-patching.\\n\\nif value is None: ...             # never == None\\n```\\n\\nAvoid `from module import *`. Use `isinstance`, never `type(x) ==`.\\n\\n## Toolchain\\n\\n**black** to format, **isort** for imports, **ruff** to lint. Type annotations on every function signature — no exceptions for \\\"obvious\\\" returns.\\n\\n## Resources and laziness\\n\\nContext managers (`with`) for anything holding a resource. Generators for lazy iteration over anything you would otherwise materialize into a list.\\n\\n## Security\\n\\n- Read secrets with `os.environ[\\\"API_KEY\\\"]`, never `.get()` — a missing secret must raise `KeyError` at startup, not surface as `None` at request time.\\n- `python-dotenv` loads a local `.env` in development only. Never commit one.\\n- `bandit -r src/` for static security analysis.\\n\\nPATH: /Users/bob/.agents/skills/python-testing/SKILL.md\\n---\\nname: python-testing\\ndescription: Use when writing, fixing, or reviewing Python tests with pytest. Covers TDD, fixtures, mocking, parametrization, coverage. Triggers on \\\"pytest\\\", \\\"python test\\\", \\\"conftest\\\", \\\"pytest.mark\\\", \\\"parametrize\\\", \\\"pytest fixture\\\", \\\"python mock\\\".\\ncompatibility: \\\"Portable standalone compatibility copy of the strunk plugin skill for agents without that plugin; Claude Code uses the namespaced plugin skill.\\\"\\n---\\n\\n# Python Testing\\n\\nTDD: write the failing test (RED), make it pass (GREEN), refactor. One behavior per test. No logic in tests. Coverage target 80%, higher on critical paths.\\n\\n## Rulings\\n\\n**Mocking**\\n- Patch where the name is *used*, not where it's defined: `@patch(\\\"myapp.service.requests\\\")`, not `@patch(\\\"requests\\\")`.\\n- `autospec=True` so the mock rejects calls the real API would reject.\\n- Mock at the boundary (I/O, network, clock). Never mock the thing under test.\\n- Async: assert with `assert_awaited_once()`, not `assert_called_once()`.\\n\\n**Fixtures**\\n- `yield` for teardown; the line after `yield` is the cleanup.\\n- Default to function scope. Widen to `module`/`session` only for genuinely expensive resources.\\n- `autouse` hides dependencies — use sparingly; prefer explicit injection.\\n- `tmp_path` for temp files (auto-cleanup, `Path` API), not `tempfile`.\\n\\n**Parametrize**\\n- `@pytest.mark.parametrize` over loops — distinct test IDs, filterable with `-k`.\\n- Pass `ids=[...]` when params are objects or tuples; pytest already auto-names plain str/int/bool readably.\\n\\n**Config**\\n- One config in `pyproject.toml` (`[tool.pytest.ini_options]`), not a separate `pytest.ini`.\\n- `--strict-markers` so a typo'd marker fails instead of silently passing.\\n- Register custom markers — e.g. `slow`, `@pytest.mark.unit`, `@pytest.mark.integration` — so `--strict-markers` catches a typo.\\n- Coverage locally: `pytest --cov=src --cov-report=term-missing` — the missing-line report is the point; a bare percentage is not actionable.\\n\\n**Async (pytest-asyncio)**\\n- Default mode is `strict`: an async test with no `@pytest.mark.asyncio` is silently not collected. Set `asyncio_mode = \\\"auto\\\"` in `[tool.pytest.ini_options]` to drop the per-test marker.\\n- Use `@pytest_asyncio.fixture` for async fixtures in strict mode.\\n\\nPATH: tests/drivers/test_codex_detail_safety.py\\nMISSING\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a124f0-1d92-7032-9129-a3c02ede9f09","create_time":1791621101.855424}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
