+{"timestamp":"2026-10-10T08:55:24.768Z","ordinal":20,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a12506-016b-7cd3-8623-7c626aa9ed07","turn_id":"01a12506-01bd-7992-a4dc-77d940f87b6d","session_id":"01a124e8-496d-7440-9872-5a4cd870b000","root_turn_id":"01a124e8-52ac-7961-9e8c-72ebb43216e7","response_id":"resp_055f70855de41c54016ac9fd7639148191bc3d924eadc31820","usage":{"input_tokens":28765,"cached_input_tokens":23040,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":209,"reasoning_output_tokens":83,"total_tokens":28974},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":51983,"cached_input_tokens":45824,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":300,"reasoning_output_tokens":83,"total_tokens":52283},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":51983,"cached_input_tokens":45824,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":300,"reasoning_output_tokens":83,"total_tokens":52283}}}
+{"timestamp":"2026-10-10T08:55:24.833Z","ordinal":21,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a12506-016b-7cd3-8623-7c626aa9ed07","turn_id":"01a12506-01bd-7992-a4dc-77d940f87b6d","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-28d21528-6528-4f85-b50a-20ace42dd430","process_id":"99747","command":["/opt/homebrew/bin/bash","-lc","python -c 'import subprocess,sys; subprocess.run([sys.executable,\"-c\",\"from pathlib import Path; print(Path(\\\"docs/driver-protocol.md\\\").read_text()); print(Path(\\\"CHANGELOG.md\\\").read_text())\"],check=True,timeout=60)'"],"cwd":"file:///Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/ovcaq","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"python -c 'import subprocess,sys; subprocess.run([sys.executable,\"-c\",\"from pathlib import Path; print(Path(\\\"docs/driver-protocol.md\\\").read_text()); print(Path(\\\"CHANGELOG.md\\\").read_text())\"],check=True,timeout=60)'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"# Driver and observation protocol\n\nOVCAQ version 1 separates provider-specific drivers from provider-neutral\nobservations. The public contracts described here are the implemented exports of\n`ovcaq.drivers` and `ovcaq.events`; this document is not a command inventory.\n\n## Driver contract\n\n`AgentDriver` implementations provide:\n\n- `name`, the advertised driver name;\n- `default_prompt(phase)`, the driver's native phase prompt;\n- `probe()`, which reports `DriverHealth`;\n- `build_launch(request)`, which converts a `LaunchRequest` into a `LaunchSpec`;\n- `decode(raw_line)`, which converts one raw line into zero or more\n  `Observation` values; and\n- `summarize(observations, exit_code)`, which returns a `SessionSummary`.\n\n`LaunchSpec` contains an argv tuple, an environment overlay, a `Path` working\ndirectory, and the raw-output format. The later supervisor deliberately merges\nthat overlay into its prepared host environment. OVCAQ executes argv directly;\nconfigured commands are never shell-evaluated.\n\nExtensions expose a `DriverPlugin(api_version, factory)`, where `factory`\nimplements `DriverFactory`. `discover_drivers` combines built-in drivers with\nplugins from the `ovcaq.drivers` entry-point group. Discovery validates the\nadvertised name, rejects duplicate names (`DuplicateDriverError`), rejects\nconstruction or health failures (`DriverDiscoveryError`), and requires the\nplugin API version to equal `DRIVER_API_VERSION`, currently `2`. A different\nversion is rejected with `IncompatibleDriverError`; version negotiation is not\nperformed.\n\nThe CLI foreground launch path calls the selected driver's `probe()` before\nrunning the supervisor. Unhealthy version or authentication evidence rejects\nthe launch with a bounded, sanitized runtime error before agent execution.\nDirectly constructed supervisors do not probe their injected drivers.\n\n`DriverFactory` is a zero-argument callable, but a registration may use a\nclosure to capture configuration and the per-run identifier, for\nexample `lambda: ClaudeDriver(settings, run_id=run_id)`, where the caller\nsupplies `run_id`. A decoder has mutable session state, and the supervisor\nreuses its driver between sessions. `build_launch` must reset session-local\ndecoder state while retaining the supplied run identifier. Codex resets its\nthread identity, cumulative usage and remembered item/lane identities. A new\nsession must not inherit earlier usage or failure evidence; summarization uses\nthe supplied observations, not stale private decoder fields.\n\nBefore OVCAQ 1.0, driver ABI and observation-protocol changes may require\ncoordinated upgrades of core and extensions. Compatibility is deliberately\nstrict: there are no compatibility shims or dual writers.\n\n## Observation contract\n\n`PROTOCOL_VERSION` is exactly `1`. An `Observation` has a validated UTC\ntimestamp and identifiers, an `ObservationType`, and a deeply immutable\npayload. Version 1 observation types are:\n\n- `session.started`, `session.completed`, and `session.failed`;\n- `activity.message`;\n- `tool.started`, `tool.completed`, and `tool.failed`;\n- `lane.started`, `lane.progress`, `lane.completed`, and `lane.failed`;\n- `usage.updated`;\n- `rate_limit.started` and `rate_limit.cleared`; and\n- `raw.unknown`.\n\nDuring supervised decoding, envelope `run_id` and `session_id` identify the\nowning OVCAQ run and session. Before rebinding those IDs, the raw-first decoder\nretains each startup observation's original session ID in its sanitized\n`source_session_id` payload field. Reduction compares that normalized source\nidentity independently of envelope ownership: distinct startup identities\nproduce an `unknown` failure even with terminal completion or a nonzero exit;\nrepeated identical startup identities do not. Raw and normalized evidence are\nretained. This uses observation protocol 1 and driver API 2 unchanged.\n\nA `rate_limit.started` payload has exactly `reset_at` (an RFC 3339 UTC string\nor null), `window` (a text limit-window label or null), and `detail` (text).\nA provider `allowed_warning` arrives as `activity.message` with payload `kind`\n`rate_limit_warning` plus `window`, `reset_at`, and `detail`, and never as\n`rate_limit.cleared`. A terminal banner reset applies unless a rejected event\nwith a credible future reset is still live (not cleared by a later `allowed`).\nA later rejected event without a credible reset ends that live limit, so a\nbanner after `rejected(R) -> rejected(no reset)` applies its own reset. A dated\nbanner picks its year first (the anchor's year, or the next one once that date\nhas passed) and resolves to no reset when the date is invalid in that year.\n\n`FailureClass` values are `rate_limit`, `connectivity`, `permission`,\n`authentication`, `timeout`, `agent`, and `unknown`. `Usage` represents usage\nreported by observations. `session_failure_observation` constructs the public\nfailure observation form.\n\nUse `observation_record` and `observation_from_record` for the serialized\nobservation representation. `sanitize_text` and `sanitize_json` redact\ncredential-shaped keys and credential assignments. Unknown previews are\nbounded. A preview of a frame longer than its 512-byte window is cut back to\nthe last whitespace; a window with no whitespace previews as empty when it\ncontains `sk-` (case-insensitive) and is kept otherwise. A frame's trailing CR\nnever reaches the preview. Malformed, oversized, unknown, or decoder-failing\ninput is handled totally: it becomes a `raw.unknown` observation with a bounded\nsanitized preview and SHA-256 evidence instead of crashing the supervisor.\n\n`reduce_session` retains the observations and deterministically produces a\n`Reduction` containing a `SessionSummary`. `summary_record` serializes that\nsummary. Summaries report outcome, failure, reset, and usage information; they\nnever select workflow phases. Provider-native data never enters workflow state.\nLifecycle decisions use committed state and normalized results, not prose or a\nsummary choosing the next phase.\n\nEvery `usage.updated` observation is a cumulative snapshot for its session,\nnever a per-turn delta. Message-derived totals are estimated; a terminal result\nsnapshot is exact and contains only fields reported by the provider. Reduction\nmerges each numeric field independently by maximum, including cost, so duplicate\nand out-of-order snapshots cannot reduce or double-count totals. An exact\nsnapshot clears `estimated` only for winning values it exactly replaces; the\naggregate remains estimated while any fieldwise maximum has no exact\nreplacement.\n\n## Codex exec contract\n\nThe supported evidence pin is **codex-cli 0.160.0**, with raw format\n`codex-exec-json-v1`. The [versioned provenance](../tests/fixtures/codex/v1/provenance.json)\ndistinguishes captured version/help stdout from schema-synthetic JSONL. Captured\nhelp replaces the generic launcher-home config directory with `[LAUNCHER_HOME]`;\nprovenance records this artifact-safety substitution. Its\nofficial sources are the pinned [exec event schema](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/rust-v0.160.0/codex-rs/exec/src/exec_events.rs)\nand [JSONL producer](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/rust-v0.160.0/codex-rs/exec/src/event_processor_with_jsonl_output.rs).\nThe fixtures are not live workflow or authentication transcripts, and fake\nphase execution is not an external-plugin smoke test. Future native records\nmay remain raw-only rather than silently extending this contract.\n\n### Launch and configuration\n\n`CodexDriver(settings: AgentSettings, *, run_id: str)` accepts only `sandbox`,\n`approval` and `allow_explicit_model` options. Sandbox and approval default to\n`workspace-write` and `never`; other values or wrong types fail with\n`ValueError`. `allow_explicit_model` defaults to `False` and must be a bool.\nUnknown options fail. Configuration stores the command as an argv tuple,\nincluding any wrapper prefix arguments.\n\nThe launch order is exactly:\n\n```python\nargv = (\n    *settings.command,\n    \"--ask-for-approval\",\n    \"never\",\n    \"exec\",\n    \"--json\",\n    \"--sandbox\",\n    \"workspace-write\",\n    \"-C\",\n    str(request.root),\n    # Optional model segment:\n    \"-m\",\n    resolved_model,\n    # Optional mapped effort segment (high example):\n    \"-c\",\n    'model_reasoning_effort=\"high\"',\n    \"--\",\n    request.prompt,\n)\n```\n\nAn absent model omits its segment. A configured tier resolves through\n`settings.tiers`; an already resolved tier value is accepted unchanged. An\narbitrary explicit model requires `allow_explicit_model=True`. Shared modules\nprovide no provider model default. Absent effort omits its segment. `low`,\n`medium`, `high` and `xhigh` map identically to `model_reasoning_effort` with a\nquoted TOML value. Blank effort fails; another nonblank value logs one warning\ncontaining the requested value and omits the override without guessing.\n\n`LaunchSpec.cwd` is `request.root`; its environment is a copy of the configured\noverlay, without provider-specific host-variable or credential copies. The\nlauncher manages inherited authenticated storage. `build_launch` neither\nspawns nor provisions. The shared runner closes stdin and spawns without a\nshell; wrapper arguments and prompt shell syntax remain literal data. The\nfinal `--` also protects prompts beginning with `-`. Runtime enforces the\nrequest timeout without another CLI flag.\n\n### Decoding and terminal evidence\n\nCodex observations use protocol 1, provider `codex`, the supplied run ID and\ncurrent thread as session ID before supervised ownership rebinding. The native\nthread ID then survives in startup `source_session_id` payloads. Pinned native\nrecords have no timestamp, so the adapter uses aware UTC receipt time.\nValidated observations contain normalized,\nsanitized payloads. Malformed JSON (including recursion errors), wrong shapes,\nunsupported item kinds/statuses and future records fall back to `raw.unknown`\nwith an original-line SHA-256, sanitized preview of at most 512 characters and\na bounded reason. Raw evidence remains available even when projection fails.\n\n| Native evidence | Normalized projection |\n| --- | --- |\n| `thread.started` | `session.started` with thread identity |\n| `turn.started` | `activity.message`, kind `turn` |\n| Agent/reasoning items | `activity.message`, kind `text`/`thinking`, bounded text and item ID |\n| Command/file/MCP/web items | Tool lifecycle with item ID as `tool_id`, neutral name and bounded detail |\n| Todo items | `activity.message`, kind `plan`, bounded steps |\n| Collaboration items | Tool lifecycle and receiver-attributed lane evidence when available |\n| Nonfatal error item | `activity.message`, kind `error`; no session failure |\n| `turn.completed` | `usage.updated` and `session.completed` |\n| `turn.failed` | `session.failed`, plus `rate_limit.started` when classified |\n| Top-level `error` | Provisional error activity with normalized failure metadata |\n\nThe adapter recognizes `item.started`, `item.updated` and `item.completed`.\nTool failure requires native failure/decline status, explicit native error or\nnonzero command exit. Ordinary command output is not provider-health evidence.\nFile detail summarizes paths and change kinds; MCP and web detail is sanitized\nand bounded to 4096 characters, rather than retained as wholesale results.\n\nCollaboration requires `agents_states` keys matching native receiver thread\nIDs. Pending/running receivers start or progress a lane, completed receivers\ncomplete it, and errored receivers fail it. Interrupted, shutdown and not-found\nreceivers emit `lane.failed` only when their lane was previously started;\nunstarted receivers and missing attribution remain raw unknown evidence.\nLane payloads supply `label` and bounded `detail`; a completed collaboration\ntool does not prove receiver completion.\nThe checked-in `lanes.jsonl` deliberately mismatches these identities and\ntherefore supplies raw missing-attribution evidence, not successful lanes.\nAt most 4096 item/lane identities are remembered; eviction prefers the oldest\nterminal identity, then the oldest identity, and emits a visible raw gap.\n\n`summarize` consumes its supplied iterable once. If native terminal evidence\nexists, it returns the shared reduction summary unchanged. Otherwise the latest\ntop-level provisional error's normalized metadata supplies a synthetic\n`session.failed` to reduction, plus `rate_limit.started` when applicable. This\ndoes not mutate workflow state or parse native prose in shared code. A recovered\nerror followed by completion adds no synthetic failure or rate limit. With no\nterminal or provisional error, the shared reducer remains authoritative;\nzero process exit alone does not prove success. Conflicting thread identities\nremain represented and reduce to unknown failure rather than merged success.\nEach valid thread header emits exactly one `session.started`. A conflicting\nheader resets usage and queues a raw gap for the next `turn.completed`, using\nthat completion record's digest and sanitized preview.\n\n### Usage and unknown telemetry\n\nNormalized input excludes separately counted cache reads/writes, matching the\nClaude adapter. Native input includes cached input. With valid inclusive input,\ncache read and an explicit zero cache write, normalized input is inclusive\ninput minus cache read. Native `120 input / 100 read / 0 write / 30 output`\ntherefore becomes `20 input / 100 read / 0 creation / 30 output`.\nReads exceeding inclusive input produce unknown uncached input and a raw gap.\nPositive cache writes expose their native value as cache creation and retain\nvalid cache reads, but leave uncached input unknown with a partition gap and\n`estimated=True`: the pinned schema does not establish the write partition.\nMissing/invalid partition components also leave uncached input unknown.\n\nCounters are validated individually as nonnegative integers, rejecting bools,\nfloats, nonfinite and wrong-shaped values. Valid components survive malformed\nneighbors; omitted components remain unknown and malformed supplied usage emits\na raw gap. Reasoning tokens are not added to output again. `cost_usd` and\n`tool_uses` remain `None`, without inferred prices or invented zeroes. Inclusive\nnative totals survive in private raw evidence only.\n\nThe pinned producer's `usage_from_last_total` reads thread-cumulative totals.\nCodex takes fieldwise maxima across validated normalized snapshots for that\nthread, never sums terminal snapshots: native input snapshots 100 then 200\nyield 200, not 300. Replays, out-of-order completions and additional turns do\nnot change that arithmetic or require a turn ledger. Usage identity is\n`thread:<id-or-unknown>` and is observational only. Known counters are exact\nunless a partition gap marks them estimated; unknown fields remain `None` and\ngaps remain visible. Default all-zero native telemetry yields unknown fields\nand an absence gap; a later zero snapshot cannot reduce earlier known maxima.\n\n### Native phase prompt and projection\n\n`default_prompt` accepts exactly `build`, `review` and `done`, rejecting other\nphases with `ValueError`. It returns this literal native invocation and contract:\n\n```text\n$run-autopilot\nRead the committed OVCAQ state to determine the current phase. Use `ovcaq state`\nand `ovcaq workflow` commands for all durable mutations. Perform only the\ncurrent phase, commit exactly one successful phase handoff through the OVCAQ\nworkflow command, then exit the session. Never edit state.json directly. Do not\nstart a second phase in this session. Completion is determined by committed\nstate, not your final message. Do not install, upgrade, or modify launcher\nplugins or marketplace content.\n```\n\nThe external skill must expose the native name `run-autopilot`. A different\ninstalled name requires an explicit verified phase prompt override.\n`AgentSettings.prompts` are literal supervisor overrides subject to the same\nhandoff contract; the driver does not embed skill files or open plugin caches.\nState transitions determine successful handoff and phase completion. One\nlaunched session performs one phase and exits after one successful committed\nhandoff, with no second-phase work in that session.\n\n`ProjectObserver` and panels consume normalized observations and metrics to\nshow provider identity, attributed lanes and exact/estimated usage. Normalized\ntool lifecycles and text, thinking, error and plan activity render alongside\nlanes. Tool names persist through terminal events that omit them. Activity is\nrestricted to the selected run and session, retains at most 256 identities,\nand displays bounded literal details without interpreting markup.\nUnavailable cost is unknown or omitted. Unsupported fields and\nnormalization gaps retain raw-only visibility; out-of-order lane progress can\nremain unknown.\nNative receiver IDs do not establish OVCAQ task correlation. The TUI adds no\nprovider parser, model names or lifecycle policy.\n\n## Evidence and permissions\n\nSession output is raw-first: OVCAQ writes and flushes each raw record before\nframing or decoding it. Normalized JSONL contains only validated, sanitized\nobservations. The private raw file preserves the provider-native evidence needed\nfor recovery and debugging after a host crash, including records that could not\nbe decoded.\n\nRaw output can contain provider-native content or credentials. Its file mode is\n`0600`; access must remain restricted to the operator account. Sanitization of\nnormalized data does not make the raw evidence safe to share.\n\nClaude v1 accepts only `permission_mode=\"auto\"`. A missing or unsafe permission\nmode is a configuration failure. A runtime failure classified as `permission`\nis an operator alert, not an instruction for the workflow to change phase.\n\n## Operator runbook\n\nWhen a session fails or becomes unknown:\n\n1. Read the normalized `SessionSummary` and observations to identify the outcome\n   and `FailureClass`.\n2. If decoding is incomplete, inspect the session's private raw evidence as the\n   operator account. Keep it restricted because it may contain credentials.\n3. Treat `permission` failures as alerts. For Claude, confirm the configured\n   permission mode is exactly `auto`; do not weaken the mode to force progress.\n4. Correct the relevant configuration, credentials, connectivity, or extension\n   version. An API-version mismatch requires coordinated upgrades, not a shim.\n5. Retry through the applicable lifecycle command. Never edit `state.json`\n   directly, and never infer or choose a phase from an agent message or summary.\n\nThe public driver surface is `DRIVER_API_VERSION`, `AgentDriver`,\n`DriverDiscoveryError`, `DriverFactory`, `DriverHealth`, `DriverPlugin`,\n`DuplicateDriverError`, `IncompatibleDriverError`, `LaunchRequest`, `LaunchSpec`,\nand `discover_drivers`. The public event surface is `PROTOCOL_VERSION`,\n`FailureClass`, `Observation`, `ObservationType`, `Reduction`, `SessionSummary`,\n`Usage`, `observation_from_record`, `observation_record`, `reduce_session`,\n`sanitize_json`, `sanitize_text`, `session_failure_observation`, and\n`summary_record`.\n\n# Changelog\n\nAll notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.\n\nThe format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/).\n\n## [Unreleased]\n\n### Added\n\n- **drivers**: run supervised workflows with Codex, configured models and effort, verified version/authentication checks, and state-based phase handoffs.\n- **drivers**: show Codex activity, tools, receiver lanes and cache-aware usage with retained raw evidence; conflicting native threads fail safely even after supervisor ownership binding.\n- **tui**: add `ovcaq attach`, TTY-default attachment for `ovcaq run`, detachable interactive controls with plain fallback, and interactive `resume` that attaches to a live supervisor or launches once after a boundary pause.\n- **runtime**: run batch capabilities as durable provider-neutral attempts with private session evidence, final metrics, stale-owner reconciliation, and explicit one-attempt retry of failed capabilities.\n- **state**: add guarded compound supervisor mutations for pause/resume, progress fingerprints, capability attempts, failure attention, and empty-drain completion.\n- **runtime**: publish one immutable canonical metric row per phase or capability session, reconcile interrupted evidence deterministically, and project versioned metrics through legacy-compatible reports.\n- **config**: resolve provider-neutral build, review, and done routes with validated effort, timeout, model-tier, retry-policy, and literal prompt configuration; drivers now expose the version-two default-prompt contract.\n- **config**: load immutable per-agent argv, tier, environment, and JSON option settings with user/project merging and credential-shaped value rejection.\n- **drivers**: define the immutable provider-neutral launch, health, plugin, and discovery contracts with exact-version, duplicate-name, and fail-closed probe validation.\n- **drivers**: add the shell-free Claude launch adapter with explicit unattended permissions, configured tier and fallback resolution, isolated environment construction, executable health checks, and neutral summary reduction.\n- **drivers**: normalize Claude init, activity, tool, lane, usage, rate-limit, and terminal stream records with bounded replay-safe identity tracking and closed failure classification.\n- **events**: define the immutable version-one observation, usage, failure, summary, redaction, validation, and JSON record contract.\n- **events**: deterministically reduce normalized sessions, fold cumulative usage without double counting, preserve typed completion usage, and synthesize sanitized failure observations.\n- **events**: persist private normalized JSONL and decode bounded newline frames only after exact raw bytes are durably recorded, with observable sanitized fallbacks for oversized or failing frames.\n- **runtime**: resolve secure project, runtime, fleet, and evidence paths with validated limits and stable documented field order; probe required macOS process and durability capabilities; and publish, observe, prune, and conditionally remove identity-pinned fleet records behind inode-stable run locks without launch contention.\n- **runtime**: own shell-free process groups through persistent anchors, preserve descendant ownership and validated cleanup ancestry without mutating substituted paths, escalate timed-out groups from TERM to KILL through serialized bounded watchdogs, map supervisor signals to exit statuses, and durably remove matching ownership records while retaining evidence and lock files.\n- **runtime**: persist complete bounded raw session bytes; publish private metadata and summaries that reject unsafe identifiers, mismatches, non-UTC timestamps, and empty required fields; inventory retained evidence; and apply explicit active-safe purge plans with verified ancestry, locks, bounded malformed-row preservation, and durable deletion.\n- **runtime**: gate launches on durable emergency state-write evidence, preserve concurrent evidence, reject malformed or deeply nested records, revalidate evidence before acknowledgement, archive the exact acknowledged record, and restore the gate after failed acknowledgement.\n- **cli**: expose runtime status, actionable doctor resource checks, retained-log lookup/follow, non-applying purge previews, and explicit purge application; reject conflicting parent log options, omit internal path metadata, and reuse validated state for emergency acknowledgement.\n- Import-only package boundaries and a help-only `ovcaq` command.\n- Versioned v1 state schema/defaults and the exhaustive legacy-field crosswalk.\n- Closed v1 state validation with nested, resource, and cross-field invariants.\n- Locked state-store transactions with canonical publication, raw backups, and restore.\n- Monotonic revisions and locked compare-and-set conflict checks for transactions and restores.\n- Create-only initialization requires an existing runtime directory and repairs failed publications.\n- Pure JSON-path parsing and in-memory get/set/append/delete recovery operations.\n- Atomic StateService task, attempt, metadata, count, and contract-card commands.\n- Parsed-token protection for generic state recovery, with explicit unsafe override.\n- P01-T10 state CLI dispatch, stable errors, root discovery, and parser-derived command documentation.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **drivers**: remove terminal controls before redacting and bounding Codex diagnostic details so split credentials cannot survive normalization.\n- **events**: retain native JSON frames containing invalid Unicode scalars as raw gaps before adapter state can change.\n- **events**: reject surrogate code points in shared observation and summary text before redaction or truncation while preserving valid Unicode scalars.\n- **cli**: reject unhealthy adapters before foreground agent execution using sanitized health diagnostics.\n- **tui**: show bounded normalized tool, message and plan activity alongside selected-session lanes with literal markup.\n\n- **tui**: surface selected-run observation read errors as gaps while preserving ownership checks and process-control exceptions.\n- **runtime**: keep standalone `run_once()` usable without full-loop ownership metadata while preserving supervisor identity across full-run sessions.\n- **runtime**: preserve unknown numeric usage in final metrics while retaining exact fields and strict validation of known values.\n- **tui**: fall back after ordinary interactive-view failures, detach plain viewers on real EOF, and print only changed frames while preserving terminal results.\n- **tui**: persist theme changes from both views, honor trusted XDG aliases, and use private no-follow theme I/O with replacement checks that tolerate concurrent readers.\n- **tui**: sanitize both fleet selectors and show stale/malformed registry counts and empty-fleet status without selecting a run.\n- **launcher**: recognize verified fast completion and attachment cleanup races, preserve bounded private startup diagnostics, honor configured runtime roots, and guide duplicate-owner failures to attach even while the new child is still starting.\n- **cli**: require explicit foreground mode for JSON runs and load viewer dependencies only for interactive commands.\n- **tui**: guard selected-run pause/resume through durable publication, reuse those controls for interactive resume, and honor the injected fleet registry for stop.\n- **runtime**: provide shared ownership exclusion for guarded state mutations, preserving state and backup bytes when a selected owner becomes stale during publication.\n- **tui**: keep controls responsive during observation reads and show elapsed selected-run duration instead of summed session times; terminal duration uses durable end evidence so late polls cannot inflate it.\n- **runtime**: bind terminal observations to the exact supervisor and revalidate evidence reads without competing for the launch lock or discarding ordinary active state progress.\n- **tui**: display normalized lane descriptions when an explicit lane label is absent.\n- **events**: bind normalized observations to the owning supervisor run and session while retaining exact native raw evidence.\n- **tui**: retain selected final state and telemetry after supervisor cleanup, reject replacement evidence, and render terminal outcomes before viewers exit.\n- **tui**: follow active session telemetry across rollover, retain stream damage diagnostics, preserve live usage and numeric costs, show the latest capability attempt's evidence path, and distinguish raw-only content without changing evidence presence flags.\n- **paths**: align workflow, runtime, report, launcher, and test defaults on the\n  `docs/dev/project-management` store tree.\n- **runtime**: keep supervisor session collection within style limits while preserving timeout evidence and classification.\n- **runtime**: honor supplied phase settings on every workflow launch while preserving agent overrides and literal prompts.\n- **workflow**: include canonical phase metrics in reports when usage is missing or malformed while excluding capability attempts from workflow totals.\n- **runtime**: share capability execution and failure evidence across pending runs and retries, honor bounded capability timeout precedence, and defer aggregate publication until completion.\n- **runtime**: persist operator stops until resume and halt state failures behind sanitized durable attention or an emergency restart barrier, preserving existing attention notices.\n- **runtime**: bound unchanged attempts across failure classes while preserving terminal halts, honor committed controls before capabilities and drain completion, and preserve phase-pause reasons on resume.\n- **runtime**: initialize batch identity before sessions, preserve capability failure and retry attention, and archive completed batches using the supervisor clock.\n\n- **events**: normalized evidence redacts `x-api-key`, `apiKey`, and other separator or case variants of API-key names, and raw fallback previews redact before truncating so no secret prefix survives the cut.\n- **events**: redaction stays linear on long unbroken text, and raw fallback previews read a bounded head of the frame, so one bad frame can no longer stall decoding or expose a secret cut at the frame limit.\n- **events**: credential assignments behind sensitive key names longer than 64 characters are redacted again.\n- **events**: raw fallback previews of a cut-off frame with no whitespace come out empty whenever an `sk-` token is in view, and short CRLF frames no longer carry the trailing CR.\n- **drivers**: a dated limit banner whose date does not exist in its year (such as Feb 29 in a non-leap year) yields no reset instead of jumping to a later leap year.\n- **drivers**: rate-limit warnings no longer clear a live limit, a rejected event's reset wins over the terminal banner, real clock-and-zone banners such as `resets 8:10pm (Europe/Prague)` yield a reset, and the limit window is structured payload data.\n- **drivers**: dated limit banners such as `resets Sep 20 at 11am (Europe/Prague)` yield a multi-day reset, and a terminal banner reset now applies after a rejected event that carried no credible future reset.\n- Agent configuration applies command defaults and shared validation; normalized text redacts bare credential tokens while preserving usage counters.\n- Deferred directory setup diagnostics report the full configured path while preserving the originating OS error.\n- Deferred publication revalidates the complete confirmed runtime ancestry before returning success.\n- Deferred ancestry confirmation detects replaced ancestors on reopen, supports search-only access, and avoids redundant ancestor creation and root confirmation.\n- Shared publication contention reports `publication busy; retry: <path>` for both reports and deferred ledgers.\n- Deferred retries and replays confirm existing runtime ancestry after abrupt exits and preserve directories created by concurrent callers when confirmation fails.\n- Deferred publication validates the complete prospective ledger and persists newly created runtime ancestors; overlapping report renders no longer lose successful sections.\n- Report JSON returns the complete published batch text after PRD updates and exact rerenders.\n- Deferred ledgers reject duplicate keys and excessive nesting; replaced sidecar locks refuse publication without losing competing appends.\n- Park/stall recovery retains the original PRD's filesystem identity through restart and preserves unfinished work on substitution.\n- Batch reports reconstruct all deferred details with PRD attribution; report, audit, and archive retries complete directory durability.\n- Blank decision content is omitted consistently from counts and audits; named payload and configuration files reject nonregular inputs promptly.\n- PRD moves atomically refuse concurrent held work; filesystem evidence remains checked through saga state publication.\n- Recovery validates batch identity and operation inputs before creating lifecycle directories or durable intent.\n- Installed workflow commands honor configured roots and active abstract tier keys, with explicit path and tier overrides.\n- Metric inputs reject FIFO/nonregular files promptly; systemic park pauses return a distinct halt status and actionable output.\n- Workflow orchestration, render sections, and installed/saga tests are split by responsibility while retaining failpoints and acceptance coverage.\n\n- Drained workflows remain terminal, and stall refuses conflicting recovery intents while park retains reconciliation behavior.\n- Workflow reports preserve unknown legacy totals, order equal-key metric rows deterministically, and reject nonregular output targets without blocking.\n- Deferred ledger publication and saga commit now verify the configured directory identity through durable writes and recovery.\n- State validation now uses canonical persisted-byte sizing, precise nested diagnostics, and cached type metadata.\n- CLI task payloads now reject non-object JSON, option abbreviations are disabled at every parser level, and resource limits use typed errors.\n- State transactions now overwrite mutator-supplied revisions before validation and close lock descriptors on every setup failure.\n- The installed state service now owns restore routing, and the protocol documents the complete schema, durability, and error contracts.\n- Project initialization now preserves existing config metadata and remains retryable when config creation fails.\n\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":1916}},"started_at_ms":1791622524833,"completed_at_ms":1791622524833}}
+{"timestamp":"2026-10-10T08:55:24.835Z","ordinal":22,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a12506-2fa3-7f11-873e-e2aa6ae7f56f","call_id":"call_eef395039c584648b668b3498feb0b3a","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"014eb4\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000001916,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":8359,\"output\":\"# Driver and observation protocol\\n\\nOVCAQ version 1 separates provider-specific drivers from provider-neutral\\nobservations. The public contracts described here are the implemented exports of\\n`ovcaq.drivers` and `ovcaq.events`; this document is not a command inventory.\\n\\n## Driver contract\\n\\n`AgentDriver` implementations provide:\\n\\n- `name`, the advertised driver name;\\n- `default_prompt(phase)`, the driver's native phase prompt;\\n- `probe()`, which reports `DriverHealth`;\\n- `build_launch(request)`, which converts a `LaunchRequest` into a `LaunchSpec`;\\n- `decode(raw_line)`, which converts one raw line into zero or more\\n  `Observation` values; and\\n- `summarize(observations, exit_code)`, which returns a `SessionSummary`.\\n\\n`LaunchSpec` contains an argv tuple, an environment overlay, a `Path` working\\ndirectory, and the raw-output format. The later supervisor deliberately merges\\nthat overlay into its prepared host environment. OVCAQ executes argv directly;\\nconfigured commands are never shell-evaluated.\\n\\nExtensions expose a `DriverPlugin(api_version, factory)`, where `factory`\\nimplements `DriverFactory`. `discover_drivers` combines built-in drivers with\\nplugins from the `ovcaq.drivers` entry-point group. Discovery validates the\\nadvertised name, rejects duplicate names (`DuplicateDriverError`), rejects\\nconstruction or health failures (`DriverDiscoveryError`), and requires the\\nplugin API version to equal `DRIVER_API_VERSION`, currently `2`. A different\\nversion is rejected with `IncompatibleDriverError`; version negotiation is not\\nperformed.\\n\\nThe CLI foreground launch path calls the selected driver's `probe()` before\\nrunning the supervisor. Unhealthy version or authentication evidence rejects\\nthe launch with a bounded, sanitized runtime error before agent execution.\\nDirectly constructed supervisors do not probe their injected drivers.\\n\\n`DriverFactory` is a zero-argument callable, but a registration may use a\\nclosure to capture configuration and the per-run identifier, for\\nexample `lambda: ClaudeDriver(settings, run_id=run_id)`, where the caller\\nsupplies `run_id`. A decoder has mutable session state, and the supervisor\\nreuses its driver between sessions. `build_launch` must reset session-local\\ndecoder state while retaining the supplied run identifier. Codex resets its\\nthread identity, cumulative usage and remembered item/lane identities. A new\\nsession must not inherit earlier usage or failure evidence; summarization uses\\nthe supplied observations, not stale private decoder fields.\\n\\nBefore OVCAQ 1.0, driver ABI and observation-protocol changes may require\\ncoordinated upgrades of core and extensions. Compatibility is deliberately\\nstrict: there are no compatibility shims or dual writers.\\n\\n## Observation contract\\n\\n`PROTOCOL_VERSION` is exactly `1`. An `Observation` has a validated UTC\\ntimestamp and identifiers, an `ObservationType`, and a deeply immutable\\npayload. Version 1 observation types are:\\n\\n- `session.started`, `session.completed`, and `session.failed`;\\n- `activity.message`;\\n- `tool.started`, `tool.completed`, and `tool.failed`;\\n- `lane.started`, `lane.progress`, `lane.completed`, and `lane.failed`;\\n- `usage.updated`;\\n- `rate_limit.started` and `rate_limit.cleared`; and\\n- `raw.unknown`.\\n\\nDuring supervised decoding, envelope `run_id` and `session_id` identify the\\nowning OVCAQ run and session. Before rebinding those IDs, the raw-first decoder\\nretains each startup observation's original session ID in its sanitized\\n`source_session_id` payload field. Reduction compares that normalized source\\nidentity independently of envelope ownership: distinct startup identities\\nproduce an `unknown` failure even with terminal completion or a nonzero exit;\\nrepeated identical startup identities do not. Raw and normalized evidence are\\nretained. This uses observation protocol 1 and driver API 2 unchanged.\\n\\nA `rate_limit.started` payload has exactly `reset_at` (an RFC 3339 UTC string\\nor null), `window` (a text limit-window label or null), and `detail` (text).\\nA provider `allowed_warning` arrives as `activity.message` with payload `kind`\\n`rate_limit_warning` plus `window`, `reset_at`, and `detail`, and never as\\n`rate_limit.cleared`. A terminal banner reset applies unless a rejected event\\nwith a credible future reset is still live (not cleared by a later `allowed`).\\nA later rejected event without a credible reset ends that live limit, so a\\nbanner after `rejected(R) -> rejected(no reset)` applies its own reset. A dated\\nbanner picks its year first (the anchor's year, or the next one once that date\\nhas passed) and resolves to no reset when the date is invalid in that year.\\n\\n`FailureClass` values are `rate_limit`, `connectivity`, `permission`,\\n`authentication`, `timeout`, `agent`, and `unknown`. `Usage` represents usage\\nreported by observations. `session_failure_observation` constructs the public\\nfailure observation form.\\n\\nUse `observation_record` and `observation_from_record` for the serialized\\nobservation representation. `sanitize_text` and `sanitize_json` redact\\ncredential-shaped keys and credential assignments. Unknown previews are\\nbounded. A preview of a frame longer than its 512-byte window is cut back to\\nthe last whitespace; a window with no whitespace previews as empty when it\\ncontains `sk-` (case-insensitive) and is kept otherwise. A frame's trailing CR\\nnever reaches the preview. Malformed, oversized, unknown, or decoder-failing\\ninput is handled totally: it becomes a `raw.unknown` observation with a bounded\\nsanitized preview and SHA-256 evidence instead of crashing the supervisor.\\n\\n`reduce_session` retains the observations and deterministically produces a\\n`Reduction` containing a `SessionSummary`. `summary_record` serializes that\\nsummary. Summaries report outcome, failure, reset, and usage information; they\\nnever select workflow phases. Provider-native data never enters workflow state.\\nLifecycle decisions use committed state and normalized results, not prose or a\\nsummary choosing the next phase.\\n\\nEvery `usage.updated` observation is a cumulative snapshot for its session,\\nnever a per-turn delta. Message-derived totals are estimated; a terminal result\\nsnapshot is exact and contains only fields reported by the provider. Reduction\\nmerges each numeric field independently by maximum, including cost, so duplicate\\nand out-of-order snapshots cannot reduce or double-count totals. An exact\\nsnapshot clears `estimated` only for winning values it exactly replaces; the\\naggregate remains estimated while any fieldwise maximum has no exact\\nreplacement.\\n\\n## Codex exec contract\\n\\nThe supported evidence pin is **codex-cli 0.160.0**, with raw format\\n`codex-exec-json-v1`. The [versioned provenance](../tests/fixtures/codex/v1/provenance.json)\\ndistinguishes captured version/help stdout from schema-synthetic JSONL. Captured\\nhelp replaces the generic launcher-home config directory with `[LAUNCHER_HOME]`;\\nprovenance records this artifact-safety substitution. Its\\nofficial sources are the pinned [exec event schema](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/rust-v0.160.0/codex-rs/exec/src/exec_events.rs)\\nand [JSONL producer](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/rust-v0.160.0/codex-rs/exec/src/event_processor_with_jsonl_output.rs).\\nThe fixtures are not live workflow or authentication transcripts, and fake\\nphase execution is not an external-plugin smoke test. Future native records\\nmay remain raw-only rather than silently extending this contract.\\n\\n### Launch and configuration\\n\\n`CodexDriver(settings: AgentSettings, *, run_id: str)` accepts only `sandbox`,\\n`approval` and `allow_explicit_model` options. Sandbox and approval default to\\n`workspace-write` and `never`; other values or wrong types fail with\\n`ValueError`. `allow_explicit_model` defaults to `False` and must be a bool.\\nUnknown options fail. Configuration stores the command as an argv tuple,\\nincluding any wrapper prefix arguments.\\n\\nThe launch order is exactly:\\n\\n```python\\nargv = (\\n    *settings.command,\\n    \\\"--ask-for-approval\\\",\\n    \\\"never\\\",\\n    \\\"exec\\\",\\n    \\\"--json\\\",\\n    \\\"--sandbox\\\",\\n    \\\"workspace-write\\\",\\n    \\\"-C\\\",\\n    str(request.root),\\n    # Optional model segment:\\n    \\\"-m\\\",\\n    resolved_model,\\n    # Optional mapped effort segment (high example):\\n    \\\"-c\\\",\\n    'model_reasoning_effort=\\\"high\\\"',\\n    \\\"--\\\",\\n    request.prompt,\\n)\\n```\\n\\nAn absent model omits its segment. A configured tier resolves through\\n`settings.tiers`; an already resolved tier value is accepted unchanged. An\\narbitrary explicit model requires `allow_explicit_model=True`. Shared modules\\nprovide no provider model default. Absent effort omits its segment. `low`,\\n`medium`, `high` and `xhigh` map identically to `model_reasoning_effort` with a\\nquoted TOML value. Blank effort fails; another nonblank value logs one warning\\ncontaining the requested value and omits the override without guessing.\\n\\n`LaunchSpec.cwd` is `request.root`; its environment is a copy of the configured\\noverlay, without provider-specific host-variable or credential copies. The\\nlauncher manages inherited authenticated storage. `build_launch` neither\\nspawns nor provisions. The shared runner closes stdin and spawns without a\\nshell; wrapper arguments and prompt shell syntax remain literal data. The\\nfinal `--` also protects prompts beginning with `-`. Runtime enforces the\\nrequest timeout without another CLI flag.\\n\\n### Decoding and terminal evidence\\n\\nCodex observations use protocol 1, provider `codex`, the supplied run ID and\\ncurrent thread as session ID before supervised ownership rebinding. The native\\nthread ID then survives in startup `source_session_id` payloads. Pinned native\\nrecords have no timestamp, so the adapter uses aware UTC receipt time.\\nValidated observations contain normalized,\\nsanitized payloads. Malformed JSON (including recursion errors), wrong shapes,\\nunsupported item kinds/statuses and future records fall back to `raw.unknown`\\nwith an original-line SHA-256, sanitized preview of at most 512 characters and\\na bounded reason. Raw evidence remains available even when projection fails.\\n\\n| Native evidence | Normalized projection |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| `thread.started` | `session.started` with thread identity |\\n| `turn.started` | `activity.message`, kind `turn` |\\n| Agent/reasoning items | `activity.message`, kind `text`/`thinking`, bounded text and item ID |\\n| Command/file/MCP/web items | Tool lifecycle with item ID as `tool_id`, neutral name and bounded detail |\\n| Todo items | `activity.message`, kind `plan`, bounded steps |\\n| Collaboration items | Tool lifecycle and receiver-attributed lane evidence when available |\\n| Nonfatal error item | `activity.message`, kind `error`; no session failure |\\n| `turn.completed` | `usage.updated` and `session.completed` |\\n| `turn.failed` | `session.failed`, plus `rate_limit.started` when classified |\\n| Top-level `error` | Provisional error activity with normalized failure metadata |\\n\\nThe adapter recognizes `item.started`, `item.updated` and `item.completed`.\\nTool failure requires native failure/decline status, explicit native error or\\nnonzero command exit. Ordinary command output is not provider-health evidence.\\nFile detail summarizes paths and change kinds; MCP and web detail is sanitized\\nand bounded to 4096 characters, rather than retained as wholesale results.\\n\\nCollaboration requires `agents_states` keys matching native receiver thread\\nIDs. Pending/running receivers start or progress a lane, completed receivers\\ncomplete it, and errored receivers fail it. Interrupted, shutdown and not-found\\nreceivers emit `lane.failed` only when their lane was previously started;\\nunstarted receivers and missing attribution remain raw unknown evidence.\\nLane payloads supply `label` and bounded `detail`; a completed collaboration\\ntool does not prove receiver completion.\\nThe checked-in `lanes.jsonl` deliberately mismatches these identities and\\ntherefore supplies raw missing-attribution evidence, not successful lanes.\\nAt most 4096 item/lane identities are remembered; eviction prefers the oldest\\nterminal identity, then the oldest identity, and emits a visible raw gap.\\n\\n`summarize` consumes its supplied iterable once. If native terminal evidence\\nexists, it returns the shared reduction summary unchanged. Otherwise the latest\\ntop-level provisional error's normalized metadata supplies a synthetic\\n`session.failed` to reduction, plus `rate_limit.started` when applicable. This\\ndoes not mutate workflow state or parse native prose in shared code. A recovered\\nerror followed by completion adds no synthetic failure or rate limit. With no\\nterminal or provisional error, the shared reducer remains authoritative;\\nzero process exit alone does not prove success. Conflicting thread identities\\nremain represented and reduce to unknown failure rather than merged success.\\nEach valid thread header emits exactly one `session.started`. A conflicting\\nheader resets usage and queues a raw gap for the next `turn.completed`, using\\nthat completion record's digest and sanitized preview.\\n\\n### Usage and unknown telemetry\\n\\nNormalized input excludes separately counted cache reads/writes, matching the\\nClaude adapter. Native input includes cached input. With valid inclusive input,\\ncache read and an explicit zero cache write, normalized input is inclusive\\ninput minus cache read. Native `120 input / 100 read / 0 write / 30 output`\\ntherefore becomes `20 input / 100 read / 0 creation / 30 output`.\\nReads exceeding inclusive input produce unknown uncached input and a raw gap.\\nPositive cache writes expose their native value as cache creation and retain\\nvalid cache reads, but leave uncached input unknown with a partition gap and\\n`estimated=True`: the pinned schema does not establish the write partition.\\nMissing/invalid partition components also leave uncached input unknown.\\n\\nCounters are validated individually as nonnegative integers, rejecting bools,\\nfloats, nonfinite and wrong-shaped values. Valid components survive malformed\\nneighbors; omitted components remain unknown and malformed supplied usage emits\\na raw gap. Reasoning tokens are not added to output again. `cost_usd` and\\n`tool_uses` remain `None`, without inferred prices or invented zeroes. Inclusive\\nnative totals survive in private raw evidence only.\\n\\nThe pinned producer's `usage_from_last_total` reads thread-cumulative totals.\\nCodex takes fieldwise maxima across validated normalized snapshots for that\\nthread, never sums terminal snapshots: native input snapshots 100 then 200\\nyield 200, not 300. Replays, out-of-order completions and additional turns do\\nnot change that arithmetic or require a turn ledger. Usage identity is\\n`thread:<id-or-unknown>` and is observational only. Known counters are exact\\nunless a partition gap marks them estimated; unknown fields remain `None` and\\ngaps remain visible. Default all-zero native telemetry yields unknown fields\\nand an absence gap; a later zero snapshot cannot reduce earlier known maxima.\\n\\n### Native phase prompt and projection\\n\\n`default_prompt` accepts exactly `build`, `review` and `done`, rejecting other\\nphases with `ValueError`. It returns this literal native invocation and contract:\\n\\n```text\\n$run-autopilot\\nRead the committed OVCAQ state to determine the current phase. Use `ovcaq state`\\nand `ovcaq workflow` commands for all durable mutations. Perform only the\\ncurrent phase, commit exactly one successful phase handoff through the OVCAQ\\nworkflow command, then exit the session. Never edit state.json directly. Do not\\nstart a second phase in this session. Completion is determined by committed\\nstate, not your final message. Do not install, upgrade, or modify launcher\\nplugins or marketplace content.\\n```\\n\\nThe external skill must expose the native name `run-autopilot`. A different\\ninstalled name requires an explicit verified phase prompt override.\\n`AgentSettings.prompts` are literal supervisor overrides subject to the same\\nhandoff contract; the driver does not embed skill files or open plugin caches.\\nState transitions determine successful handoff and phase completion. One\\nlaunched session performs one phase and exits after one successful committed\\nhandoff, with no second-phase work in that session.\\n\\n`ProjectObserver` and panels consume normalized observations and metrics to\\nshow provider identity, attributed lanes and exact/estimated usage. Normalized\\ntool lifecycles and text, thinking, error and plan activity render alongside\\nlanes. Tool names persist through terminal events that omit them. Activity is\\nrestricted to the selected run and session, retains at most 256 identities,\\nand displays bounded literal details without interpreting markup.\\nUnavailable cost is unknown or omitted. Unsupported fields and\\nnormalization gaps retain raw-only visibility; out-of-order lane progress can\\nremain unknown.\\nNative receiver IDs do not establish OVCAQ task correlation. The TUI adds no\\nprovider parser, model names or lifecycle policy.\\n\\n## Evidence and permissions\\n\\nSession output is raw-first: OVCAQ writes and flushes each raw record before\\nframing or decoding it. Normalized JSONL contains only validated, sanitized\\nobservations. The private raw file preserves the provider-native evidence needed\\nfor recovery and debugging after a host crash, including records that could not\\nbe decoded.\\n\\nRaw output can contain provider-native content or credentials. Its file mode is\\n`0600`; access must remain restricted to the operator account. Sanitization of\\nnormalized data does not make the raw evidence safe to share.\\n\\nClaude v1 accepts only `permission_mode=\\\"auto\\\"`. A missing or unsafe permission\\nmode is a configuration failure. A runtime failure classified as `permission`\\nis an operator alert, not an instruction for the workflow to change phase.\\n\\n## Operator runbook\\n\\nWhen a session fails or becomes unknown:\\n\\n1. Read the normalized `SessionSummary` and observations to identify the outcome\\n   and `FailureClass`.\\n2. If decoding is incomplete, inspect the session's private raw evidence as the\\n   operator account. Keep it restricted because it may contain credentials.\\n3. Treat `permission` failures as alerts. For Claude, confirm the configured\\n   permission mode is exactly `auto`; do not weaken the mode to force progress.\\n4. Correct the relevant configuration, credentials, connectivity, or extension\\n   version. An API-version mismatch requires coordinated upgrades, not a shim.\\n5. Retry through the applicable lifecycle command. Never edit `state.json`\\n   directly, and never infer or choose a phase from an agent message or summary.\\n\\nThe public driver surface is `DRIVER_API_VERSION`, `AgentDriver`,\\n`DriverDiscoveryError`, `DriverFactory`, `DriverHealth`, `DriverPlugin`,\\n`DuplicateDriverError`, `IncompatibleDriverError`, `LaunchRequest`, `LaunchSpec`,\\nand `discover_drivers`. The public event surface is `PROTOCOL_VERSION`,\\n`FailureClass`, `Observation`, `ObservationType`, `Reduction`, `SessionSummary`,\\n`Usage`, `observation_from_record`, `observation_record`, `reduce_session`,\\n`sanitize_json`, `sanitize_text`, `session_failure_observation`, and\\n`summary_record`.\\n\\n# Changelog\\n\\nAll notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.\\n\\nThe format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/).\\n\\n## [Unreleased]\\n\\n### Added\\n\\n- **drivers**: run supervised workflows with Codex, configured models and effort, verified version/authentication checks, and state-based phase handoffs.\\n- **drivers**: show Codex activity, tools, receiver lanes and cache-aware usage with retained raw evidence; conflicting native threads fail safely even after supervisor ownership binding.\\n- **tui**: add `ovcaq attach`, TTY-default attachment for `ovcaq run`, detachable interactive controls with plain fallback, and interactive `resume` that attaches to a live supervisor or launches once after a boundary pause.\\n- **runtime**: run batch capabilities as durable provider-neutral attempts with private session evidence, final metrics, stale-owner reconciliation, and explicit one-attempt retry of failed capabilities.\\n- **state**: add guarded compound supervisor mutations for pause/resume, progress fingerprints, capability attempts, failure attention, and empty-drain completion.\\n- **runtime**: publish one immutable canonical metric row per phase or capability session, reconcile interrupted evidence deterministically, and project versioned metrics through legacy-compatible reports.\\n- **config**: resolve provider-neutral build, review, and done routes with validated effort, timeout, model-tier, retry-policy, and literal prompt configuration; drivers now expose the version-two default-prompt contract.\\n- **config**: load immutable per-agent argv, tier, environment, and JSON option settings with user/project merging and credential-shaped value rejection.\\n- **drivers**: define the immutable provider-neutral launch, health, plugin, and discovery contracts with exact-version, duplicate-name, and fail-closed probe validation.\\n- **drivers**: add the shell-free Claude launch adapter with explicit unattended permissions, configured tier and fallback resolution, isolated environment construction, executable health checks, and neutral summary reduction.\\n- **drivers**: normalize Claude init, activity, tool, lane, usage, rate-limit, and terminal stream records with bounded replay-safe identity tracking and closed failure classification.\\n- **events**: define the immutable version-one observation, usage, failure, summary, redaction, validation, and JSON record contract.\\n- **events**: deterministically reduce normalized sessions, fold cumulative usage without double counting, preserve typed completion usage, and synthesize sanitized failure observations.\\n- **events**: persist private normalized JSONL and decode bounded newline frames only after exact raw bytes are durably recorded, with observable sanitized fallbacks for oversized or failing frames.\\n- **runtime**: resolve secure project, runtime, fleet, and evidence paths with validated limits and stable documented field order; probe required macOS process and durability capabilities; and publish, observe, prune, and conditionally remove identity-pinned fleet records behind inode-stable run locks without launch contention.\\n- **runtime**: own shell-free process groups through persistent anchors, preserve descendant ownership and validated cleanup ancestry without mutating substituted paths, escalate timed-out groups from TERM to KILL through serialized bounded watchdogs, map supervisor signals to exit statuses, and durably remove matching ownership records while retaining evidence and lock files.\\n- **runtime**: persist complete bounded raw session bytes; publish private metadata and summaries that reject unsafe identifiers, mismatches, non-UTC timestamps, and empty required fields; inventory retained evidence; and apply explicit active-safe purge plans with verified ancestry, locks, bounded malformed-row preservation, and durable deletion.\\n- **runtime**: gate launches on durable emergency state-write evidence, preserve concurrent evidence, reject malformed or deeply nested records, revalidate evidence before acknowledgement, archive the exact acknowledged record, and restore the gate after failed acknowledgement.\\n- **cli**: expose runtime status, actionable doctor resource checks, retained-log lookup/follow, non-applying purge previews, and explicit purge application; reject conflicting parent log options, omit internal path metadata, and reuse validated state for emergency acknowledgement.\\n- Import-only package boundaries and a help-only `ovcaq` command.\\n- Versioned v1 state schema/defaults and the exhaustive legacy-field crosswalk.\\n- Closed v1 state validation with nested, resource, and cross-field invariants.\\n- Locked state-store transactions with canonical publication, raw backups, and restore.\\n- Monotonic revisions and locked compare-and-set conflict checks for transactions and restores.\\n- Create-only initialization requires an existing runtime directory and repairs failed publications.\\n- Pure JSON-path parsing and in-memory get/set/append/delete recovery operations.\\n- Atomic StateService task, attempt, metadata, count, and contract-card commands.\\n- Parsed-token protection for generic state recovery, with explicit unsafe override.\\n- P01-T10 state CLI dispatch, stable errors, root discovery, and parser-derived command documentation.\\n\\n### Fixed\\n\\n- **drivers**: remove terminal controls before redacting and bounding Codex diagnostic details so split credentials cannot survive normalization.\\n- **events**: retain native JSON frames containing invalid Unicode scalars as raw gaps before adapter state can change.\\n- **events**: reject surrogate code points in shared observation and summary text before redaction or truncation while preserving valid Unicode scalars.\\n- **cli**: reject unhealthy adapters before foreground agent execution using sanitized health diagnostics.\\n- **tui**: show bounded normalized tool, message and plan activity alongside selected-session lanes with literal markup.\\n\\n- **tui**: surface selected-run observation read errors as gaps while preserving ownership checks and process-control exceptions.\\n- **runtime**: keep standalone `run_once()` usable without full-loop ownership metadata while preserving supervisor identity across full-run sessions.\\n- **runtime**: preserve unknown numeric usage in final metrics while retaining exact fields and strict validation of known values.\\n- **tui**: fall back after ordinary interactive-view failures, detach plain viewers on real EOF, and print only changed frames while preserving terminal results.\\n- **tui**: persist theme changes from both views, honor trusted XDG aliases, and use private no-follow theme I/O with replacement checks that tolerate concurrent readers.\\n- **tui**: sanitize both fleet selectors and show stale/malformed registry counts and empty-fleet status without selecting a run.\\n- **launcher**: recognize verified fast completion and attachment cleanup races, preserve bounded private startup diagnostics, honor configured runtime roots, and guide duplicate-owner failures to attach even while the new child is still starting.\\n- **cli**: require explicit foreground mode for JSON runs and load viewer dependencies only for interactive commands.\\n- **tui**: guard selected-run pause/resume through durable publication, reuse those controls for interactive resume, and honor the injected fleet registry for stop.\\n- **runtime**: provide shared ownership exclusion for guarded state mutations, preserving state and backup bytes when a selected owner becomes stale during publication.\\n- **tui**: keep controls responsive during observation reads and show elapsed selected-run duration instead of summed session times; terminal duration uses durable end evidence so late polls cannot inflate it.\\n- **runtime**: bind terminal observations to the exact supervisor and revalidate evidence reads without competing for the launch lock or discarding ordinary active state progress.\\n- **tui**: display normalized lane descriptions when an explicit lane label is absent.\\n- **events**: bind normalized observations to the owning supervisor run and session while retaining exact native raw evidence.\\n- **tui**: retain selected final state and telemetry after supervisor cleanup, reject replacement evidence, and render terminal outcomes before viewers exit.\\n- **tui**: follow active session telemetry across rollover, retain stream damage diagnostics, preserve live usage and numeric costs, show the latest capability attempt's evidence path, and distinguish raw-only content without changing evidence presence flags.\\n- **paths**: align workflow, runtime, report, launcher, and test defaults on the\\n  `docs/dev/project-management` store tree.\\n- **runtime**: keep supervisor session collection within style limits while preserving timeout evidence and classification.\\n- **runtime**: honor supplied phase settings on every workflow launch while preserving agent overrides and literal prompts.\\n- **workflow**: include canonical phase metrics in reports when usage is missing or malformed while excluding capability attempts from workflow totals.\\n- **runtime**: share capability execution and failure evidence across pending runs and retries, honor bounded capability timeout precedence, and defer aggregate publication until completion.\\n- **runtime**: persist operator stops until resume and halt state failures behind sanitized durable attention or an emergency restart barrier, preserving existing attention notices.\\n- **runtime**: bound unchanged attempts across failure classes while preserving terminal halts, honor committed controls before capabilities and drain completion, and preserve phase-pause reasons on resume.\\n- **runtime**: initialize batch identity before sessions, preserve capability failure and retry attention, and archive completed batches using the supervisor clock.\\n\\n- **events**: normalized evidence redacts `x-api-key`, `apiKey`, and other separator or case variants of API-key names, and raw fallback previews redact before truncating so no secret prefix survives the cut.\\n- **events**: redaction stays linear on long unbroken text, and raw fallback previews read a bounded head of the frame, so one bad frame can no longer stall decoding or expose a secret cut at the frame limit.\\n- **events**: credential assignments behind sensitive key names longer than 64 characters are redacted again.\\n- **events**: raw fallback previews of a cut-off frame with no whitespace come out empty whenever an `sk-` token is in view, and short CRLF frames no longer carry the trailing CR.\\n- **drivers**: a dated limit banner whose date does not exist in its year (such as Feb 29 in a non-leap year) yields no reset instead of jumping to a later leap year.\\n- **drivers**: rate-limit warnings no longer clear a live limit, a rejected event's reset wins over the terminal banner, real clock-and-zone banners such as `resets 8:10pm (Europe/Prague)` yield a reset, and the limit window is structured payload data.\\n- **drivers**: dated limit banners such as `resets Sep 20 at 11am (Europe/Prague)` yield a multi-day reset, and a terminal banner reset now applies after a rejected event that carried no credible future reset.\\n- Agent configuration applies command defaults and shared validation; normalized text redacts bare credential tokens while preserving usage counters.\\n- Deferred directory setup diagnostics report the full configured path while preserving the originating OS error.\\n- Deferred publication revalidates the complete confirmed runtime ancestry before returning success.\\n- Deferred ancestry confirmation detects replaced ancestors on reopen, supports search-only access, and avoids redundant ancestor creation and root confirmation.\\n- Shared publication contention reports `publication busy; retry: <path>` for both reports and deferred ledgers.\\n- Deferred retries and replays confirm existing runtime ancestry after abrupt exits and preserve directories created by concurrent callers when confirmation fails.\\n- Deferred publication validates the complete prospective ledger and persists newly created runtime ancestors; overlapping report renders no longer lose successful sections.\\n- Report JSON returns the complete published batch text after PRD updates and exact rerenders.\\n- Deferred ledgers reject duplicate keys and excessive nesting; replaced sidecar locks refuse publication without losing competing appends.\\n- Park/stall recovery retains the original PRD's filesystem identity through restart and preserves unfinished work on substitution.\\n- Batch reports reconstruct all deferred details with PRD attribution; report, audit, and archive retries complete directory durability.\\n- Blank decision content is omitted consistently from counts and audits; named payload and configuration files reject nonregular inputs promptly.\\n- PRD moves atomically refuse concurrent held work; filesystem evidence remains checked through saga state publication.\\n- Recovery validates batch identity and operation inputs before creating lifecycle directories or durable intent.\\n- Installed workflow commands honor configured roots and active abstract tier keys, with explicit path and tier overrides.\\n- Metric inputs reject FIFO/nonregular files promptly; systemic park pauses return a distinct halt status and actionable output.\\n- Workflow orchestration, render sections, and installed/saga tests are split by responsibility while retaining failpoints and acceptance coverage.\\n\\n- Drained workflows remain terminal, and stall refuses conflicting recovery intents while park retains reconciliation behavior.\\n- Workflow reports preserve unknown legacy totals, order equal-key metric rows deterministically, and reject nonregular output targets without blocking.\\n- Deferred ledger publication and saga commit now verify the configured directory identity through durable writes and recovery.\\n- State validation now uses canonical persisted-byte sizing, precise nested diagnostics, and cached type metadata.\\n- CLI task payloads now reject non-object JSON, option abbreviations are disabled at every parser level, and resource limits use typed errors.\\n- State transactions now overwrite mutator-supplied revisions before validation and close lock descriptors on every setup failure.\\n- The installed state service now owns restore routing, and the protocol documents the complete schema, durability, and error contracts.\\n- Project initialization now preserves existing config metadata and remains retryable when config creation fails.\\n\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a12506-01bd-7992-a4dc-77d940f87b6d","create_time":1791622524.835829}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
