+{"timestamp":"2026-10-10T09:04:02.431Z","ordinal":17,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1250d-f5f8-7a20-b271-ba0589ad2b81","turn_id":"01a1250d-f64e-70c3-83c6-d24ee9c2da9e","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-dce80f0c-a554-4ae2-9548-4dd8b2de7063","process_id":"31725","command":["/opt/homebrew/bin/bash","-lc","rg -n '^' /Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md AGENTS.md docs/dev/tmp/dispatch-ivan-3-final.txt"],"cwd":"file:///Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/ovcaq","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n '^' /Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md AGENTS.md docs/dev/tmp/dispatch-ivan-3-final.txt","query":"^","path":"AGENTS.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"AGENTS.md:1:# AGENTS.md — OVCAQ\nAGENTS.md:2:\nAGENTS.md:3:OVCAQ means **Orchestration, Visibility, and Control for Agent Queues**. It is\nAGENTS.md:4:an installable Python CLI/TUI that supervises a PRD-driven workflow through\nAGENTS.md:5:Claude Code, Codex, or another headless coding agent.\nAGENTS.md:6:\nAGENTS.md:7:The global `AGENTS.md` remains in force. This file adds project-specific rules.\nAGENTS.md:8:\nAGENTS.md:9:## Current state\nAGENTS.md:10:\nAGENTS.md:11:The repository is in planning/bootstrap. Until an implementation PRD replaces\nAGENTS.md:12:it, `docs/dev/project-management/plans/implementation-plan.md` is the architecture and sequencing\nAGENTS.md:13:authority. Do not create production modules opportunistically outside that\nAGENTS.md:14:sequence.\nAGENTS.md:15:\nAGENTS.md:16:## Product boundary\nAGENTS.md:17:\nAGENTS.md:18:OVCAQ core owns state, lifecycle commands, process supervision, agent adapters,\nAGENTS.md:19:normalized events, observability, the TUI, and launcher provisioning checks.\nAGENTS.md:20:\nAGENTS.md:21:OVCAQ core does **not** contain launcher-specific skills, agents, hooks, or\nAGENTS.md:22:plugins. Those live in external marketplaces. Core may carry declarative\nAGENTS.md:23:identifiers and version requirements needed to reconcile them.\nAGENTS.md:24:\nAGENTS.md:25:## Architecture invariants\nAGENTS.md:26:\nAGENTS.md:27:1. **One state writer.** Every durable `state.json` mutation goes through the\nAGENTS.md:28:   state store. Agents mutate state only through `ovcaq state` or a higher-level\nAGENTS.md:29:   OVCAQ command. Never instruct an agent to edit `state.json` directly.\nAGENTS.md:30:2. **State decides; prose does not.** Session completion, phase handoff, pause,\nAGENTS.md:31:   drain, retry, and failure decisions derive from committed state plus a\nAGENTS.md:32:   normalized session result. A final agent message is never authoritative.\nAGENTS.md:33:3. **Provider code stays at the edge.** Claude/Codex argv, environment variables,\nAGENTS.md:34:   event formats, model names, rate-limit parsing, and connectivity checks live\nAGENTS.md:35:   under their adapter. Shared runtime and UI code must not parse provider-native\nAGENTS.md:36:   events.\nAGENTS.md:37:4. **Raw evidence survives normalization.** Preserve each session's raw output.\nAGENTS.md:38:   Normalizers are tolerant and must degrade to an unknown/raw observation\nAGENTS.md:39:   instead of crashing the supervisor.\nAGENTS.md:40:5. **The TUI observes shared models.** It reads state, registry, metrics, and\nAGENTS.md:41:   normalized observations. It does not implement lifecycle policy or maintain a\nAGENTS.md:42:   second liveness algorithm.\nAGENTS.md:43:6. **External capabilities stay external.** Provisioning invokes a launcher's\nAGENTS.md:44:   supported marketplace/plugin CLI and verifies the result. It does not copy\nAGENTS.md:45:   plugin contents into this package or hand-edit launcher caches.\nAGENTS.md:46:7. **No shell evaluation of configured commands.** Store agent commands as argv\nAGENTS.md:47:   arrays and spawn them without `shell=True`.\nAGENTS.md:48:\nAGENTS.md:49:## Target layout\nAGENTS.md:50:\nAGENTS.md:51:```text\nAGENTS.md:52:src/ovcaq/\nAGENTS.md:53:  state/        # schema, transactions, compound mutations\nAGENTS.md:54:  workflow/     # PRD selection, transitions, records, policy\nAGENTS.md:55:  runtime/      # supervisor, decisions, registry, process ownership\nAGENTS.md:56:  drivers/      # Claude, Codex, and generic command adapters\nAGENTS.md:57:  events/       # normalized event model and session reduction\nAGENTS.md:58:  tui/          # discovery and Textual/Rich presentation\nAGENTS.md:59:  provision/    # marketplace/plugin reconciliation\nAGENTS.md:60:tests/          # mirrors package boundaries; provider JSONL fixtures\nAGENTS.md:61:docs/           # tracked architecture and operator documentation\nAGENTS.md:62:```\nAGENTS.md:63:\nAGENTS.md:64:## Toolchain\nAGENTS.md:65:\nAGENTS.md:66:- Python 3.12+ managed through `mise`.\nAGENTS.md:67:- `uv` for environments, locking, build, and command execution.\nAGENTS.md:68:- `pytest` for tests; `ruff` for formatting/linting; `mypy` for package types.\nAGENTS.md:69:- Keep the runtime dependency set small. Textual/Rich are justified by the TUI;\nAGENTS.md:70:  prefer the standard library for state, locking, configuration, and subprocesses.\nAGENTS.md:71:\nAGENTS.md:72:Planned verification commands once the scaffold lands:\nAGENTS.md:73:\nAGENTS.md:74:```bash\nAGENTS.md:75:uv sync --all-groups\nAGENTS.md:76:uv run pytest\nAGENTS.md:77:uv run ruff check .\nAGENTS.md:78:uv run ruff format --check .\nAGENTS.md:79:uv run mypy src/ovcaq\nAGENTS.md:80:uv build\nAGENTS.md:81:```\nAGENTS.md:82:\nAGENTS.md:83:## Development rules\nAGENTS.md:84:\nAGENTS.md:85:- Characterize current behavior before moving it from `~/.claude`.\nAGENTS.md:86:- Extract state first, then pure workflow logic, runtime, adapters, TUI, and\nAGENTS.md:87:  provisioning in that order.\nAGENTS.md:88:- Sanitized Claude and Codex JSONL fixtures belong in `tests/fixtures/`; tests\nAGENTS.md:89:  never read live files from `~/.claude` or `~/.codex`.\nAGENTS.md:90:- Test process-group, lock, and signal behavior with real subprocesses in\nAGENTS.md:91:  addition to unit tests.\nAGENTS.md:92:- Documentation for commands comes from the same command registry/parser as the\nAGENTS.md:93:  implementation; do not maintain a second hand-written verb inventory.\nAGENTS.md:94:- Keep provider-neutral modules free of the strings `claude`, `anthropic`,\nAGENTS.md:95:  `codex`, and provider model identifiers except in tests that enforce this rule.\nAGENTS.md:96:- Use conventional commits. No compatibility shim, dual writer, or rollback\nAGENTS.md:97:  framework is required for the initial cutover.\nAGENTS.md:98:\nAGENTS.md:99:## Migration stance\nAGENTS.md:100:\nAGENTS.md:101:This is a one-way early-stage extraction. Start a fresh OVCAQ batch/state tree;\nAGENTS.md:102:do not build an importer for the current `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` unless\nAGENTS.md:103:a later requirement explicitly needs it. Temporary delegation during a local\nAGENTS.md:104:canary is acceptable, but do not ship `autoclaude`, `autopilot`, `tracon`, or\nAGENTS.md:105:`statectl` aliases as permanent public APIs.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:1:# AI assistant instructions\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:2:\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:3:- Solo developer. Be extremely concise, skip formalities.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:4:- Simplest safe assumption when ambiguity isn't material.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:5:- This file (`~/.claude/AGENTS.md`) is the single source of user-global agent instructions. `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` is only an `@AGENTS.md` pointer and `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` symlinks here. Edit this file, never the pointers.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:6:\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:7:## Workflow\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:8:\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:9:- After completing all PRD tasks, run `/autopilot:review-work-completion` in a fresh session - never from the work session (hand off first; see the review-phase rule below).\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:10:- For end-to-end PRD execution, use `/autopilot:run-autopilot`.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:11:- Procedures mandated by an invoked skill count as explicitly requested: ponytail (and any other minimalism mode) must never thin, skip, or down-scope skill-mandated steps. Minimalism governs what you build, not whether you follow the skill.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:12:- Never skip or thin the review phase of `/autopilot:run-autopilot`: every review cycle runs all lenses - consensus (Alice), blind/PRD-only (Blake), doubt+de-slop with rubric R1-R5 (Bob) - regardless of how small, simple, or well-specified the PRD looks. Do not ask whether the review is worth it - the answer is always yes. The worst issues land precisely when the code feels obviously correct. Reviewers get isolated contexts by construction (subagent prompts, external CLIs); the review phase itself runs in a fresh session after the build hand-off (end the turn: set `state.next_phase`, print the banner, STOP; in loop mode the headless session exits and the `autoclaude` wrapper relaunches from `state.json`). Never invoke `/autopilot:review-work-completion` from the work session, even if context budget appears sufficient. Scope (decided 2026-09-14, discovery 00203): this rule governs the full lane and every fast-track roster. Lane routing is decided by code (`cli/lane.py`, autopilot PRD 00204), never by an agent's judgment; the solo lane's one-pass zero-context review of docs/test-only PRDs, with code-decided escalation to the full gate on any production or security-ish diff, a CRITICAL or a surviving HIGH (PRD 00205), is the user's design, not an agent thinning the review.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:13:- After completing work, clean up stale worktrees, orphan branches, temp files, and executed plan files in the repo's `docs/dev/project-management/plans/`.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:14:- Meta-budget: Claude-on-Claude work (the `~/.claude` project) targets <= 30% of monthly spend; the portfolio brief surfaces the share monthly (red above ceiling). Released-plugin repo work counts as product, not meta.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:15:- If a secret may have been exposed, rotate it.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:16:\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:17:## Compaction\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:18:\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:19:- After failed approach: compact\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:20:- Mid-implementation: don't compact\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:21:- After completing a PRD, before next: compact\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:22:\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:23:With 1M context, research stays in-flight through implementation.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:24:\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:25:## Planning\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:26:\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:27:- End plans with unresolved questions.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:28:- One question at a time, concise, with enough context to answer quickly.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:29:\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:30:## Toolchain\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:31:\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:32:- CLI tools and language runtimes are managed with **mise**. Installs live under `~/.local/share/mise/installs/<tool>/<version>/`; shims at `~/.local/share/mise/shims/`.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:33:- Global `npm ls -g`, `/opt/homebrew/bin`, `/usr/local/bin` will NOT list mise-managed tools - don't conclude a tool is missing from those. Locate one with `mise which <tool>`; run one not on PATH via `mise exec -- <tool>`.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:34:- Not every install gets a shim - `mise which` can resolve a tool even when `command -v` fails. If a tool is missing from PATH, suggest `mise reshim`.\n/Users/bob/.claude/AGENTS.md:35:- The default model carries `[1m]` (1M window). The autopilot context cap (`autopilot_context_cap_hook.USAGE_CAP` in the autopilot plugin's `skills/run-autopilot/scripts/`, mirrored in `tracon/model.py` beside it) is 500K, coupled to that 1M default: never drop it toward 150K unless the default window also shrinks