+{"timestamp":"2026-10-10T09:41:19.275Z","ordinal":19,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a12530-0b4b-7bc1-bb9f-cbf937ca0337","turn_id":"01a12530-0f18-7dd0-be1b-bc3a1e3e56e4","session_id":"01a12530-0b4b-7bc1-bb9f-cbf937ca0337","root_turn_id":"01a12530-0f18-7dd0-be1b-bc3a1e3e56e4","response_id":"resp_050988c53c93d9fa016aca083acf048191a11043d16de5d255","usage":{"input_tokens":26838,"cached_input_tokens":26368,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":116,"reasoning_output_tokens":0,"total_tokens":26954},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":53447,"cached_input_tokens":39552,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":216,"reasoning_output_tokens":0,"total_tokens":53663},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":53447,"cached_input_tokens":39552,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":216,"reasoning_output_tokens":0,"total_tokens":53663}}}
+{"timestamp":"2026-10-10T09:41:19.316Z","ordinal":20,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a12530-0b4b-7bc1-bb9f-cbf937ca0337","turn_id":"01a12530-0f18-7dd0-be1b-bc3a1e3e56e4","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-d9534983-35ca-44b0-9b2c-560c8d3e224a","process_id":"12378","command":["/opt/homebrew/bin/bash","-lc","cat ~/.claude/AGENTS.md AGENTS.md /Users/bob/.claude/plugins/cache/buvis-plugins/autopilot/0.9.0/skills/run-autopilot/SKILL.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/ovcaq","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"cat ~/.claude/AGENTS.md AGENTS.md /Users/bob/.claude/plugins/cache/buvis-plugins/autopilot/0.9.0/skills/run-autopilot/SKILL.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"# AI assistant instructions\n\n- Solo developer. Be extremely concise, skip formalities.\n- Simplest safe assumption when ambiguity isn't material.\n- This file (`~/.claude/AGENTS.md`) is the single source of user-global agent instructions. `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` is only an `@AGENTS.md` pointer and `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` symlinks here. Edit this file, never the pointers.\n\n## Workflow\n\n- After completing all PRD tasks, run `/autopilot:review-work-completion` in a fresh session - never from the work session (hand off first; see the review-phase rule below).\n- For end-to-end PRD execution, use `/autopilot:run-autopilot`.\n- Procedures mandated by an invoked skill count as explicitly requested: ponytail (and any other minimalism mode) must never thin, skip, or down-scope skill-mandated steps. Minimalism governs what you build, not whether you follow the skill.\n- Never skip or thin the review phase of `/autopilot:run-autopilot`: every review cycle runs all lenses - consensus (Alice), blind/PRD-only (Blake), doubt+de-slop with rubric R1-R5 (Bob) - regardless of how small, simple, or well-specified the PRD looks. Do not ask whether the review is worth it - the answer is always yes. The worst issues land precisely when the code feels obviously correct. Reviewers get isolated contexts by construction (subagent prompts, external CLIs); the review phase itself runs in a fresh session after the build hand-off (end the turn: set `state.next_phase`, print the banner, STOP; in loop mode the headless session exits and the `autoclaude` wrapper relaunches from `state.json`). Never invoke `/autopilot:review-work-completion` from the work session, even if context budget appears sufficient. Scope (decided 2026-09-14, discovery 00203): this rule governs the full lane and every fast-track roster. Lane routing is decided by code (`cli/lane.py`, autopilot PRD 00204), never by an agent's judgment; the solo lane's one-pass zero-context review of docs/test-only PRDs, with code-decided escalation to the full gate on any production or security-ish diff, a CRITICAL or a surviving HIGH (PRD 00205), is the user's design, not an agent thinning the review.\n- After completing work, clean up stale worktrees, orphan branches, temp files, and executed plan files in the repo's `docs/dev/project-management/plans/`.\n- Meta-budget: Claude-on-Claude work (the `~/.claude` project) targets <= 30% of monthly spend; the portfolio brief surfaces the share monthly (red above ceiling). Released-plugin repo work counts as product, not meta.\n- If a secret may have been exposed, rotate it.\n\n## Compaction\n\n- After failed approach: compact\n- Mid-implementation: don't compact\n- After completing a PRD, before next: compact\n\nWith 1M context, research stays in-flight through implementation.\n\n## Planning\n\n- End plans with unresolved questions.\n- One question at a time, concise, with enough context to answer quickly.\n\n## Toolchain\n\n- CLI tools and language runtimes are managed with **mise**. Installs live under `~/.local/share/mise/installs/<tool>/<version>/`; shims at `~/.local/share/mise/shims/`.\n- Global `npm ls -g`, `/opt/homebrew/bin`, `/usr/local/bin` will NOT list mise-managed tools - don't conclude a tool is missing from those. Locate one with `mise which <tool>`; run one not on PATH via `mise exec -- <tool>`.\n- Not every install gets a shim - `mise which` can resolve a tool even when `command -v` fails. If a tool is missing from PATH, suggest `mise reshim`.\n- The default model carries `[1m]` (1M window). The autopilot context cap (`autopilot_context_cap_hook.USAGE_CAP` in the autopilot plugin's `skills/run-autopilot/scripts/`, mirrored in `tracon/model.py` beside it) is 500K, coupled to that 1M default: never drop it toward 150K unless the default window also shrinks below 200K, or the cap misfires at ~163K aborting healthy build sessions (PRD 00073; 150K pin reverted 2026-07-20 when `[1m]` was restored).\n- Before launching a `~/.claude` autopilot batch, `export _AUTOPILOT_WRITE_SCOPE_EXTRA=\"$HOME/.config/bash:$HOME/.codex:$HOME/.local/bin\"` - the buvis repo spans those roots and the write-scope fence (`hooks/enforce_write_scope.py`, armed by `CLAUDE_UNATTENDED=1`) denies them otherwise; `_AUTOPILOT_WRITE_SCOPE=off` disarms the fence for one batch.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Repos live at `~/git/src/github.com/{org}/{repo}` (Go-style layout).\n- Name tools, plugins, and projects with evocative single words (warden, strunk) over descriptive concatenations; offer 3-4 candidates with rationale.\n- When a standing rule already mandates a change discovered mid-task, produce the reversible artifact instead of asking; flag user-facing impact in the summary.\n- **Skills live in `~/git/src/github.com/buvis/agent-skills`, not in buvis** (since 2026-08-25): edit and commit there, then `braid --check`; never edit through the link farm or stage a skill path into buvis. Layout, hops and policy: `rules/memory.md` and `~/.agents/README.md`.\n- Kept-but-dormant material lives in `~/.claude/.archive/`: `skills/` (parked skills, not scanned, so they cost no boot context - `mv` one back into `~/.claude/skills/` to reactivate it) and `rules/` (retired rule text). `rules-library/` is NOT an archive: `rationalizations.md` is read at runtime by `hooks/cartographer-echo.py`.\n- Native Bash grants in `settings.json` are deliberately broad (`git`, `rmdir`, `docker`, `kubectl` at `:*`), and so are the `tmp` Edit wildcards. `rm` is NOT granted natively - warden gates every `rm`. **Warden is the gate**, not the native allowlist: it filters every Bash command and asks or denies on its own rules. Config audits will keep flagging these as broad grants - that verdict is accepted, decided 2026-08-02. Do not re-narrow them audit by audit. Revisit only if warden stops gating Bash.\n\n## Dotfiles repo\n\n- Tracked in a bare repo at `~/.buvis`, work-tree is `$HOME`. Run git as `git --git-dir=~/.buvis --work-tree=~ <cmd>` (no shell alias assumed).\n- **This repo answers \"not tracked\" by staying silent. That has already caused a wrong conclusion.** Treat ANY empty output from `~/.buvis` as unverified until a known-tracked control path proves the command shape works.\n  - The index IS populated and matches HEAD (verified 2026-08-17: `ls-files` and `ls-tree -r --name-only HEAD` both return 641 entries), so `ls-files` is a valid tracking check. Still prefer `git diff HEAD` over plain `git diff`: the index is shared with the user's live sessions, so anything they staged is invisible to an index-vs-worktree diff.\n  - Pathspecs resolve against **cwd**, not the work-tree. From anywhere but `~`, `-- .claude/foo` silently searches `<cwd>/.claude/foo` and finds nothing. Anchor every pathspec with `:(top)`, e.g. `add ':(top).claude/rules/foo.md'`. `git log` and `show --stat` keep working regardless, which makes the repo look fine while the path looks absent.\n- The `rules/changelog.md` mandate does **not** apply here: no releases, no CHANGELOG. Commit `feat`/`fix` directly without a CHANGELOG entry, and don't add a CHANGELOG.md to this repo.\n# AGENTS.md — OVCAQ\n\nOVCAQ means **Orchestration, Visibility, and Control for Agent Queues**. It is\nan installable Python CLI/TUI that supervises a PRD-driven workflow through\nClaude Code, Codex, or another headless coding agent.\n\nThe global `AGENTS.md` remains in force. This file adds project-specific rules.\n\n## Current state\n\nThe repository is in planning/bootstrap. Until an implementation PRD replaces\nit, `docs/dev/project-management/plans/implementation-plan.md` is the architecture and sequencing\nauthority. Do not create production modules opportunistically outside that\nsequence.\n\n## Product boundary\n\nOVCAQ core owns state, lifecycle commands, process supervision, agent adapters,\nnormalized events, observability, the TUI, and launcher provisioning checks.\n\nOVCAQ core does **not** contain launcher-specific skills, agents, hooks, or\nplugins. Those live in external marketplaces. Core may carry declarative\nidentifiers and version requirements needed to reconcile them.\n\n## Architecture invariants\n\n1. **One state writer.** Every durable `state.json` mutation goes through the\n   state store. Agents mutate state only through `ovcaq state` or a higher-level\n   OVCAQ command. Never instruct an agent to edit `state.json` directly.\n2. **State decides; prose does not.** Session completion, phase handoff, pause,\n   drain, retry, and failure decisions derive from committed state plus a\n   normalized session result. A final agent message is never authoritative.\n3. **Provider code stays at the edge.** Claude/Codex argv, environment variables,\n   event formats, model names, rate-limit parsing, and connectivity checks live\n   under their adapter. Shared runtime and UI code must not parse provider-native\n   events.\n4. **Raw evidence survives normalization.** Preserve each session's raw output.\n   Normalizers are tolerant and must degrade to an unknown/raw observation\n   instead of crashing the supervisor.\n5. **The TUI observes shared models.** It reads state, registry, metrics, and\n   normalized observations. It does not implement lifecycle policy or maintain a\n   second liveness algorithm.\n6. **External capabilities stay external.** Provisioning invokes a launcher's\n   supported marketplace/plugin CLI and verifies the result. It does not copy\n   plugin contents into this package or hand-edit launcher caches.\n7. **No shell evaluation of configured commands.** Store agent commands as argv\n   arrays and spawn them without `shell=True`.\n\n## Target layout\n\n```text\nsrc/ovcaq/\n  state/        # schema, transactions, compound mutations\n  workflow/     # PRD selection, transitions, records, policy\n  runtime/      # supervisor, decisions, registry, process ownership\n  drivers/      # Claude, Codex, and generic command adapters\n  events/       # normalized event model and session reduction\n  tui/          # discovery and Textual/Rich presentation\n  provision/    # marketplace/plugin reconciliation\ntests/          # mirrors package boundaries; provider JSONL fixtures\ndocs/           # tracked architecture and operator documentation\n```\n\n## Toolchain\n\n- Python 3.12+ managed through `mise`.\n- `uv` for environments, locking, build, and command execution.\n- `pytest` for tests; `ruff` for formatting/linting; `mypy` for package types.\n- Keep the runtime dependency set small. Textual/Rich are justified by the TUI;\n  prefer the standard library for state, locking, configuration, and subprocesses.\n\nPlanned verification commands once the scaffold lands:\n\n```bash\nuv sync --all-groups\nuv run pytest\nuv run ruff check .\nuv run ruff format --check .\nuv run mypy src/ovcaq\nuv build\n```\n\n## Development rules\n\n- Characterize current behavior before moving it from `~/.claude`.\n- Extract state first, then pure workflow logic, runtime, adapters, TUI, and\n  provisioning in that order.\n- Sanitized Claude and Codex JSONL fixtures belong in `tests/fixtures/`; tests\n  never read live files from `~/.claude` or `~/.codex`.\n- Test process-group, lock, and signal behavior with real subprocesses in\n  addition to unit tests.\n- Documentation for commands comes from the same command registry/parser as the\n  implementation; do not maintain a second hand-written verb inventory.\n- Keep provider-neutral modules free of the strings `claude`, `anthropic`,\n  `codex`, and provider model identifiers except in tests that enforce this rule.\n- Use conventional commits. No compatibility shim, dual writer, or rollback\n  framework is required for the initial cutover.\n\n## Migration stance\n\nThis is a one-way early-stage extraction. Start a fresh OVCAQ batch/state tree;\ndo not build an importer for the current `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` unless\na later requirement explicitly needs it. Temporary delegation during a local\ncanary is acceptable, but do not ship `autoclaude`, `autopilot`, `tracon`, or\n`statectl` aliases as permanent public APIs.\n---\nname: run-autopilot\ndescription: Use when running a PRD end-to-end autonomously - catchup, plan, work, and a review-rework loop running consensus, blind, and doubt lenses every cycle. Triggers on \"autopilot\", \"run autopilot\", \"autopilot status\", \"drain backlog\".\nargument-hint: \"[<prd-filename> | status]\"\ncompatibility: \"Requires Bob personal Claude/autoclaude environment or equivalent host adapters for sub-agents, state, and review handoffs.\"\n---\n\n> **Paths in this pack.** This pack's root is `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` - that line\n> is substituted when this skill loads, so what you just read is the real\n> directory. Files under `references/` are read at runtime and are **not**\n> substituted: when one shows `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/...`, swap in the root above\n> before running anything. Never pass the literal placeholder to a shell - it\n> expands to the empty string and the path silently becomes `/...`.\n\n> **Role effort:** main sessions default to low coordinator effort. This does\n> not reduce worker or reviewer effort. Every native Claude task dispatch uses\n> `autopilot:worker-<tier>` with the full rendered persona and selected model:\n> haiku/low, sonnet/medium, opus/high, human-authorized fable/xhigh. Named\n> Alice/Blake reviewers run at high; other native reviewers use the high-effort\n> `autopilot:worker-opus` wrapper with their selected model. External Claude\n> reviews pass `sonnet-run.sh --effort high`. Preserve all review gates, lenses,\n> isolation, and escalation rules. Other provider hosts use their host mapping.\n\n# Autopilot\n\nOrchestrate the full PRD lifecycle: catchup → design → plan-tasks → work → review-rework loop (consensus + blind + doubt lenses, every cycle) → done.\n\nMakes autonomous decisions backed by research (dependencies, recurring issues, API/schema changes when PRD-driven) and pauses only for critical security, requirements ambiguity, or blocking decisions.\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- Pack skills (invoked as phases): `design-solution`, `plan-tasks`, `work`,\n  `review-work-completion`\n- Plugin skills: `git-ferry:catchup` (Phase 1, unless the PRD sets `catchup: skip`)\n- Files read from other skill dirs:\n  `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/review-work-completion/scripts/await_reviewer_outputs.py`\n  (Watcher keep-alive)\n- Hooks outside the skill dir: `~/.claude/hooks/notify.py`\n- Shell wrapper outside `~/.claude`: `autoclaude` in\n  `~/.config/bash/plugins/development.plugin.bash` - operator subcommands +\n  tracon front-end; it hands off to `autopilot loop` (`cli/loop.py`), the\n  unattended session loop since PRD 00106\n- Scripts (own dir, model-invoked as a CLI): `scripts/statectl.py` — the sole\n  `state.json` mutator (`get`/`set`/`append`/`del`; atomic, advisory-locked,\n  one rotating `.bak`)\n- CLIs: `claude` (headless `-p`), `python3`, `git`, `awk`\n- Optional: `tracon` (`scripts/tracon/`; the default `autoclaude` front-end since PRD 00062, `_AUTOPILOT_TRACON=0` or no tty/`uv` falls back to `render_stream.py`) - dashboard only, absence changes nothing\n\n## Execution Model\n\n**Run all phases in sequence without stopping.** After each phase completes, immediately update state and proceed to the next phase. Do not pause between phases, do not summarize progress, do not wait for user input - unless the phase explicitly says PAUSE or STOP. Completing a sub-skill invocation (`/git-ferry:catchup`, `/autopilot:plan-tasks`, `/autopilot:work`, `/autopilot:review-work-completion`, etc.) is NOT a stopping point. It is an intermediate step. Continue.\n\nReview sessions run on opus at xhigh in cycle 1 and at high on cycle 2 and later; _AUTOPILOT_EFFORT_REVIEW forces one effort for every cycle and _AUTOPILOT_EFFORT_REVIEW_RERUN sets the rerun value. A rework resume (the cycle's review file exists and `rework_task_ids` names an unfinished task) runs on the queued tasks' own tier instead, sonnet unless one is opus or fable (`references/model-ladder.md` § Review sessions, PRD 00207).\n\n## Entry Points\n\n- `/autopilot:run-autopilot` — auto-select PRD (wip first, then backlog), run full cycle\n- `/autopilot:run-autopilot <prd-filename>` — full cycle with specific PRD\n- `/autopilot:run-autopilot status` — print current dashboard, no action\n- `/autopilot:run-autopilot review-batch` — interactively review a finished batch's deferred items\n\nIf invoked with `status`, read `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json`, print phase/cycle/task summary, and stop.\n\nIf invoked with `review-batch`, load the newest `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/deferred/{batch_id}-deferred.json` and run the \"Batch-End Review\" presentation (`references/phase-done.md`) against it — chunked by PRD, wait for user decisions, execute \"fix now\" items. No state changes; the batch is already closed. Stop when all chunks are reviewed (or the user says stop).\n\nPreflight for a batch rooted at `~/.claude`: the launching shell must `export _AUTOPILOT_WRITE_SCOPE_EXTRA=\"$HOME/.config/bash:$HOME/.codex:$HOME/.local/bin\"`, or the write-scope fence (`hooks/enforce_write_scope.py`) false-denies edits under those roots and the task parks; `_AUTOPILOT_WRITE_SCOPE=off` disarms the fence for the batch.\n\n## Gate Dispatch\n\nThe full per-phase instructions live in three gate reference files; this core deliberately carries only the shared contract plus the invariants below. After the entry-point checks, read `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` and **Read the matching gate file now; do not execute a gate from memory**:\n\nA state carrying `stall_op` runs `autopilot park` to reconcile before any gate file is read.\n\n| resume state | Read this gate file |\n|--------------|---------------------|\n| `phase: \"build\"` — or `state.json` missing (fresh start) | `references/phase-build.md` |\n| `phase: \"review\"` — or a legacy `\"blind\"`/`\"doubt\"` value (pre-00015 state), which maps to `review` | `references/phase-review.md` |\n| `phase: \"done\"` | `references/phase-done.md` |\n| `phase: \"paused\"` — or `stall_reason` set | `references/phase-build.md` (its Phase 0 abort/pause handlers own this resume) |\n\n`status` and `review-batch` invocations do not dispatch a gate (see Entry Points).\n\n## State Management\n\nAll autopilot artifacts live under `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/`, organized by type:\n\n```\ndocs/dev/project-management/autopilot/\n  state.json                              # current cycle state\n  last-session.log                        # loop-teed raw output of the last headless session\n  reports/{batch_id}-report.md            # batch audit report\n  deferred/{batch_id}-deferred.json       # unresolved items across PRDs\n```\n\nState file: `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` — see `references/state-schema.md` for schema.\n\n**`statectl` is the sole writer of `state.json`.** Apply every field mutation with `python3 ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/run-autopilot/scripts/statectl.py <state.json> set|append|del <json-path> <json-value>` (read one field with `get`); it takes an advisory lock, backs up to `<state.json>.bak`, changes only the named path while preserving all siblings, and replaces the file atomically — so it cannot leave a half-written or unparseable state, and it never trips the \"not read yet\" / \"modified since read\" errors the editing tools hit. Since PRD 00089 it is a shim over `cli/state.transaction`, so every write is schema-validated too: a value that is malformed **for the field it targets** is rejected with exit 1 and the file left byte-unchanged (an unrelated pre-existing odd field blocks nothing). **Phase transitions are not field mutations** — use `autopilot phase-done --outcome <outcome>` (§ Session handoff procedure), never a hand-written `set phase` / `set next_phase` pair, so a transition cannot land without the effects it mandates. Resolve `<state.json>` by walking up from the resolved physical cwd to the autopilot dir (the `_walk_up.py` pattern in `/autopilot:work` step 2). The terse `set X` / `write field Y` / per-PRD-reset instructions throughout these skills and the gate files name the fields to change; each is applied with `statectl`, never by hand-editing the file.\n\nDocumented human fallback: outside the loop a person may still Edit or Write `state.json` by hand (for forensics or recovery — the model itself never hand-edits `state.json`; a statectl failure has its own marker-based recovery below); `validate_state_json_hook.py` catches a hand-edit that leaves the file unparseable and now also runs `cli.schema.validate` on it, and `statectl` reads whatever valid JSON it finds on its next mutation.\n\n**When `statectl` itself fails** (nonzero exit — advisory-lock contention, a Python error, an unreadable `state.json`): retry the exact command ONCE. If it fails again, do NOT improvise a partial write and do NOT proceed on the stale state — a lost mutation strands the loop on the wrong `next_phase`. Run `autopilot restore` (rolls `state.json` back to `<state.json>.bak`) and retry the statectl command once more. If the failure persists (`autopilot restore` itself fails, or the subsequent statectl write still fails), WRITE THE MARKER `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state-write-failed` with the Write tool — ONE line of JSON `{\"site\": \"statectl_fail\", \"detail\": \"<the statectl command and its raw stderr>\"}` — and END THE TURN: the wrapper halts on the marker and surfaces the `detail` itself, so no further state write is needed. Interactive sessions ALSO report the same failure to the user directly (there is no wrapper watching an interactive terminal). The raw stderr rides into the marker's `detail` so the failure is diagnosable, never silent (`rules/operating-principles.md`). (Lock contention on the single-writer model implies a second loop or a stale lock; the concurrent-loop guard below now refuses a second loop up front, so a persistent lock is a stale one — the retry clears it or the marker records it.)\n\nCreate `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/` and subdirectories if missing. Initialize state file at PRD selection. Update state at every phase transition. Autopilot also keeps a per-PRD **decision audit log** at `docs/dev/project-management/reviews/<prd-base>-audit.md`, **rendered once at Phase 9 finalize from the `state.json` decision arrays** (`autonomous_decisions`, `deferred_decisions`, `doubts`). `state.json` is the single in-run source of truth — decisions are NOT mirrored to `audit.md` incrementally per decision. Each rendered entry carries a **source** label (`autonomous`, `deferred`, or `doubt`); cycle/phase context goes in the entry body so the Phase 9 `decisions.md` projection can filter autonomous entries by label. The render is `autopilot render audit` (PRD 00107); the label set and the projection live in `references/audit-log-format.md`.\n\n**Invariant:** every state mutation that advances `phase` SHOULD also set `next_phase` to the same value. The three gates are `build` | `review` | `done` (plus `paused`). `build` is ONE session: selection, catchup, design, planning, and work all run under `phase: \"build\"` with no mid-build handoff. The review surface runs in its own fresh session; blind and doubt scrutiny are LENSES inside every review cycle (Blake and Bob in `review-work-completion`'s roster), not separate phases — reviewers get isolated contexts by construction (subagent prompts, external CLIs). Legacy `blind`/`doubt` phase values in pre-00015 state files map to `review` on resume. Per-task implementor tiering inside `/autopilot:work` is unchanged. The authoritative resume signal is `phase` + `phases_completed`; build sub-step skipping is by ARTIFACT (capsule freshness, design-doc-exists, tasks-exist, all-done), not by `phases_completed` membership. This resume decision (phase + phases_completed + artifact checks → next step) is encoded canonically in `cli/resume.py` (`scripts/resume_target.py` re-exports it), which `scripts/test_autopilot_resume.py` imports — editing the resume logic there flips a test red rather than silently drifting from this prose. Run `autopilot resume-target` at Phase 0 to print what that encoding says the next action is, and reconcile it against the handler you are about to follow: a disagreement means the prose and the encoded contract have drifted, and the encoding wins. It also runs the schema-version preflight, so an old or future `state.json` is surfaced there rather than resumed blindly. It answers for the `build` and `review` gates; `phase: \"done\"` returns `unknown phase: done` by design — the done gate is routed by the table above, not by this function.\n\n### Retention\n\nEverything under `docs/dev/project-management/` is tracked work-management state. It is never age-purged. Completed PRDs, reviews, designs, audit logs, reports, ledgers, deferred records, telemetry, and the project capsule therefore remain available to remotes. Runtime control files such as `state.json`, marker files, lane files, and wave slots may still be removed by their explicit lifecycle transitions, but never by a generic stale-file collector.\n\n- **Durable** (never delete): `docs/dev/project-management/prds/done/` and its PRDs, `docs/dev/project-management/reviews/` (per-cycle review files, blind/doubt reviews, and the `<prd-base>-audit.md` audit renders), `docs/dev/project-management/designs/` (per-PRD design docs), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/reports/` (batch reports), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/deferred/` (the `{batch_id}-deferred.json` records), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/ledger/loop-metrics.jsonl` (the ledger mirror, the durable copy to read), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/ledger/attempts.jsonl` (per-attempt rows appended at every PRD close, `references/state-schema.md` § Attempt ledger), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/ledger/custody.jsonl` (the custody journal, the push guard's restore source when the `critical-on-master` marker is removed, `references/state-schema.md` § Custody journal), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/ledger/dispatch-metrics.jsonl` (per-dispatch timing rows and session-handoff edges, `work/references/subagent-dispatch.md` § Dispatch telemetry), `docs/dev/project-management/meta/project-capsule.md`, and `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/ledger/<wave id>-wave.md` (the ledger mirror of the wave summary report `autopilot wave assemble` writes, the durable copy to read). The `reports/<wave id>-wave.md` copy it mirrors is tracked as well, but the ledger mirror is the one to read.\n- **Not durable** (untracked scratch): `docs/dev/tmp/`, the only tree `purge-devtmp` touches.\n- **Disposable** (removed by their lifecycle transitions, by name): `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/last-session.log`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/wrapper.log`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/pause-requested`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/paused-by-operator`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/session-brief.md` (re-rendered at every handoff), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.session-left` (overwritten at every loop-mode handoff), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/lanes/` (one marker per live CLI reviewer lane, removed when the lane exits), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` (at batch end only — in loop mode the wrapper archives it to `reports/{batch_id}-state-final.json`; see `references/phase-done.md`), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/wave.json`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/wave-slots/` and `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/review-paths` (a wave's control file, its per-wave review-slot directory, and the review's path-scoping list — the last removed with the assembly worktree at wave end, same lifecycle as the other two — none is a durable PRD-lifecycle artifact; `references/waves.md`), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/replan-context.md`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json.bak`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/**/*.lock` (the flock files beside `state.json` and beside each `deferred/*.json`), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.turn-counts.json`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.handoff-requested`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.cap-fired`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/contract-card.md`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/park-requested`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/last-verification.json`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.review-gate-blocks`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.review-gate-failed`, and `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.lane-guard-blocks`.\n\nThe machine form of this list is `docs/dev/project-management/.gitignore`, shipped by the Phase 0 `mkdir` step; a test keeps the two in step.\n\nTemporary review context and other reproducible scratch material belongs under the globally ignored `docs/dev/tmp/` tree. `purge-devtmp` operates only there, preserves files linked to live PRDs, and never traverses `docs/dev/project-management/`.\n\nBatch end and cleanup steps enumerate lifecycle-owned runtime files explicitly; they never remove a directory wholesale. This keeps a completed PRD's complete review trail intact after the batch closes (`references/design-rationale.md` § Verified moves).\n\n### Resuming\n\nWhen `/autopilot:run-autopilot` is invoked and `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` exists with `batch.completed_prds`, this is a continuation after a session restart. Preserve `batch.completed_prds` (including `batch.id`) and proceed to the build gate's Phase 0 to pick the next PRD.\n\nDelete `state.pause_reason` from `state.json` if present — unconditional, on every invocation, NOT gated on `batch.completed_prds`. A new session means any pause is being resumed; `pause_reason` belongs only to an unresolved pause and is not overwritten by normal progression, so it must be cleared here or it halts the resumed PRD's next hand-off. Cap-pause detection is unaffected — it keys on `cap_pause_reason`, which this delete does not touch.\n\n### Operator runbook (unattended batches)\n\nAll interaction with a running `autoclaude` batch happens at session boundaries — the only safe point. (Operator-shell commands for the human's own terminal, not for the model's Bash tool — `tail -f` would hang a session and pipes are hook-blocked.)\n\n- **Watch**: `tail -f docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/last-session.log` — the wrapper tees the live event log of the running headless turn there.\n- **Pause**: `touch docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/pause-requested` — the wrapper consumes the marker at the next session boundary, notifies \"paused by operator\", and stops the loop with state intact. It leaves `paused-by-operator` behind so tracon shows `⏸ paused` rather than `⚠ orphaned`; plain `autoclaude` resumes (nothing to clear, no decision to make). The marker may carry a JSON reason, `{\"reason\": \"...\"}`, which the stop line and the notification quote; a session that stands down for a peer writes one with a `condition` beside it, after asking the peer first (§ Session Loop, stand-down procedure).\n- **Take over**: with the loop stopped (pause marker, or Ctrl-C on the wrapper), run `/autopilot:run-autopilot` in a normal interactive session — resume-by-artifact reads the same `state.json`, and full interactive semantics (questions, PAUSEs) apply because `$_AUTOPILOT_LOOP` is unset. Restart `autoclaude` afterwards to go unattended again.\n- **Resume after a PAUSE** (`phase: \"paused\"` in `state.json`, \"session paused\" banner/ntfy): same take-over recipe, and it is the ONLY way forward — re-running `autoclaude` reads the paused state and exits again immediately. Interactively, the phase that paused re-runs with `AskUserQuestion` available, so the blockers that halted the loop are asked as decisions instead. Answer them, let the session hand off or finish the PRD, then re-run `autoclaude`.\n- **Un-park a sidelined PRD**: a PRD the loop parked (a `wrapper_died` death-park, or a loop-mode `design_gate` / `blocking_escalation` stall) sits in `docs/dev/project-management/prds/hold/` with its stall recorded in the batch deferred JSON; the batch kept draining without it. To resume it, `mv docs/dev/project-management/prds/hold/<00XXX-…>.md docs/dev/project-management/prds/backlog/` (keep the `00XXX-` prefix). Autopilot never reads `hold/`, so the next `/autopilot:run-autopilot` (or `autoclaude` relaunch) re-selects it from `backlog/` and re-enters the build gate, reusing its existing design doc via the Phase 1.5 artifact skip (a `design_gate` park then re-fires the gate interactively). Batch-end review lists every parked PRD (`{m} stalled`), so none is silently forgotten.\n- **Forensics**: `claude --resume <session-id>` (the id is in the init event at the top of `last-session.log`) reopens a finished headless conversation for \n... command output truncated for persistence ...\nr batch's row out; when no such `leave` row exists, skip the mtime test and let the dirty-tree test and the reply decide). That comparison holds only because every handoff site writes its `leave` row AFTER its last state and card write (§ Session handoff procedure orders step 1 before step 2; `/autopilot:work`'s task-boundary handoff must set `next_phase` before its row too): a site that touches `state.json` after its row makes every next session read its own hand-off as peer evidence. A bare recent mtime is not evidence (a reader that inspects state while the loop writes, or this batch's own per-PRD reset, satisfies it), commit trailers are not consulted (no commit here carries a session trailer), and a peer that only reads, or never answers, never pauses a batch. To stand down: do not select, move, or write anything else; write `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/pause-requested` with the Write tool as one JSON line, `{\"reason\": \"<peer session> owns <prd> (<evidence>)\", \"condition\": \"<peer_claimed | dirty_tree | state_after_leave>\"}`, and end the turn. The loop reads a marker written during the session as a stand-down (branch 5 above): it stops with state intact, burns no retry, writes no park marker, quotes the reason in its stop line and notification, and records `stood_down` and `stood_down_condition` in the session's `loop-metrics.jsonl` row; `autoclaude` alone resumes once the peer is done. Before this procedure existed (2026-09-02, agent-skills PRD 00010) four stand-downs read as deaths and parked a healthy PRD mid-task.\n\nThe model's only job at a hand-off is to write `state.next_phase` (and `phase`/`stall_reason`) accurately, print the banner, and end the turn. The model never writes any signal and never inspects the loop's decision table — writing accurate state IS the hand-off. Interactive (non-loop) semantics are identical minus the loop: the same state writes happen, and the user re-invokes `/autopilot:run-autopilot` manually.\n\n**End the turn only at a real hand-off.** A phase is complete only when its artifacts are written and `state` is advanced (`phases_completed` updated, `next_phase` set). Dispatched work — `Agent` calls and background Bash — returns its results **within the same headless turn**: the harness re-invokes you with each `<task-notification>` before the turn can end, so dispatch, overlap independent work, wait for the results, and finish the phase. Do not end the turn to \"wait for\" something you dispatched.\n\n**Background Bash alone cannot hold a headless session open.** Documented `-p` behavior: live subagents keep the process alive across turn ends and re-invoke on completion, but background Bash tasks are killed ~5s after the final result. A turn that ends while ONLY background Bash (codex/gemini/qwen, `cargo test`, …) is still running kills that work and strands the phase (2026-07-12: the review session died this way twice — codex killed mid-review, state untouched, loop halted). Whenever dispatched background Bash may outlive every live subagent, dispatch a **Watcher subagent** in the same message that re-runs `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/review-work-completion/scripts/await_reviewer_outputs.py` against the expected output files until `DONE` (procedure: `/autopilot:review-work-completion` step 5). The loop's session runner exports `CLAUDE_CODE_PRINT_BG_WAIT_CEILING_MS=0` so the subagent wait has no 10-minute ceiling; the session wall-clock cap stays the backstop.\n\nAn idle end-of-turn (phase unfinished, nothing pending) no longer thrashes anything — the loop relaunches and the next session resumes the phase by artifact (self-healing) — but it burns a session start, so treat it as waste, not as a mechanism.\n\nFor long **Bash** (builds, tests), still prefer the FOREGROUND with an explicit `timeout` (up to 600000 ms) so the result is in hand directly. If genuine work cannot finish in this session, that is a PAUSE (`phase: \"paused\"` + `state.pause_reason = {\"site\": \"work_incomplete\", \"detail\": \"<what could not finish>\"}` + end turn), not a silent idle stop.\n\n### Session handoff procedure (canonical — gate files invoke it by site name)\n\nEvery session handoff is the same three steps:\n\n1. **Apply the transition** with one `autopilot phase-done --outcome <outcome>` call (table below). It reads the current phase from `state.json`, looks the pair up in `cli/transitions.py`, and commits the next phase together with every field effect that transition mandates — in ONE transaction. There is no `--phase` flag and no per-effect flag: a caller cannot supply a mismatched phase, and cannot forget an effect.\n2. **Write the session brief, then the `leave` handoff row, then record the store, then print the site's banner** (shown at the invoking site in the gate file). The brief comes first, after the contract card (§ Contract card): `python3 ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/run-autopilot/scripts/statectl.py <state.json> write-brief docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/session-brief.md` renders `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/session-brief.md` from the state step 1 committed (where the batch stands, the card verbatim, the files the next gate needs), so the next session orients from one page (PRD 00201); the loop's launch prompt names it whenever it exists. A failed write is one stderr line, never a phase failure: carry on to the row. The row is `python3 ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/work/scripts/record_dispatch.py handoff --site <build|review|done> --edge leave --phase <state.phase> --prd <state.prd>`, best-effort (`work/references/subagent-dispatch.md` § Dispatch telemetry); `state.phase` already names the next session's phase because step 1 committed it. A new site row in the table below gets its row from this step, not from a per-site sentence. Under `$_AUTOPILOT_LOOP` the row is also the session's point of no return: `hooks/note_session_leave.py` records it, and `hooks/guard_skill_after_leave.py` denies any `autopilot:*` (or `git-ferry:catchup`) `Skill` call this session makes afterwards, so a session that prints its banner and then re-invokes a gate is stopped by the harness, not only by this prose (PRD 00211). After the row, and as the LAST write before STOP, record the store with `autopilot record-store`: `python3 ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/run-autopilot/cli/__main__.py record-store --state <state.json> --site <build|review|done> --prd <state.prd>`, best-effort like the row (a failure is one stderr line, never a reason to skip the row or the STOP). It runs at every site in the table below and comes after the row, never between the brief and the row: the row itself appends to the tracked `dispatch-metrics.jsonl`, so only a commit that follows it sweeps that append in.\n3. **End the turn.** In loop mode the loop driver reads `state.json` and relaunches per its decision table above; outside the loop the user re-invokes `/autopilot:run-autopilot` and the same resume logic applies. Do NOT continue into the next gate's phases in this session, even if context budget appears sufficient.\n\n| site | `--outcome` | what the transition commits |\n|------|-------------|-----------------------------|\n| **build → review** (`phase-build.md`, after Phase 3) | `tasks_done` | `phase`/`next_phase: \"review\"`. `phases_completed` unchanged — the build gate leaves no membership marker. `state.tasks` is already current — every producer (`task-add`/`task-start`/`task-done`/`task-set-meta`) writes it directly via `statectl`, so there is nothing to query or snapshot at this handoff. |\n| **review → review** (`phase-review.md` Phase 6, after rework when the loop continues) | `rework` | `phase`/`next_phase: \"review\"`, `cycle` incremented, `rework_task_ids` cleared — all in one commit. `state.tasks` untouched. `phases_completed` unchanged — only convergence adds `\"review\"`. |\n| **review → done** (`phase-review.md`, on loop convergence) | `converged` | `phase`/`next_phase: \"done\"`, and `\"review\"` appended to `phases_completed` — the convergence marker the review gate's loop-level skip reads. It lands *because this is convergence*, not because a flag asked for it. |\n| **build → done** (`references/lane-solo.md` § Close, a lane-routed PRD whose one review pass wrote its review file) | `lane_reviewed` | Convergence's own effect set: `phase`/`next_phase: \"done\"` and `\"review\"` appended once to `phases_completed`, so `review_coverage_hook.py` gates the lane's `-review-1.md` with `--require-codex-guard` exactly as a full-lane cycle's. An escalating solo build takes the **build → review** row instead. |\n| **PRD → PRD** (`phase-done.md`, step 10 more-PRDs branch) | `more_prds` | `phase`/`next_phase: \"build\"`, `phases_completed` reset to `[]`, and the whole per-PRD reset (`cli/records.PER_PRD_RESET_FIELDS` — the one authoritative list); `batch` preserved in full. |\n| **batch end** (`phase-done.md`, no more PRDs) | `drained` | `phase: \"done\"` and the EMPTY `next_phase` the loop reads as drained. Not a handoff — nothing relaunches. Also legal from `build`, which is where Phase 0's \"No PRDs anywhere\" reaches it. |\n\nWriting any of these fields by hand with `statectl set` is a bug: it is how a mandatory effect gets dropped. The `cycle` increment is the one that has already cost a real batch — the Phase 5 cap gate reads it, and losing it once let a loop run past its cap.\n\n### Contract card (compaction re-anchor)\n\nWithin-session compaction can drop this skill's text mid-procedure, after which the session drifts off-contract with nothing to re-anchor it. To survive that, **at each gate transition write a compact contract card** — the current step, the active invariants, and the next gate:\n\n- **Autopilot (state.json world):** write the body to `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/contract-card.md` with the **Write tool**, then load it with one call:\n  ```bash\n  python3 ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/run-autopilot/scripts/statectl.py <state.json> set-contract-card docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/contract-card.md\n  ```\n  Keep it a few lines: `step: <phase/step> | invariants: <the 2-3 that bind right now> | next: <the next gate + its precondition>`. **Never pass the card as an inline shell argument** — it carries quotes, newlines and `$`, and the inline `set contract_card '<string>'` form failed three consecutive times on quoting in a real build session (2026-08-02, engram session 6).\n- **Interactive (no state.json):** the file write above is the whole step; skip the statectl call.\n\nThe session brief (`write-brief`, § Session handoff procedure step 2) is rendered right after this call so it carries the card just loaded; `/autopilot:work`'s task-boundary handoff and `/autopilot:review-work-completion`'s cycle transition write the brief the same way where they write their cards.\n\n`reinject_contract_card.py` is a SessionStart hook **matched to `compact` only** (startup/resume/clear stay unmatched, so there is no standing token cost); after a compaction it reads the card back and re-injects it as additionalContext. `/autopilot:work` (task-boundary transitions) and `/autopilot:review-work-completion` (cycle transitions) write their own cards the same way — `contract_card` in `references/state-schema.md`.\n\n### Loop Detection\n\nThe `autoclaude` wrapper exports `_AUTOPILOT_LOOP=$$` before launching each headless session. Skills branch on it for loop-mode behavior only — the `AskUserQuestion` ban (Error Handling), git-push deferral, notify suppression in `~/.claude/hooks/notify.py`. Hand-off sites do NOT check it: the state writes are the same in and out of the loop.\n\n**Review-file gate (in-session quality gate).** `review_coverage_hook.py` stays registered on Stop: at the done hand-off, when the saved review file is missing or fails the `cli/gate.py` shape check (missing reviewer section, verdict, or tests line — PRD 00016, absorbed into the CLI as `autopilot gate` by PRD 00107), it exit-2-blocks the turn's end and feeds the gap back to the model so the review can be finished before the turn ends. Exit-2 Stop-hook blocking works in `-p` mode (`references/design-rationale.md` § Review-file gate). This is a completeness gate on review artifacts, not loop orchestration.\n\n**State-write gate (in-session integrity gate).** `statectl` is the normal writer and cannot produce an invalid `state.json`, but a stray hand-edit still can, so `validate_state_json_hook.py` stays registered on PostToolUse for the `Edit`/`Write`/`MultiEdit` matchers: any such mutation that leaves the file unparseable exit-2-feeds the parse error back to the model immediately, while the session can still rewrite it. Without it, the corruption surfaces only at session exit, where the wrapper's `jq` check misreads a healthy PAUSE as `died` (state file unreadable) and halts the loop (harness-tag bleed appended `</content>` to a hand-edited write, ddb 2026-07-16).\n\n**Loop sketch** (the real driver — `cli/loop.py`, invoked as `autopilot loop` — adds the memory circuit-breaker, the session wall-clock cap, orphan cleanup, metrics, notifications, and the operator-view renderer — `scripts/render_stream.py` turns the stream-json terminal output into one-line summaries while `last-session.log` keeps the raw events; the bash `autoclaude` wrapper keeps only the operator subcommands, the tracon presentation front-end, and the hand-off):\n\n```\nautoclaude                 # bash: subcommands + tracon front-end, then\n  -> autopilot loop        # cli/loop.py, the driver:\n       while True:\n           pause marker?  -> consume it, notify, stop\n           spawn the routed `claude -p \"/autopilot:run-autopilot\"` (raw events kept in last-session.log)\n           read state.json, branch per the decision table above (0-5)\n```\n\n## Phase 0 invariants (selection — full procedure: `references/phase-build.md`)\n\nTest-pinned invariants the build gate's Phase 0 references; they live here so every session carries them.\n\n`autopilot enter` is the first Bash call of a build session: it runs this whole step chain and prints one JSON line (`references/phase-build.md` § \"Enter in one call\").\n\n**Lifecycle directories first.** Before the abort handlers and before PRD selection, as its own Bash call:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p docs/dev/project-management/prds/backlog docs/dev/project-management/prds/wip docs/dev/project-management/prds/done docs/dev/project-management/prds/hold docs/dev/project-management/reviews docs/dev/tmp docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/reports docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/deferred\n```\n\nIdempotent, safe on every invocation. `mkdir` before any move is mandatory: a move into a missing destination silently misplaces the PRD (`references/design-rationale.md` § Verified moves).\n\nThen, right after that `mkdir -p` and as its own Bash call, run `autopilot ensure-store`: `python3 ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/run-autopilot/cli/__main__.py ensure-store --state <state.json>`. It writes `docs/dev/project-management/.gitignore`, the machine form of the § Retention Disposable list, whenever that file is missing or differs from the list; it is idempotent, exits 0 either way, and needs no `state.json` to exist yet. The file is written by code, never with the Write or Edit tool: `hooks/enforce_prd_location.py` rejects a top-level store file outside its keeper list for editor tools, and that refusal is correct and stays. The patterns themselves live once, as `STORE_GITIGNORE` in `cli/store_tree.py`, and a test holds them against § Retention. `autopilot enter` runs this step itself.\n\n**Verified moves.** Every lifecycle `mv` (backlog→`wip/` at selection, and equally wip→done / wip→hold at the other gates) is immediately followed by a verification: confirm the moved PRD now exists in the destination directory. If it does not, the `mv` failed — set `state.phase = \"paused\"` and `state.next_phase = \"paused\"`, write `state.pause_reason = {\"site\": \"mv_verify\", \"detail\": \"<source, destination, and the mv error>\"}`, and PAUSE naming the source, the destination, and the `mv` error; do not continue past a failed move.\n\n**Batch-identity rollover.** When `state.batch` already exists at selection, mint a fresh `batch.id` (new `<yyyymmddHHMM>` timestamp, reset `completed_prds: []`) ONLY for a *genuinely closed* surviving batch: `phase == \"done\"` AND `next_phase == \"\"` (empty). Both conditions are required — only the batch-end \"No more PRDs\" branch writes the empty `next_phase`, while Phase 9 step 2 sets a transient `phase: \"done\"` (with `next_phase: \"done\"`) BEFORE the verified wip→done move, so a failed move or mid-Phase-9 crash leaves that shape and must NOT roll over (rolling over there would wipe the in-progress batch's `completed_prds` and mint a spurious id). Every normal in-progress resume preserves `batch.id` unchanged. (Forensics: `references/design-rationale.md` § Batch-identity rollover.)\n\nOn that closed-batch rollover, delete `batch.unavailable_reviewers`,\n`batch.unavailable_reviewer_details` and `batch.minted_stubs` (so the next\nbatch's `{s} stubs` count starts empty, PRD 00195) in the same state update as\nthe new id.\nInteractive batch end retains `state.json`, so expiry cannot depend on file\ndeletion. Every in-progress resume preserves both fields, including transitions\nto another PRD in the same batch; only a new batch tries a rejected Carl again.\n\n## Phase 3 invariants (work — full procedure: `references/phase-build.md`)\n\n**`work_start_sha` is captured once per PRD.** Run `git rev-parse HEAD` and write it to `state.work_start_sha` before dispatching `/autopilot:work`, but only if it is unset for the current PRD (`state.work_start_sha` absent or empty). If it is already set, **do NOT re-capture** — a cap-rotation (or any other build re-entry on resume) re-enters the build gate with pending tasks, and the existing value marks the true PRD start; re-capturing the HEAD-at-rotation would shrink the review diff (`work_start_sha..HEAD`) to post-rotation commits only. `review-work-completion` uses `work_start_sha..HEAD` as the full-review diff range, so the doubt lens sees the PRD's whole work range. Phase 9 step 10 clears the field on the PRD-to-PRD reset, so each PRD in a multi-PRD batch captures fresh and ranges never overlap.\n\n**If `git rev-parse HEAD` fails at capture** — an unborn branch or a repo with no commits yet (a *detached* HEAD resolves fine and is not this case; a bare-repo-backed root like `~/.claude` must run rev-parse in the SAME git context `repo_root` uses — the `--git-dir`/`--work-tree` flags — not a bare `git rev-parse` that would fail or resolve a parent repo): there is no base commit to diff against. Do NOT leave `work_start_sha` unset (an absent value makes the review range undefined and crashes the doubt lens). Set it to the git **empty-tree sentinel** `4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904`, log a one-line warning, and note the fallback in the batch report — the review range `4b825dc…..HEAD` then spans the whole tree once `/autopilot:work` has committed, which is the correct scope for a first-commit repo.\n\n## Phase 9 invariants (completion — full procedure: `references/phase-done.md`)\n\n**Verified finalize move.** The move of the PRD from `wip/` to `done/` keeps the `00XXX-` prefix and is verified: confirm the PRD now exists in `docs/dev/project-management/prds/done/`; on failure PAUSE per the verified-moves rule above (`site: \"mv_verify\"`) — never append to `completed_prds` or advance to the next PRD with the PRD in the wrong folder.\n\n**Report identity.** The batch report filename is built from the current `state.batch.id` — `reports/{state.batch.id}-report.md`; before appending, verify the target filename's id matches `state.batch.id`. Never glob `reports/*.md` to choose a file, and never append to a report whose id differs from `state.batch.id` — a mismatch is a batch-identity error; create a fresh `{state.batch.id}-report.md` instead.\n\n## Design-gate invariant: empty-review-log gate (Phase 1.5 — full procedure: `references/phase-build.md`)\n\nPhase 1.5 must verify the design doc's `## Review log` actually holds at least one reviewer dispatch summary line — a silently-skipped review leaves it empty, and nothing else checks that the review ran. Run the gate on this success path (after a successful `/autopilot:design-solution` run) AND on this artifact-reuse path (when an existing design doc is reused); `design_mode == \"skip\"` bypasses the empty-review-log gate entirely (no doc exists by design). Inline the design-doc path from `state.design_doc` directly into this exact section-scoped check (one `awk`, no pipe, no shell variable — assignments break permission-prefix matching; exit-code based — it counts only pinned dispatch-summary lines that appear inside the `## Review log` section, so the design doc's own example lines in `## Interfaces & contracts` cannot false-pass it):\n\n```\nawk '/^## Review log/{f=1;next} /^## /{f=0} f && /dispatch [0-9]+ \\((claude|codex|claude-fallback)\\): cardinal-sin [0-9]+, blocker [0-9]+, non-blocker [0-9]+, question [0-9]+/{hit=1} END{exit !hit}' <path-from-state.design_doc>\n```\n\n- **exit 0** (≥1 in-section dispatch summary line) → proceed to Phase 2 (planning).\n- **exit non-zero** (empty `## Review log` — the review never ran) → treat as a sub-skill failure: set `state.phase = \"paused\"` and `state.next_phase = \"paused\"`, write `state.pause_reason = {\"site\": \"sub_skill_fail\", \"detail\": \"design doc has empty ## Review log (review never ran)\"}`, and do NOT proceed to planning. Surface the remedy: delete the design doc and let Phase 1.5 regenerate it.\n\nThe check is deterministic (the pinned `awk` above), NOT a model judgment.\n\n## Shell Command Rules\n\n- Never call bash `head`, `tail`, `cat`, `grep`, or `find` - a hook blocks them. Use the Read tool (offset/limit), `rg`, or `rg --files` instead. Never pipe between heterogeneous commands and never combine an inspection (read, list, search, diff) with a test, lint or build invocation in one Bash call - run them as separate calls. Pass an explicit `timeout` on every Bash call: 60000 ms for an inspection, 300000 ms for a lint run or a narrow test run, 600000 ms for a full suite or a full build.\n- **Never chain commands** with `&&`, `|`, or `;` in a single Bash call. Use separate Bash tool calls instead.\n- **Never use redirections** like `2>/dev/null`. Handle missing files by checking existence or catching errors in the tool result.\n- Use `Read` for file contents; for existence and listing checks Bash `ls` is fine (the native `Glob` tool is absent in this build - rules/tools.md).\n- Use `mkdir -p` in its own Bash call when creating directories.\n\n## Error Handling\n\n| Situation | Interactive | Loop mode (`$_AUTOPILOT_LOOP` set, PRD 00017) |\n|-----------|-------------|------------------------------------------------|\n| Sub-skill invocation fails outright (no usable result; the phase cannot proceed) | PAUSE, report which skill failed and error. A transient reviewer/sub-skill error *during the review-rework cycle* is the review gate's Safety Checks row's domain instead (graceful degradation, not a PAUSE). | Re-invoke the sub-skill ONCE; if it fails again, stall the PRD (`recovery.md` → \"Loop-mode stall procedure\", `site: \"sub_skill_fail\"`) and continue the batch |\n| No PRDs anywhere | STOP with message about /create-prd | Run `autopilot phase-done --outcome drained` first (it writes `phase: \"done\"` and the empty `next_phase`) so the wrapper stops as drained, not died. **If `state.json` does not exist yet** — a fresh loop pointed at an already-empty backlog — `phase-done` exits 2 and the wrapper reads that as *died*; run `autopilot init --prd \"\"` first, then `phase-done --outcome drained`. |\n| State file corrupted | Delete it, restart from Phase 0 | Same (in-session recovery, no pause) |\n| `statectl` state-write fails (lock contention, python error, unreadable state) | Retry once; still failing → `autopilot restore` and retry once more; still failing → write the `state-write-failed` marker (`site: \"statectl_fail\"`) with the raw stderr in `detail`, end the turn, and also report the failure to the user directly — never proceed on the stale state | Retry once; still failing → `autopilot restore` and retry once more; still failing → write the `state-write-failed` marker (`site: \"statectl_fail\"`) with the raw stderr in `detail`, end the turn — the wrapper halts on it and surfaces the detail |\n| Review produces no parseable output | PAUSE, report — don't retry | Re-run the review ONCE; still unparseable → stall the PRD (`site: \"reviewer_fail\"`), continue the batch |\n| All reviewers fail | PAUSE, report — partial results usable if user confirms | Re-invoke ONCE; still nothing → stall the PRD (`site: \"reviewer_fail\"`), continue the batch |\n| `docs/dev/project-management/` doesn't exist | Create it | Same |\n| Task tools unavailable | STOP, report — can't operate without tasks | Same (a broken harness is not a per-PRD failure) |\n| Git push fails (auth, locked signing agent, network) | Report and let the user retry. A push denied by hooks/guard_push_on_critical.py names a pending cap_critical custody: resolve it with autopilot custody resolve, never bypass the hook. | Log to `deferred_decisions[]`, leave the commits local (the user pushes manually per Phase 9), CONTINUE — a locked signing agent on an unattended host is expected (`references/design-rationale.md` § Git push failures). A guard denial is closed at the next attended Phase 0 (`references/phase-build.md` § Handle pending custody), where the operator picks revert, branch-and-revert or accept; loop mode leaves the commits local and continues without any git mutation. |\n| `mv` verify fails (backlog→wip, wip→done, wip→hold) | PAUSE per the mv-verify sites | Retry the `mv` ONCE after re-running `mkdir -p`; persistent failure is a sanctioned batch halt (row 3 of the sanctioned batch-halt list below) |\n| **Security-critical finding** (exposed secret, vulnerability being shipped) | PAUSE | **PAUSE — sanctioned batch halt, row 1** (set `phase: \"paused\"` + `pause_reason`; the wrapper notifies and halts) |\n| **Detected data-loss risk** | PAUSE | **PAUSE — sanctioned batch halt, row 2** (same mechanics) |\n\n**Loop mode never halts the whole batch for a per-PRD decision.** After PRD 00066 the loop-mode turn-ending *decision*-PAUSE list is empty: a blocking escalation and the Phase 1.5 design gate STALL the PRD (`references/recovery.md` Loop-mode stall procedure), a scope alarm DEFERS-and-continues, and a Phase 2 ambiguity is resolved by the simplest safe assumption (or stalls under `pause_on_ambiguity: true`) — none write `phase: \"paused\"`. Everything else stalls the PRD or defers and continues. The ONLY conditions that stop the whole batch (a loud halt, not a per-PRD park/stall) are the sanctioned batch-halt list below. Future edits must not re-grow the loop-mode decision-PAUSE list, nor add a halt outside that list.\n\n**Sanctioned batch-halt list (the complete set of whole-batch loud stops):**\n\n1. **Security-critical finding** — exposed secret or a vulnerability being shipped (including a cap_critical custody capture that fails twice) (skill PAUSE; `pause_reason` set by the Error Handling row above).\n2. **Detected data-loss risk** (skill PAUSE).\n3. **`mv`-verify exhaustion** — a lifecycle move that keeps failing after the retry (skill PAUSE, `mv_verify`).\n4. **Sustained memory pressure** (wrapper memory circuit-breaker).\n5. **Usage-limit beyond `_AUTOPILOT_LIMIT_WAIT_MAX`** (wrapper `died`).\n6. **Net-retries exhausted** (wrapper `died`).\n7. **Operator pause** — the `pause-requested` marker (wrapper stop).\n8. **Died bootstrap session** — a session died before any PRD was selected, so there is nothing to park (wrapper `died`).\n9. **Systemic-park breaker** — 2 consecutive `wrapper_died` parks with nothing healthy between (skill PAUSE, `systemic_park`; Phase 0 park handler, `references/phase-build.md`).\n10. **Unwritable or unconsumed park marker** — the park hand-off itself is broken: the wrapper cannot write the `park-requested` marker (the write-failure guard in the `park)` else branch, so it never hands off an empty marker) OR a written marker is never consumed (the park-loop guard). Either is a wrapper loud halt.\n11. **Plugin version drift** (PRD 00086 R3) — the loop's plugin-pin preflight (`plugin_drift` in `cli/loop.py`) found an enforcement plugin (aegis/warden) whose installed version differs from the `state.batch.plugin_versions` pin recorded at batch selection: the batch's enforcement code auto-updated mid-run, so the wrapper halts loud with state intact rather than run a session on unpinned enforcement code.\n\nRows 1–8 are the PRD 00066 Success-Metrics list; rows 9–10 are the two systemic backstops the PRD Risks section mandates (adopted at the design gate, 2026-07-18); row 11 is the plugin-pin preflight (PRD 00086 R3). Nothing outside these eleven stops the batch — Success Metric 2 is read as \"no UNSANCTIONED halts\".\n\n**Turn-ending PAUSE rows must set `state.phase = \"paused\"` (and `state.next_phase = \"paused\"`) before stopping, and must also write `state.pause_reason = {\"site\": \"<slug>\", \"detail\": \"<one-line human string>\"}`.** `pause_reason` is a durable marker so the loop halts even if the model forgets `phase=\"paused\"`; unlike `phase` it is not overwritten by normal progression, so it must be cleared on resume (see `### Resuming` cleanup). Without it the wrapper — seeing a non-empty `next_phase` — would take its continue branch and relaunch the failed phase instead of stopping for you to intervene; a paused state is the wrapper's stop-and-notify branch (Session Loop branch 1), and the wrapper surfaces `pause_reason.detail` in its notification. This applies to \"Sub-skill invocation fails outright\" (`pause_reason.site = \"sub_skill_fail\"`), \"Review produces no parseable output\" (`\"reviewer_fail\"`), \"All three reviewers fail\" (`\"reviewer_fail\"`), and \"statectl state-write fails\" (`\"statectl_fail\"`). Exceptions that need no `phase` change: \"State file corrupted\" (delete it and restart from Phase 0 in the same session; the freshly-written state is what the wrapper reads at turn end) and \"No PRDs anywhere\" (see its row — the drained state write covers the loop). PAUSE sites that ask via `AskUserQuestion` mid-turn (Phase 1.5 design gate, Phase 2 clarification, Phase 5 blocking escalation and scope alarm) do NOT end the turn and need no `phase` change — **but only outside the loop. When `$_AUTOPILOT_LOOP` is set there is no human to answer, and (post-00066) none of these pause the batch: each takes its per-PRD path instead. The design gate and a blocking escalation run the Loop-mode stall procedure (`references/recovery.md`; `site: \"design_gate\"` recording the design doc path, `site: \"blocking_escalation\"` recording the blocking issue in the deferred JSON `detail`); a scope alarm defers-and-continues (a `scope-overflow` deferred record — the top-10 fixes proceed); a Phase 2 ambiguity is resolved by the simplest safe assumption, or stalls (`site: \"clarification\"`) under `pause_on_ambiguity: true`. None writes `phase: \"paused\"` — the batch keeps draining and a human resolves the parked PRD on the next manual `/autopilot:run-autopilot` (see `references/decision-framework.md` → \"Autonomy in loop mode\"). A mid-turn question on the unattended path has stranded the loop for hours (`references/design-rationale.md` § No mid-turn questions).**\n\n## Reference Files\n\n- `references/phase-build.md` — build gate (selection/aborts, catchup, design, planning, work)\n- `references/phase-review.md` — review gate (lenses, decision gate, cap, rework)\n- `references/phase-done.md` — done gate (completion, continuation, batch-end review)\n- `references/state-schema.md` — state file JSON schema and skip logic\n- `references/decision-framework.md` — auto-fix vs escalate classification rules\n- `references/recovery.md` — rare-path handlers (abort/replan, stalls, escalation-exhausted, cap-pause resume)\n- `references/design-rationale.md` — incident history behind the rules (non-normative)\n- `references/dashboard-format.md` — live dashboard via tracon (render_stream fallback; plain text via `autopilot status`)\n- `references/batch-report-format.md` — batch report sources and semantics (render: `autopilot render report`)\n- `references/audit-log-format.md` — audit label set and decisions.md projection (render: `autopilot render audit`)\n- `references/doubt-review-rubric.md` — Bob's doubt-lens rubric (D1-D5)\n- `references/waves.md` — the `autopilot wave` verbs, what a lane worktree holds, and what is deferred\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":3084}},"started_at_ms":1791625279316,"completed_at_ms":1791625279316}}
+{"timestamp":"2026-10-10T09:41:19.319Z","ordinal":21,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a12530-3757-7491-91ea-74f9ef629b8c","call_id":"call_867dffd07638478ab7b395dba4fd66c2","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 19077)\nTotal output lines: 1\n\n{\"chunk_id\":\"a9f4a8\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000003084,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":18864,\"output\":\"# AI assistant instructions\\n\\n- Solo developer. Be extremely concise, skip formalities.\\n- Simplest safe assumption when ambiguity isn't material.\\n- This file (`~/.claude/AGENTS.md`) is the single source of user-global agent instructions. `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` is only an `@AGENTS.md` pointer and `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` symlinks here. Edit this file, never the pointers.\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n- After completing all PRD tasks, run `/autopilot:review-work-completion` in a fresh session - never from the work session (hand off first; see the review-phase rule below).\\n- For end-to-end PRD execution, use `/autopilot:run-autopilot`.\\n- Procedures mandated by an invoked skill count as explicitly requested: ponytail (and any other minimalism mode) must never thin, skip, or down-scope skill-mandated steps. Minimalism governs what you build, not whether you follow the skill.\\n- Never skip or thin the review phase of `/autopilot:run-autopilot`: every review cycle runs all lenses - consensus (Alice), blind/PRD-only (Blake), doubt+de-slop with rubric R1-R5 (Bob) - regardless of how small, simple, or well-specified the PRD looks. Do not ask whether the review is worth it - the answer is always yes. The worst issues land precisely when the code feels obviously correct. Reviewers get isolated contexts by construction (subagent prompts, external CLIs); the review phase itself runs in a fresh session after the build hand-off (end the turn: set `state.next_phase`, print the banner, STOP; in loop mode the headless session exits and the `autoclaude` wrapper relaunches from `state.json`). Never invoke `/autopilot:review-work-completion` from the work session, even if context budget appears sufficient. Scope (decided 2026-09-14, discovery 00203): this rule governs the full lane and every fast-track roster. Lane routing is decided by code (`cli/lane.py`, autopilot PRD 00204), never by an agent's judgment; the solo lane's one-pass zero-context review of docs/test-only PRDs, with code-decided escalation to the full gate on any production or security-ish diff, a CRITICAL or a surviving HIGH (PRD 00205), is the user's design, not an agent thinning the review.\\n- After completing work, clean up stale worktrees, orphan branches, temp files, and executed plan files in the repo's `docs/dev/project-management/plans/`.\\n- Meta-budget: Claude-on-Claude work (the `~/.claude` project) targets <= 30% of monthly spend; the portfolio brief surfaces the share monthly (red above ceiling). Released-plugin repo work counts as product, not meta.\\n- If a secret may have been exposed, rotate it.\\n\\n## Compaction\\n\\n- After failed approach: compact\\n- Mid-implementation: don't compact\\n- After completing a PRD, before next: compact\\n\\nWith 1M context, research stays in-flight through implementation.\\n\\n## Planning\\n\\n- End plans with unresolved questions.\\n- One question at a time, concise, with enough context to answer quickly.\\n\\n## Toolchain\\n\\n- CLI tools and language runtimes are managed with **mise**. Installs live under `~/.local/share/mise/installs/<tool>/<version>/`; shims at `~/.local/share/mise/shims/`.\\n- Global `npm ls -g`, `/opt/homebrew/bin`, `/usr/local/bin` will NOT list mise-managed tools - don't conclude a tool is missing from those. Locate one with `mise which <tool>`; run one not on PATH via `mise exec -- <tool>`.\\n- Not every install gets a shim - `mise which` can resolve a tool even when `command -v` fails. If a tool is missing from PATH, suggest `mise reshim`.\\n- The default model carries `[1m]` (1M window). The autopilot context cap (`autopilot_context_cap_hook.USAGE_CAP` in the autopilot plugin's `skills/run-autopilot/scripts/`, mirrored in `tracon/model.py` beside it) is 500K, coupled to that 1M default: never drop it toward 150K unless the default window also shrinks below 200K, or the cap misfires at ~163K aborting healthy build sessions (PRD 00073; 150K pin reverted 2026-07-20 when `[1m]` was restored).\\n- Before launching a `~/.claude` autopilot batch, `export _AUTOPILOT_WRITE_SCOPE_EXTRA=\\\"$HOME/.config/bash:$HOME/.codex:$HOME/.local/bin\\\"` - the buvis repo spans those roots and the write-scope fence (`hooks/enforce_write_scope.py`, armed by `CLAUDE_UNATTENDED=1`) denies them otherwise; `_AUTOPILOT_WRITE_SCOPE=off` disarms the fence for one batch.\\n\\n## Conventions\\n\\n- Repos live at `~/git/src/github.com/{org}/{repo}` (Go-style layout).\\n- Name tools, plugins, and projects with evocative single words (warden, strunk) over descriptive concatenations; offer 3-4 candidates with rationale.\\n- When a standing rule already mandates a change discovered mid-task, produce the reversible artifact instead of asking; flag user-facing impact in the summary.\\n- **Skills live in `~/git/src/github.com/buvis/agent-skills`, not in buvis** (since 2026-08-25): edit and commit there, then `braid --check`; never edit through the link farm or stage a skill path into buvis. Layout, hops and policy: `rules/memory.md` and `~/.agents/README.md`.\\n- Kept-but-dormant material lives in `~/.claude/.archive/`: `skills/` (parked skills, not scanned, so they cost no boot context - `mv` one back into `~/.claude/skills/` to reactivate it) and `rules/` (retired rule text). `rules-library/` is NOT an archive: `rationalizations.md` is read at runtime by `hooks/cartographer-echo.py`.\\n- Native Bash grants in `settings.json` are deliberately broad (`git`, `rmdir`, `docker`, `kubectl` at `:*`), and so are the `tmp` Edit wildcards. `rm` is NOT granted natively - warden gates every `rm`. **Warden is the gate**, not the native allowlist: it filters every Bash command and asks or denies on its own rules. Config audits will keep flagging these as broad grants - that verdict is accepted, decided 2026-08-02. Do not re-narrow them audit by audit. Revisit only if warden stops gating Bash.\\n\\n## Dotfiles repo\\n\\n- Tracked in a bare repo at `~/.buvis`, work-tree is `$HOME`. Run git as `git --git-dir=~/.buvis --work-tree=~ <cmd>` (no shell alias assumed).\\n- **This repo answers \\\"not tracked\\\" by staying silent. That has already caused a wrong conclusion.** Treat ANY empty output from `~/.buvis` as unverified until a known-tracked control path proves the command shape works.\\n  - The index IS populated and matches HEAD (verified 2026-08-17: `ls-files` and `ls-tree -r --name-only HEAD` both return 641 entries), so `ls-files` is a valid tracking check. Still prefer `git diff HEAD` over plain `git diff`: the index is shared with the user's live sessions, so anything they staged is invisible to an index-vs-worktree diff.\\n  - Pathspecs resolve against **cwd**, not the work-tree. From anywhere but `~`, `-- .claude/foo` silently searches `<cwd>/.claude/foo` and finds nothing. Anchor every pathspec with `:(top)`, e.g. `add ':(top).claude/rules/foo.md'`. `git log` and `show --stat` keep working regardless, which makes the repo look fine while the path looks absent.\\n- The `rules/changelog.md` mandate does **not** apply here: no releases, no CHANGELOG. Commit `feat`/`fix` directly without a CHANGELOG entry, and don't add a CHANGELOG.md to this repo.\\n# AGENTS.md — OVCAQ\\n\\nOVCAQ means **Orchestration, Visibility, and Control for Agent Queues**. It is\\nan installable Python CLI/TUI that supervises a PRD-driven workflow through\\nClaude Code, Codex, or another headless coding agent.\\n\\nThe global `AGENTS.md` remains in force. This file adds project-specific rules.\\n\\n## Current state\\n\\nThe repository is in planning/bootstrap. Until an implementation PRD replaces\\nit, `docs/dev/project-management/plans/implementation-plan.md` is the architecture and sequencing\\nauthority. Do not create production modules opportunistically outside that\\nsequence.\\n\\n## Product boundary\\n\\nOVCAQ core owns state, lifecycle commands, process supervision, agent adapters,\\nnormalized events, observability, the TUI, and launcher provisioning checks.\\n\\nOVCAQ core does **not** contain launcher-specific skills, agents, hooks, or\\nplugins. Those live in external marketplaces. Core may carry declarative\\nidentifiers and version requirements needed to reconcile them.\\n\\n## Architecture invariants\\n\\n1. **One state writer.** Every durable `state.json` mutation goes through the\\n   state store. Agents mutate state only through `ovcaq state` or a higher-level\\n   OVCAQ command. Never instruct an agent to edit `state.json` directly.\\n2. **State decides; prose does not.** Session completion, phase handoff, pause,\\n   drain, retry, and failure decisions derive from committed state plus a\\n   normalized session result. A final agent message is never authoritative.\\n3. **Provider code stays at the edge.** Claude/Codex argv, environment variables,\\n   event formats, model names, rate-limit parsing, and connectivity checks live\\n   under their adapter. Shared runtime and UI code must not parse provider-native\\n   events.\\n4. **Raw evidence survives normalization.** Preserve each session's raw output.\\n   Normalizers are tolerant and must degrade to an unknown/raw observation\\n   instead of crashing the supervisor.\\n5. **The TUI observes shared models.** It reads state, registry, metrics, and\\n   normalized observations. It does not implement lifecycle policy or maintain a\\n   second liveness algorithm.\\n6. **External capabilities stay external.** Provisioning invokes a launcher's\\n   supported marketplace/plugin CLI and verifies the result. It does not copy\\n   plugin contents into this package or hand-edit launcher caches.\\n7. **No shell evaluation of configured commands.** Store agent commands as argv\\n   arrays and spawn them without `shell=True`.\\n\\n## Target layout\\n\\n```text\\nsrc/ovcaq/\\n  state/        # schema, transactions, compound mutations\\n  workflow/     # PRD selection, transitions, records, policy\\n  runtime/      # supervisor, decisions, registry, process ownership\\n  drivers/      # Claude, Codex, and generic command adapters\\n  events/       # normalized event model and session reduction\\n  tui/          # discovery and Textual/Rich presentation\\n  provision/    # marketplace/plugin reconciliation\\ntests/          # mirrors package boundaries; provider JSONL fixtures\\ndocs/           # tracked architecture and operator documentation\\n```\\n\\n## Toolchain\\n\\n- Python 3.12+ managed through `mise`.\\n- `uv` for environments, locking, build, and command execution.\\n- `pytest` for tests; `ruff` for formatting/linting; `mypy` for package types.\\n- Keep the runtime dependency set small. Textual/Rich are justified by the TUI;\\n  prefer the standard library for state, locking, configuration, and subprocesses.\\n\\nPlanned verification commands once the scaffold lands:\\n\\n```bash\\nuv sync --all-groups\\nuv run pytest\\nuv run ruff check .\\nuv run ruff format --check .\\nuv run mypy src/ovcaq\\nuv build\\n```\\n\\n## Development rules\\n\\n- Characterize current behavior before moving it from `~/.claude`.\\n- Extract state first, then pure workflow logic, runtime, adapters, TUI, and\\n  provisioning in that order.\\n- Sanitized Claude and Codex JSONL fixtures belong in `tests/fixtures/`; tests\\n  never read live files from `~/.claude` or `~/.codex`.\\n- Test process-group, lock, and signal behavior with real subprocesses in\\n  addition to unit tests.\\n- Documentation for commands comes from the same command registry/parser as the\\n  implementation; do not maintain a second hand-written verb inventory.\\n- Keep provider-neutral modules free of the strings `claude`, `anthropic`,\\n  `codex`, and provider model identifiers except in tests that enforce this rule.\\n- Use conventional commits. No compatibility shim, dual writer, or rollback\\n  framework is required for the initial cutover.\\n\\n## Migration stance\\n\\nThis is a one-way early-stage extraction. Start a fresh OVCAQ batch/state tree;\\ndo not build an importer for the current `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` unless\\na later requirement explicitly needs it. Temporary delegation during a local\\ncanary is acceptable, but do not ship `autoclaude`, `autopilot`, `tracon`, or\\n`statectl` aliases as permanent public APIs.\\n---\\nname: run-autopilot\\ndescription: Use when running a PRD end-to-end autonomously - catchup, plan, work, and a review-rework loop running consensus, blind, and doubt lenses every cycle. Triggers on \\\"autopilot\\\", \\\"run autopilot\\\", \\\"autopilot status\\\", \\\"drain backlog\\\".\\nargument-hint: \\\"[<prd-filename> | status]\\\"\\ncompatibility: \\\"Requires Bob personal Claude/autoclaude environment or equivalent host adapters for sub-agents, state, and review handoffs.\\\"\\n---\\n\\n> **Paths in this pack.** This pack's root is `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` - that line\\n> is substituted when this skill loads, so what you just read is the real\\n> directory. Files under `references/` are read at runtime and are **not**\\n> substituted: when one shows `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/...`, swap in the root above\\n> before running anything. Never pass the literal placeholder to a shell - it\\n> expands to the empty string and the path silently becomes `/...`.\\n\\n> **Role effort:** main sessions default to low coordinator effort. This does\\n> not reduce worker or reviewer effort. Every native Claude task dispatch uses\\n> `autopilot:worker-<tier>` with the full rendered persona and selected model:\\n> haiku/low, sonnet/medium, opus/high, human-authorized fable/xhigh. Named\\n> Alice/Blake reviewers run at high; other native reviewers use the high-effort\\n> `autopilot:worker-opus` wrapper with their selected model. External Claude\\n> reviews pass `sonnet-run.sh --effort high`. Preserve all review gates, lenses,\\n> isolation, and escalation rules. Other provider hosts use their host mapping.\\n\\n# Autopilot\\n\\nOrchestrate the full PRD lifecycle: catchup → design → plan-tasks → work → review-rework loop (consensus + blind + doubt lenses, every cycle) → done.\\n\\nMakes autonomous decisions backed by research (dependencies, recurring issues, API/schema changes when PRD-driven) and pauses only for critical security, requirements ambiguity, or blocking decisions.\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n- Pack skills (invoked as phases): `design-solution`, `plan-tasks`, `work`,\\n  `review-work-completion`\\n- Plugin skills: `git-ferry:catchup` (Phase 1, unless the PRD sets `catchup: skip`)\\n- Files read from other skill dirs:\\n  `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/review-work-completion/scripts/await_reviewer_outputs.py`\\n  (Watcher keep-alive)\\n- Hooks outside the skill dir: `~/.claude/hooks/notify.py`\\n- Shell wrapper outside `~/.claude`: `autoclaude` in\\n  `~/.config/bash/plugins/development.plugin.bash` - operator subcommands +\\n  tracon front-end; it hands off to `autopilot loop` (`cli/loop.py`), the\\n  unattended session loop since PRD 00106\\n- Scripts (own dir, model-invoked as a CLI): `scripts/statectl.py` — the sole\\n  `state.json` mutator (`get`/`set`/`append`/`del`; atomic, advisory-locked,\\n  one rotating `.bak`)\\n- CLIs: `claude` (headless `-p`), `python3`, `git`, `awk`\\n- Optional: `tracon` (`scripts/tracon/`; the default `autoclaude` front-end since PRD 00062, `_AUTOPILOT_TRACON=0` or no tty/`uv` falls back to `render_stream.py`) - dashboard only, absence changes nothing\\n\\n## Execution Model\\n\\n**Run all phases in sequence without stopping.** After each phase completes, immediately update state and proceed to the next phase. Do not pause between phases, do not summarize progress, do not wait for user input - unless the phase explicitly says PAUSE or STOP. Completing a sub-skill invocation (`/git-ferry:catchup`, `/autopilot:plan-tasks`, `/autopilot:work`, `/autopilot:review-work-completion`, etc.) is NOT a stopping point. It is an intermediate step. Continue.\\n\\nReview sessions run on opus at xhigh in cycle 1 and at high on cycle 2 and later; _AUTOPILOT_EFFORT_REVIEW forces one effort for every cycle and _AUTOPILOT_EFFORT_REVIEW_RERUN sets the rerun value. A rework resume (the cycle's review file exists and `rework_task_ids` names an unfinished task) runs on the queued tasks' own tier instead, sonnet unless one is opus or fable (`references/model-ladder.md` § Review sessions, PRD 00207).\\n\\n## Entry Points\\n\\n- `/autopilot:run-autopilot` — auto-select PRD (wip first, then backlog), run full cycle\\n- `/autopilot:run-autopilot <prd-filename>` — full cycle with specific PRD\\n- `/autopilot:run-autopilot status` — print current dashboard, no action\\n- `/autopilot:run-autopilot review-batch` — interactively review a finished batch's deferred items\\n\\nIf invoked with `status`, read `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json`, print phase/cycle/task summary, and stop.\\n\\nIf invoked with `review-batch`, load the newest `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/deferred/{batch_id}-deferred.json` and run the \\\"Batch-End Review\\\" presentation (`references/phase-done.md`) against it — chunked by PRD, wait for user decisions, execute \\\"fix now\\\" items. No state changes; the batch is already closed. Stop when all chunks are reviewed (or the user says stop).\\n\\nPreflight for a batch rooted at `~/.claude`: the launching shell must `export _AUTOPILOT_WRITE_SCOPE_EXTRA=\\\"$HOME/.config/bash:$HOME/.codex:$HOME/.local/bin\\\"`, or the write-scope fence (`hooks/enforce_write_scope.py`) false-denies edits under those roots and the task parks; `_AUTOPILOT_WRITE_SCOPE=off` disarms the fence for the batch.\\n\\n## Gate Dispatch\\n\\nThe full per-phase instructions live in three gate reference files; this core deliberately carries only the shared contract plus the invariants below. After the entry-point checks, read `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` and **Read the matching gate file now; do not execute a gate from memory**:\\n\\nA state carrying `stall_op` runs `autopilot park` to reconcile before any gate file is read.\\n\\n| resume state | Read this gate file |\\n|--------------|---------------------|\\n| `phase: \\\"build\\\"` — or `state.json` missing (fresh start) | `references/phase-build.md` |\\n| `phase: \\\"review\\\"` — or a legacy `\\\"blind\\\"`/`\\\"doubt\\\"` value (pre-00015 state), which maps to `review` | `references/phase-review.md` |\\n| `phase: \\\"done\\\"` | `references/phase-done.md` |\\n| `phase: \\\"paused\\\"` — or `stall_reason` set | `references/phase-build.md` (its Phase 0 abort/pause handlers own this resume) |\\n\\n`status` and `review-batch` invocations do not dispatch a gate (see Entry Points).\\n\\n## State Management\\n\\nAll autopilot artifacts live under `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/`, organized by type:\\n\\n```\\ndocs/dev/project-management/autopilot/\\n  state.json                              # current cycle state\\n  last-session.log                        # loop-teed raw output of the last headless session\\n  reports/{batch_id}-report.md            # batch audit report\\n  deferred/{batch_id}-deferred.json       # unresolved items across PRDs\\n```\\n\\nState file: `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` — see `references/state-schema.md` for schema.\\n\\n**`statectl` is the sole writer of `state.json`.** Apply every field mutation with `python3 ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/run-autopilot/scripts/statectl.py <state.json> set|append|del <json-path> <json-value>` (read one field with `get`); it takes an advisory lock, backs up to `<state.json>.bak`, changes only the named path while preserving all siblings, and replaces the file atomically — so it cannot leave a half-written or unparseable state, and it never trips the \\\"not read yet\\\" / \\\"modified since read\\\" errors the editing tools hit. Since PRD 00089 it is a shim over `cli/state.transaction`, so every write is schema-validated too: a value that is malformed **for the field it targets** is rejected with exit 1 and the file left byte-unchanged (an unrelated pre-existing odd field blocks nothing). **Phase transitions are not field mutations** — use `autopilot phase-done --outcome <outcome>` (§ Session handoff procedure), never a han…9077 tokens truncated…` — adds the memory circuit-breaker, the session wall-clock cap, orphan cleanup, metrics, notifications, and the operator-view renderer — `scripts/render_stream.py` turns the stream-json terminal output into one-line summaries while `last-session.log` keeps the raw events; the bash `autoclaude` wrapper keeps only the operator subcommands, the tracon presentation front-end, and the hand-off):\\n\\n```\\nautoclaude                 # bash: subcommands + tracon front-end, then\\n  -> autopilot loop        # cli/loop.py, the driver:\\n       while True:\\n           pause marker?  -> consume it, notify, stop\\n           spawn the routed `claude -p \\\"/autopilot:run-autopilot\\\"` (raw events kept in last-session.log)\\n           read state.json, branch per the decision table above (0-5)\\n```\\n\\n## Phase 0 invariants (selection — full procedure: `references/phase-build.md`)\\n\\nTest-pinned invariants the build gate's Phase 0 references; they live here so every session carries them.\\n\\n`autopilot enter` is the first Bash call of a build session: it runs this whole step chain and prints one JSON line (`references/phase-build.md` § \\\"Enter in one call\\\").\\n\\n**Lifecycle directories first.** Before the abort handlers and before PRD selection, as its own Bash call:\\n\\n```bash\\nmkdir -p docs/dev/project-management/prds/backlog docs/dev/project-management/prds/wip docs/dev/project-management/prds/done docs/dev/project-management/prds/hold docs/dev/project-management/reviews docs/dev/tmp docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/reports docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/deferred\\n```\\n\\nIdempotent, safe on every invocation. `mkdir` before any move is mandatory: a move into a missing destination silently misplaces the PRD (`references/design-rationale.md` § Verified moves).\\n\\nThen, right after that `mkdir -p` and as its own Bash call, run `autopilot ensure-store`: `python3 ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/run-autopilot/cli/__main__.py ensure-store --state <state.json>`. It writes `docs/dev/project-management/.gitignore`, the machine form of the § Retention Disposable list, whenever that file is missing or differs from the list; it is idempotent, exits 0 either way, and needs no `state.json` to exist yet. The file is written by code, never with the Write or Edit tool: `hooks/enforce_prd_location.py` rejects a top-level store file outside its keeper list for editor tools, and that refusal is correct and stays. The patterns themselves live once, as `STORE_GITIGNORE` in `cli/store_tree.py`, and a test holds them against § Retention. `autopilot enter` runs this step itself.\\n\\n**Verified moves.** Every lifecycle `mv` (backlog→`wip/` at selection, and equally wip→done / wip→hold at the other gates) is immediately followed by a verification: confirm the moved PRD now exists in the destination directory. If it does not, the `mv` failed — set `state.phase = \\\"paused\\\"` and `state.next_phase = \\\"paused\\\"`, write `state.pause_reason = {\\\"site\\\": \\\"mv_verify\\\", \\\"detail\\\": \\\"<source, destination, and the mv error>\\\"}`, and PAUSE naming the source, the destination, and the `mv` error; do not continue past a failed move.\\n\\n**Batch-identity rollover.** When `state.batch` already exists at selection, mint a fresh `batch.id` (new `<yyyymmddHHMM>` timestamp, reset `completed_prds: []`) ONLY for a *genuinely closed* surviving batch: `phase == \\\"done\\\"` AND `next_phase == \\\"\\\"` (empty). Both conditions are required — only the batch-end \\\"No more PRDs\\\" branch writes the empty `next_phase`, while Phase 9 step 2 sets a transient `phase: \\\"done\\\"` (with `next_phase: \\\"done\\\"`) BEFORE the verified wip→done move, so a failed move or mid-Phase-9 crash leaves that shape and must NOT roll over (rolling over there would wipe the in-progress batch's `completed_prds` and mint a spurious id). Every normal in-progress resume preserves `batch.id` unchanged. (Forensics: `references/design-rationale.md` § Batch-identity rollover.)\\n\\nOn that closed-batch rollover, delete `batch.unavailable_reviewers`,\\n`batch.unavailable_reviewer_details` and `batch.minted_stubs` (so the next\\nbatch's `{s} stubs` count starts empty, PRD 00195) in the same state update as\\nthe new id.\\nInteractive batch end retains `state.json`, so expiry cannot depend on file\\ndeletion. Every in-progress resume preserves both fields, including transitions\\nto another PRD in the same batch; only a new batch tries a rejected Carl again.\\n\\n## Phase 3 invariants (work — full procedure: `references/phase-build.md`)\\n\\n**`work_start_sha` is captured once per PRD.** Run `git rev-parse HEAD` and write it to `state.work_start_sha` before dispatching `/autopilot:work`, but only if it is unset for the current PRD (`state.work_start_sha` absent or empty). If it is already set, **do NOT re-capture** — a cap-rotation (or any other build re-entry on resume) re-enters the build gate with pending tasks, and the existing value marks the true PRD start; re-capturing the HEAD-at-rotation would shrink the review diff (`work_start_sha..HEAD`) to post-rotation commits only. `review-work-completion` uses `work_start_sha..HEAD` as the full-review diff range, so the doubt lens sees the PRD's whole work range. Phase 9 step 10 clears the field on the PRD-to-PRD reset, so each PRD in a multi-PRD batch captures fresh and ranges never overlap.\\n\\n**If `git rev-parse HEAD` fails at capture** — an unborn branch or a repo with no commits yet (a *detached* HEAD resolves fine and is not this case; a bare-repo-backed root like `~/.claude` must run rev-parse in the SAME git context `repo_root` uses — the `--git-dir`/`--work-tree` flags — not a bare `git rev-parse` that would fail or resolve a parent repo): there is no base commit to diff against. Do NOT leave `work_start_sha` unset (an absent value makes the review range undefined and crashes the doubt lens). Set it to the git **empty-tree sentinel** `4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904`, log a one-line warning, and note the fallback in the batch report — the review range `4b825dc…..HEAD` then spans the whole tree once `/autopilot:work` has committed, which is the correct scope for a first-commit repo.\\n\\n## Phase 9 invariants (completion — full procedure: `references/phase-done.md`)\\n\\n**Verified finalize move.** The move of the PRD from `wip/` to `done/` keeps the `00XXX-` prefix and is verified: confirm the PRD now exists in `docs/dev/project-management/prds/done/`; on failure PAUSE per the verified-moves rule above (`site: \\\"mv_verify\\\"`) — never append to `completed_prds` or advance to the next PRD with the PRD in the wrong folder.\\n\\n**Report identity.** The batch report filename is built from the current `state.batch.id` — `reports/{state.batch.id}-report.md`; before appending, verify the target filename's id matches `state.batch.id`. Never glob `reports/*.md` to choose a file, and never append to a report whose id differs from `state.batch.id` — a mismatch is a batch-identity error; create a fresh `{state.batch.id}-report.md` instead.\\n\\n## Design-gate invariant: empty-review-log gate (Phase 1.5 — full procedure: `references/phase-build.md`)\\n\\nPhase 1.5 must verify the design doc's `## Review log` actually holds at least one reviewer dispatch summary line — a silently-skipped review leaves it empty, and nothing else checks that the review ran. Run the gate on this success path (after a successful `/autopilot:design-solution` run) AND on this artifact-reuse path (when an existing design doc is reused); `design_mode == \\\"skip\\\"` bypasses the empty-review-log gate entirely (no doc exists by design). Inline the design-doc path from `state.design_doc` directly into this exact section-scoped check (one `awk`, no pipe, no shell variable — assignments break permission-prefix matching; exit-code based — it counts only pinned dispatch-summary lines that appear inside the `## Review log` section, so the design doc's own example lines in `## Interfaces & contracts` cannot false-pass it):\\n\\n```\\nawk '/^## Review log/{f=1;next} /^## /{f=0} f && /dispatch [0-9]+ \\\\((claude|codex|claude-fallback)\\\\): cardinal-sin [0-9]+, blocker [0-9]+, non-blocker [0-9]+, question [0-9]+/{hit=1} END{exit !hit}' <path-from-state.design_doc>\\n```\\n\\n- **exit 0** (≥1 in-section dispatch summary line) → proceed to Phase 2 (planning).\\n- **exit non-zero** (empty `## Review log` — the review never ran) → treat as a sub-skill failure: set `state.phase = \\\"paused\\\"` and `state.next_phase = \\\"paused\\\"`, write `state.pause_reason = {\\\"site\\\": \\\"sub_skill_fail\\\", \\\"detail\\\": \\\"design doc has empty ## Review log (review never ran)\\\"}`, and do NOT proceed to planning. Surface the remedy: delete the design doc and let Phase 1.5 regenerate it.\\n\\nThe check is deterministic (the pinned `awk` above), NOT a model judgment.\\n\\n## Shell Command Rules\\n\\n- Never call bash `head`, `tail`, `cat`, `grep`, or `find` - a hook blocks them. Use the Read tool (offset/limit), `rg`, or `rg --files` instead. Never pipe between heterogeneous commands and never combine an inspection (read, list, search, diff) with a test, lint or build invocation in one Bash call - run them as separate calls. Pass an explicit `timeout` on every Bash call: 60000 ms for an inspection, 300000 ms for a lint run or a narrow test run, 600000 ms for a full suite or a full build.\\n- **Never chain commands** with `&&`, `|`, or `;` in a single Bash call. Use separate Bash tool calls instead.\\n- **Never use redirections** like `2>/dev/null`. Handle missing files by checking existence or catching errors in the tool result.\\n- Use `Read` for file contents; for existence and listing checks Bash `ls` is fine (the native `Glob` tool is absent in this build - rules/tools.md).\\n- Use `mkdir -p` in its own Bash call when creating directories.\\n\\n## Error Handling\\n\\n| Situation | Interactive | Loop mode (`$_AUTOPILOT_LOOP` set, PRD 00017) |\\n|-----------|-------------|------------------------------------------------|\\n| Sub-skill invocation fails outright (no usable result; the phase cannot proceed) | PAUSE, report which skill failed and error. A transient reviewer/sub-skill error *during the review-rework cycle* is the review gate's Safety Checks row's domain instead (graceful degradation, not a PAUSE). | Re-invoke the sub-skill ONCE; if it fails again, stall the PRD (`recovery.md` → \\\"Loop-mode stall procedure\\\", `site: \\\"sub_skill_fail\\\"`) and continue the batch |\\n| No PRDs anywhere | STOP with message about /create-prd | Run `autopilot phase-done --outcome drained` first (it writes `phase: \\\"done\\\"` and the empty `next_phase`) so the wrapper stops as drained, not died. **If `state.json` does not exist yet** — a fresh loop pointed at an already-empty backlog — `phase-done` exits 2 and the wrapper reads that as *died*; run `autopilot init --prd \\\"\\\"` first, then `phase-done --outcome drained`. |\\n| State file corrupted | Delete it, restart from Phase 0 | Same (in-session recovery, no pause) |\\n| `statectl` state-write fails (lock contention, python error, unreadable state) | Retry once; still failing → `autopilot restore` and retry once more; still failing → write the `state-write-failed` marker (`site: \\\"statectl_fail\\\"`) with the raw stderr in `detail`, end the turn, and also report the failure to the user directly — never proceed on the stale state | Retry once; still failing → `autopilot restore` and retry once more; still failing → write the `state-write-failed` marker (`site: \\\"statectl_fail\\\"`) with the raw stderr in `detail`, end the turn — the wrapper halts on it and surfaces the detail |\\n| Review produces no parseable output | PAUSE, report — don't retry | Re-run the review ONCE; still unparseable → stall the PRD (`site: \\\"reviewer_fail\\\"`), continue the batch |\\n| All reviewers fail | PAUSE, report — partial results usable if user confirms | Re-invoke ONCE; still nothing → stall the PRD (`site: \\\"reviewer_fail\\\"`), continue the batch |\\n| `docs/dev/project-management/` doesn't exist | Create it | Same |\\n| Task tools unavailable | STOP, report — can't operate without tasks | Same (a broken harness is not a per-PRD failure) |\\n| Git push fails (auth, locked signing agent, network) | Report and let the user retry. A push denied by hooks/guard_push_on_critical.py names a pending cap_critical custody: resolve it with autopilot custody resolve, never bypass the hook. | Log to `deferred_decisions[]`, leave the commits local (the user pushes manually per Phase 9), CONTINUE — a locked signing agent on an unattended host is expected (`references/design-rationale.md` § Git push failures). A guard denial is closed at the next attended Phase 0 (`references/phase-build.md` § Handle pending custody), where the operator picks revert, branch-and-revert or accept; loop mode leaves the commits local and continues without any git mutation. |\\n| `mv` verify fails (backlog→wip, wip→done, wip→hold) | PAUSE per the mv-verify sites | Retry the `mv` ONCE after re-running `mkdir -p`; persistent failure is a sanctioned batch halt (row 3 of the sanctioned batch-halt list below) |\\n| **Security-critical finding** (exposed secret, vulnerability being shipped) | PAUSE | **PAUSE — sanctioned batch halt, row 1** (set `phase: \\\"paused\\\"` + `pause_reason`; the wrapper notifies and halts) |\\n| **Detected data-loss risk** | PAUSE | **PAUSE — sanctioned batch halt, row 2** (same mechanics) |\\n\\n**Loop mode never halts the whole batch for a per-PRD decision.** After PRD 00066 the loop-mode turn-ending *decision*-PAUSE list is empty: a blocking escalation and the Phase 1.5 design gate STALL the PRD (`references/recovery.md` Loop-mode stall procedure), a scope alarm DEFERS-and-continues, and a Phase 2 ambiguity is resolved by the simplest safe assumption (or stalls under `pause_on_ambiguity: true`) — none write `phase: \\\"paused\\\"`. Everything else stalls the PRD or defers and continues. The ONLY conditions that stop the whole batch (a loud halt, not a per-PRD park/stall) are the sanctioned batch-halt list below. Future edits must not re-grow the loop-mode decision-PAUSE list, nor add a halt outside that list.\\n\\n**Sanctioned batch-halt list (the complete set of whole-batch loud stops):**\\n\\n1. **Security-critical finding** — exposed secret or a vulnerability being shipped (including a cap_critical custody capture that fails twice) (skill PAUSE; `pause_reason` set by the Error Handling row above).\\n2. **Detected data-loss risk** (skill PAUSE).\\n3. **`mv`-verify exhaustion** — a lifecycle move that keeps failing after the retry (skill PAUSE, `mv_verify`).\\n4. **Sustained memory pressure** (wrapper memory circuit-breaker).\\n5. **Usage-limit beyond `_AUTOPILOT_LIMIT_WAIT_MAX`** (wrapper `died`).\\n6. **Net-retries exhausted** (wrapper `died`).\\n7. **Operator pause** — the `pause-requested` marker (wrapper stop).\\n8. **Died bootstrap session** — a session died before any PRD was selected, so there is nothing to park (wrapper `died`).\\n9. **Systemic-park breaker** — 2 consecutive `wrapper_died` parks with nothing healthy between (skill PAUSE, `systemic_park`; Phase 0 park handler, `references/phase-build.md`).\\n10. **Unwritable or unconsumed park marker** — the park hand-off itself is broken: the wrapper cannot write the `park-requested` marker (the write-failure guard in the `park)` else branch, so it never hands off an empty marker) OR a written marker is never consumed (the park-loop guard). Either is a wrapper loud halt.\\n11. **Plugin version drift** (PRD 00086 R3) — the loop's plugin-pin preflight (`plugin_drift` in `cli/loop.py`) found an enforcement plugin (aegis/warden) whose installed version differs from the `state.batch.plugin_versions` pin recorded at batch selection: the batch's enforcement code auto-updated mid-run, so the wrapper halts loud with state intact rather than run a session on unpinned enforcement code.\\n\\nRows 1–8 are the PRD 00066 Success-Metrics list; rows 9–10 are the two systemic backstops the PRD Risks section mandates (adopted at the design gate, 2026-07-18); row 11 is the plugin-pin preflight (PRD 00086 R3). Nothing outside these eleven stops the batch — Success Metric 2 is read as \\\"no UNSANCTIONED halts\\\".\\n\\n**Turn-ending PAUSE rows must set `state.phase = \\\"paused\\\"` (and `state.next_phase = \\\"paused\\\"`) before stopping, and must also write `state.pause_reason = {\\\"site\\\": \\\"<slug>\\\", \\\"detail\\\": \\\"<one-line human string>\\\"}`.** `pause_reason` is a durable marker so the loop halts even if the model forgets `phase=\\\"paused\\\"`; unlike `phase` it is not overwritten by normal progression, so it must be cleared on resume (see `### Resuming` cleanup). Without it the wrapper — seeing a non-empty `next_phase` — would take its continue branch and relaunch the failed phase instead of stopping for you to intervene; a paused state is the wrapper's stop-and-notify branch (Session Loop branch 1), and the wrapper surfaces `pause_reason.detail` in its notification. This applies to \\\"Sub-skill invocation fails outright\\\" (`pause_reason.site = \\\"sub_skill_fail\\\"`), \\\"Review produces no parseable output\\\" (`\\\"reviewer_fail\\\"`), \\\"All three reviewers fail\\\" (`\\\"reviewer_fail\\\"`), and \\\"statectl state-write fails\\\" (`\\\"statectl_fail\\\"`). Exceptions that need no `phase` change: \\\"State file corrupted\\\" (delete it and restart from Phase 0 in the same session; the freshly-written state is what the wrapper reads at turn end) and \\\"No PRDs anywhere\\\" (see its row — the drained state write covers the loop). PAUSE sites that ask via `AskUserQuestion` mid-turn (Phase 1.5 design gate, Phase 2 clarification, Phase 5 blocking escalation and scope alarm) do NOT end the turn and need no `phase` change — **but only outside the loop. When `$_AUTOPILOT_LOOP` is set there is no human to answer, and (post-00066) none of these pause the batch: each takes its per-PRD path instead. The design gate and a blocking escalation run the Loop-mode stall procedure (`references/recovery.md`; `site: \\\"design_gate\\\"` recording the design doc path, `site: \\\"blocking_escalation\\\"` recording the blocking issue in the deferred JSON `detail`); a scope alarm defers-and-continues (a `scope-overflow` deferred record — the top-10 fixes proceed); a Phase 2 ambiguity is resolved by the simplest safe assumption, or stalls (`site: \\\"clarification\\\"`) under `pause_on_ambiguity: true`. None writes `phase: \\\"paused\\\"` — the batch keeps draining and a human resolves the parked PRD on the next manual `/autopilot:run-autopilot` (see `references/decision-framework.md` → \\\"Autonomy in loop mode\\\"). A mid-turn question on the unattended path has stranded the loop for hours (`references/design-rationale.md` § No mid-turn questions).**\\n\\n## Reference Files\\n\\n- `references/phase-build.md` — build gate (selection/aborts, catchup, design, planning, work)\\n- `references/phase-review.md` — review gate (lenses, decision gate, cap, rework)\\n- `references/phase-done.md` — done gate (completion, continuation, batch-end review)\\n- `references/state-schema.md` — state file JSON schema and skip logic\\n- `references/decision-framework.md` — auto-fix vs escalate classification rules\\n- `references/recovery.md` — rare-path handlers (abort/replan, stalls, escalation-exhausted, cap-pause resume)\\n- `references/design-rationale.md` — incident history behind the rules (non-normative)\\n- `references/dashboard-format.md` — live dashboard via tracon (render_stream fallback; plain text via `autopilot status`)\\n- `references/batch-report-format.md` — batch report sources and semantics (render: `autopilot render report`)\\n- `references/audit-log-format.md` — audit label set and decisions.md projection (render: `autopilot render audit`)\\n- `references/doubt-review-rubric.md` — Bob's doubt-lens rubric (D1-D5)\\n- `references/waves.md` — the `autopilot wave` verbs, what a lane worktree holds, and what is deferred\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a12530-0f18-7dd0-be1b-bc3a1e3e56e4","create_time":1791625279.319802}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
