\\\"reason\\\": \\\"Reviewed design preserves pre-mutation rejection and strict writer errors; isolated GPT and Anthropic design reviews found no blockers. Recorded non-blockers/questions remain in design log.\\\",\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 0,\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"n/a\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"Provider rejection recovery\\\",\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"Operator requested proceeding; retry unchanged authorized repair workflow with existing provider safeguards\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"User explicitly asked to proceed after the pause; no provider switch, permission relaxation, prompt obfuscation or cap increase\\\",\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 0,\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"n/a\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"The raw-first regressions (byte-exact raw output, gap digest/preview, unchanged adapter state, later healthy terminal) live in `tests/events/test_unicode_frames.py`. The PRD's Task 2 acceptance command (`pytest tests/events/test_unicode_safety.py tests/events/test_tail.py`) and its repository structure both name `test_unicode_safety.py`, so the PRD's own command does not run them. The full suite covers them, and the split keeps the file under 800 lines. Fix: record the file in the PRD or design acceptance text\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"queued for fresh rework\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Correct design acceptance to include the approved frame-test split; preserve coverage and size limits.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"`has_invalid_unicode_scalar` never stops on cyclic input. It keeps no record of containers it has already visited, so a payload like `d = {\\\\\\\"self\\\\\\\": d}` loops forever and its pending list grows without limit. I checked this: `Observation(payload=d)` was still running after 3 s. Before this change the recursive copier raised `RecursionError`, and `RawFirstDecoder` turned that into a `raw.unknown` gap. Now a driver bug that builds a cyclic payload freezes the supervisor's decode path, along with the timeout and cleanup steps that wait on it. Both design reviewers raised this as a non-blocker. Fix: track the `id()` of containers already visited, either skipping them or raising `ValueError`. That is about 3 lines and keeps the scan iterative\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"defer to batch end\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Settled limitation: the reviewed helper contract is explicitly acyclic JSON-like containers; native json.loads cannot create cycles. Preserve the witnessed hang; no cycle-guard scope expansion.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"The receiver-lane test special-cases the exact cap input with `if detail != CAP_REPRODUCER`. It then calls `assert_safe_details(tmp_path, lanes, lane_detail)`, which compares the lane detail to itself, so the cap case never checks the exact redacted value or that `[REDACTED]` is present. I confirmed the actual lane detail equals the expected `\\\\\\\"x\\\\\\\"*4080 + \\\\\\\" [REDACTED]\\\\\\\"`. Simpler and stronger: drop the guard and call `assert_safe_details(tmp_path, lanes, expected)`. Related: `assert TOKEN not in expected` and `assert \\\\\\\"sk-abcdefghijkl\\\\\\\" not in expected` (lines 87-88) check fixed test data inside the loop. They should check `observation.payload[\\\\\\\"detail\\\\\\\"]`\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"queued for fresh rework\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Strengthen independent exact-value and redaction assertions for the receiver lane cap case.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"Hot-path scan cost. `_payload` scans the whole payload at line 224, then `sanitize_json` scans it again at line 252, and `_usage_payload` scans again through `sanitize_json`. Each scan uses a per-character Python generator: about 22 ms per MiB, against about 2.5 ms for a precompiled `re.compile(\\\\\\\"[\\\\\\\\ud800-\\\\\\\\udfff]\\\\\\\").search`. Every frame also gets re-validated by `replace()` in `_bind_observations` and by `observation_record` in the writer. Behavior-preserving fix: use the regex in the predicate, and call `_sanitize_json` inside `_payload` and `_usage_payload` once the up-front check has passed\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"low\\\",\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"queued for fresh rework\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Bounded scan optimization; preserve validation of all original strings before sensitive-key redaction, supported containers, and subclass semantics.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"Removing controls before redaction now leaks tokens that the old order caught. When a bare C0 control, DEL or lone ESC sits between a word character and a token, removal glues them together: `\\\\\\\"x\\\\\\\\x00sk-\\\\\\\" + \\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\"*30` becomes `xsk-aaaa\\\\u2026` and `\\\\\\\"auth\\\\\\\\x00Bearer \\\\\\\" + \\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\"*26` becomes `authBearer \\\\u2026`. `_LIKELY_SECRET` needs `\\\\\\\\b` before `sk-`/`Bearer`, so neither `bounded_detail` nor the Observation's second sanitizer redacts them. The old order redacted both, because the control supplied the word boundary. Confirmed by tracing the regex by hand; not executed (read-only sandbox)\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"queued for fresh rework\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Confirmed on actual bounded_detail: C0 removal destroys regex word boundaries for bare and Bearer tokens; old order redacts both. Additional pre-cleanup redaction preserves required post-cleanup redaction and final cap, without interface changes. Amend the exact design contract from this review evidence.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"The R2 class of bug still exists at display time, outside this PRD's scope. `bounded_detail` strips only C0 controls and 7-bit CSI. The TUI's `display_text` drops all Cc/Cf/Cs/Zl/Zp characters (src/ovcaq/tui/model.py:57). Confirmed: `\\\\\\\"lane sk-abc\\\\\\\\u200b<36 chars>\\\\\\\"` (also with \\\\\\\\x85, \\\\\\\\x9b, \\\\\\\\u00ad, \\\\\\\\u2060 instead of \\\\\\\\u200b) passes redaction unchanged and the TUI shows the joined `sk-abcabcdef\\\\u2026` token. Activity text and tool details (src/ovcaq/drivers/codex_events.py:32, src/ovcaq/drivers/codex_events.py:127) strip nothing, so a NUL-split token also renders whole on screen. This needs a durable home (a PRD or issue)\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"defer to batch end\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Display-time character stripping/activity UI is excluded by this PRD. Preserve durable follow-up evidence; no implicit expansion of 00054.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"The preflight's `except (ValueError, RecursionError)` branch, which hands the frame to the driver, has no test. No RawFirstDecoder test feeds a deeply nested frame. Narrowing it to `except ValueError` would still pass the suite: deep frames would then get \\\\\\\"driver failed to decode frame\\\\\\\" instead of the driver's own native gap, against the contract's \\\\\\\"ValueError or RecursionError \\\\u2192 delegate unchanged\\\\\\\"\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"queued for fresh rework\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Add independent deep-JSON RecursionError-delegation regression; preserve native fallback and adapter state.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"The preflight parse and scan sit inside the tolerant driver catch, so the try blocks nest 4 levels deep (the limit). Hoist the `json.loads` try and the scalar check above it and wrap only `tuple(self._driver.decode(frame))` in `except Exception`. This also matches the contract's \\\\\\\"consume decode under the existing tolerant catch\\\\\\\"\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"queued for fresh rework\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Simplify only while preserving tolerant validation/decode failure behavior, ValueError/RecursionError delegation, raw-first ordering and fatal publication outside catches. Reject literal hoisting if it widens error escape; helper extraction within the existing boundary is acceptable.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"Duplicated test tables: the completion construct and record-decode tests repeat the same `additions` dict and body. Hoist it to one module constant or merge them into one test parametrized over construct vs decode\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"queued for fresh rework\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Share the completion additions fixture while preserving independent construct/decode paths and assertions.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"The same strip-after-redact bug exists in the TUI. `display_text` drops Cc/Cf characters from already-sanitized activity and tool text without redacting again, so a stored `sk-abc\\\\\\\\x00defghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123` shows on screen as the rebuilt token\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"defer to batch end\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Same out-of-scope display-time defect as R6; preserve its separate citation and deferral.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"The same strip-after-redaction pattern survives outside `bounded_detail`. TUI `display_text` drops Cc/Cf characters after the sanitizer has already run. I checked that a NUL-split token in a Codex agent message (`codex_events.py:32`, where controls are never stripped) shows the full token on screen. So do ZWSP- and soft-hyphen-split tokens even in `bounded_detail` output, because format characters are not in its control regex. This is outside this PRD's scope (\\\\\\\"no speculative control forms\\\\\\\"), but the new doc sentence \\\\\\\"prevents controls inside a credential from hiding it from redaction\\\\\\\" claims more than the code delivers. Fix: narrow the wording to the recognized C0/DEL/CSI forms and file a follow-up PRD to re-sanitize at the display layer\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"low\\\",\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"queued for fresh rework\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Narrow protocol wording to recognized ANSI/control forms; broader TUI follow-up stays deferred.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"The `**runtime**` changelog entry claims a runtime fix, but no runtime production code changed (Task 4 added tests only). It repeats the `**events**` gap entry. Fix: fold the continuation and propagation wording into the events entry\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"low\\\",\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"queued for fresh rework\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Fold duplicate runtime wording into the events changelog entry, retaining continuity and fatal-writer guarantees.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"The supervisor's catch-all handler builds `SessionSummary(..., str(error), ...)`. If the error text carries surrogateescape bytes (a non-UTF-8 path), the new `SessionSummary` check raises `ValueError` inside the handler and `_conclude` is skipped, so the failure record is lost. Not a regression: the strict encode in src/ovcaq/runtime/_session_io.py:19 already failed on such text\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"low\\\",\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"defer to batch end\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Pre-existing arbitrary exception/path surrogate handling; retain for later scoped runtime failure work.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"cycle\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"The pre-decode check assumes every driver parses frames with plain