rc/ovcaq/events/model.py \\\\u2014 new module-level internal helper;\\\\n# imported directly by events.tail, not re-exported from ovcaq.events.\\\\ndef has_invalid_unicode_scalar(value: object) -> bool: ...\\\\n\\\\n# Existing public signature; validation wraps the existing recursive copier.\\\\ndef sanitize_json(value: object) -> JSONValue: ...\\\\n\\\\n# New private recursive copier: existing sanitize_json body moves here,\\\\n# and its recursion uses _sanitize_json rather than repeating root scans.\\\\ndef _sanitize_json(value: object) -> JSONValue: ...\\\\n\\\\n# Existing boundaries; signatures retained.\\\\ndef _identifier(value: object, *, optional: bool = False) -> None: ...\\\\ndef _payload(\\\\n    kind: ObservationType, payload: Mapping[str, JSONValue]\\\\n) -> dict[str, JSONValue]: ...\\\\ndef observation_record(observation: Observation) -> dict[str, JSONValue]: ...\\\\ndef observation_from_record(record: Mapping[str, JSONValue]) -> Observation: ...\\\\ndef summary_record(summary: SessionSummary) -> dict[str, JSONValue]: ...\\\\n\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n`has_invalid_unicode_scalar(value)` is a side-effect-free predicate over strings,\\\\n`Mapping` keys and values, and list/tuple elements. It returns `True` iff any\\\\nreachable string contains a code point in the inclusive range\\\\n`0xD800..0xDFFF`. Other scalar types are ignored; existing JSON/type/finite-number\\\\nvalidation remains responsible for their acceptance. Scan supported acyclic\\\\nJSON-like containers iteratively to avoid introducing a new recursion-depth\\\\nfailure during preflight. It neither redacts nor modifies input. Ordinary\\\\nUnicode, including non-BMP scalars, remains unchanged. `json.loads` decodes a\\\\nvalid escaped high/low surrogate pair to one non-BMP scalar, which is accepted;\\\\nunpaired decoded surrogates and Python strings still containing surrogate code\\\\npoints are rejected. This is validation, never replacement or ASCII escaping.\\\\n\\\\n`sanitize_json` checks the complete original input with the predicate before\\\\ncalling `_sanitize_json`; invalid input raises\\\\n`ValueError(\\\\\\\"Strings must contain only Unicode scalar values\\\\\\\")`. Check nested\\\\nkeys and every value, including values beneath sensitive keys which will later\\\\nbe replaced by `[REDACTED]`. Preserve existing valid-container copying,\\\\ncredential redaction, nonstring-key `TypeError`, nonfinite-number rejection and\\\\nunsupported-type errors. `_sanitize_json` retains existing recursive behavior\\\\nand uses its own name for recursion, so the complete scalar scan runs once per\\\\npublic `sanitize_json` invocation rather than once for every subtree.\\\\n\\\\n`_identifier` retains its nonempty-string/optional-None rules, then rejects\\\\ninvalid scalar strings with the same static `ValueError`. This protects\\\\n`Observation.provider`, `run_id`, `session_id`, `lane_id`, and the existing\\\\n`SessionSummary.session_id` caller. `_payload` checks the complete original\\\\nmapping before inspecting payload shapes or taking the `_usage_payload` early\\\\nreturn. Thus `usage.updated.identity`, nested usage keys/values and\\\\n`session.completed`'s special usage branch cannot bypass scalar validation.\\\\nInvalid strings fail before sanitization, truncation or freezing, even if those\\\\noperations would have removed them. `SessionSummary.__post_init__` checks a\\\\nnonnull string `failure_detail` before its existing sanitizer and 4096-character\\\\ncap. Existing invalid-type checks stay in place. `observation_record`,\\\\n`observation_from_record` and `summary_record` retain their signatures and field\\\\nshapes and serialize only successfully constructed validated models. Error\\\\nmessages never include the rejected string, mapping key, or credential.\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nDetails:\\\\n- Implement only this task…12681 tokens truncated…ork\\\" instead of stalling.\\n4. Otherwise (chain not exhausted), persist the escalated tier, then queue the task for rework:\\n   - `task-set-meta <task-id> <meta-json-file>` with payload `{\\\"model\\\": \\\"<next_tier>\\\", \\\"escalation_reason\\\": \\\"review_flag\\\", \\\"escalated_from\\\": \\\"<prev_tier>\\\"}` — canonical source `/autopilot:work` reads directly from `state.tasks[i]` (see `work/SKILL.md` \\\"Per-task model dispatch\\\"). `<prev_tier>` is the tier step 3 escalated from. These two fields carry onto the new attempt entry `/autopilot:work` writes at the escalated tier, keeping a review-driven escalation distinguishable from `/autopilot:work`'s own in-loop `escalation_reason:\\\"gate_failure\\\"` in `attempts[]` (`references/state-schema.md`). `task-set-meta` writes `state.tasks[i].model` in the same call — there is no separate mirror write.\\n   - Reset `state.tasks[i].status` back to `\\\"pending\\\"` via `task-set-status <task-id> pending` so `/autopilot:work` will iterate it again. **Reverse status transitions (`completed` → `pending`) are supported** — `task-set-status` writes `state.tasks[i].status` directly; the ground truth is simply whatever `statectl` wrote (the PostToolUse status-sync hook this line used to cite was retired by PRD 00063 and no longer exists).\\n5. Append the task ID to `state.rework_task_ids` (create the array if absent) — **after** the status reset, never before: Phase 4's resume rule drops every listed id whose task is still `completed`, so an id appended while its task still read `completed` would be dropped by a rotation landing between the two writes (PRD 00196). A rotation between the reset and the append leaves a `pending` task outside the list, which rework mode ignores and the next cycle re-flags: loud, not silent.\\n\\nThe \\\"no prior attempt\\\" case in step 3 covers both pre-PRD-00025 legacy plans (which lack `state.tasks[i].model` and `attempts[]` entirely) and PRD-00025 tasks that crash before the first attempt log writes (rare but possible). Both are treated as `\\\"sonnet\\\"` for the next-tier computation, so first escalation goes to `\\\"opus\\\"`.\\n\\n**In-loop ↔ Phase-6 composition.** Step 2's outcome rewrite (`\\\"completed\\\"` → `\\\"review_flagged\\\"`) only ever touches `attempts[-1]` — the terminal rung — which is never an `\\\"escalated\\\"` row (those are earlier history from `/autopilot:work`'s in-loop diagnosis), so `/autopilot:work`'s widened one-entry-per-rung cardinality does not corrupt this read. If the in-loop path already escalated all the way to `opus`, `attempts[-1].model == \\\"opus\\\"` and step 3's next-tier computation above routes straight into the \\\"escalation exhausted\\\" branch — no double-count, no skipped rung. Phase 6 composes cumulatively with in-loop escalation because it always reads the terminal rung's entry.\\n\\n### Dispatch rework\\n\\n**Design CRITICAL rework before any task-add (PRD 00194).** When this cycle's consolidated table holds at least one 🔴 CRITICAL row AND `state.cycle < state.rework_cap`, invoke `/autopilot:design-solution docs/dev/project-management/prds/wip/<state.prd> --rework <this cycle's review file>` ONCE for this cycle, before the first task is created below. Every unresolved 🔴 row of this cycle becomes (or joins) a `[D{cycle}]` task in source 2 below; its Defer-to-batch-end record (Classification) is the audit trail, not a reason to skip the fix - the Cap check reads \\\"A CRITICAL blocks until it is fixed\\\". This section is the only task-creation point for a 🔴 finding under autopilot: `review-work-completion` step 7 leaves 🔴 rows to it (PRD 00194) and creates the other severities as today. The skill writes `docs/dev/project-management/designs/<prd-stem>-rework-<cycle>-design.md` (`<cycle>` = `state.cycle`), reviewed by its own three-dispatch loop, and ends its `## Review log` with a terminal `result:` line. **Artifact rule on entry:** first the source check - `rg -q -F 'Source review: <review-file> (head_sha <head_sha>)' docs/dev/project-management/designs/<prd-stem>-rework-<cycle>-design.md`, `<review-file>` being